de-basket-59d1b7cb.webp","/images/assessment/release-media/rain-through-tent-crack-605d07ce.webp","/images/assessment/release-media/red-tulip-among-yellow-tulips-e122def3.webp","/images/assessment/release-media/slippers-under-bed-f363802c.webp","/images/assessment/release-media/snow-on-roof-f8167902.webp","/images/assessment/release-media/teddy-between-pillows-ea981f49.webp","/images/assessment/release-media/train-through-tunnel-adfa0aae.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/ball-8bec04.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/bank-bdd240.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/jeep-877f6f.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/paint-039f7b.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/print-6d0d58.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/rip-ab8e59.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/snack-1ba61a.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/snail-9054cc.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/stone-e30bfd.webp","/images/assessment/release-support/targets/wink-050522.webp","/images/assessment/rhyming/variants/ing/swing-02.webp","/images/assessment/scenes/sentence-comprehension/scene-boy-giant-jelly.webp","/images/assessment/scenes/sentence-comprehension/scene-boys-ladder-bakery.webp","/images/assessment/scenes/sentence-comprehension/scene-cat-umbrella.webp","/images/assessment/scenes/sentence-comprehension/scene-ducks-ice-cream-van.webp","/images/assessment/scenes/sentence-comprehension/scene-girl-jumps-puddle.webp","/images/assessment/scenes/sentence-comprehension/scene-grandad-child-red-kite.webp","/images/assessment/scenes/sentence-comprehension/scene-red-sock-falling.webp","/images/assessment/scenes/sentence-comprehension/scene-snowman-sunglasses.webp","/images/child-mode/blends/cloud.webp","/images/child-mode/initial-sounds/bear.webp","/images/child-mode/initial-sounds/car.webp","/images/child-mode/initial-sounds/kite.webp","/images/child-mode/initial-sounds/leaf.webp","/images/child-mode/initial-sounds/red.webp","/images/child-mode/initial-sounds/shop.webp","/images/child-mode/initial-sounds/spin.webp","/images/child-mode/reviewed/chill.webp","/images/child-mode/sentence-scenes/bag.webp","/images/child-mode/sentence-scenes/bed.webp","/images/child-mode/sentence-scenes/books.webp","/images/child-mode/sentence-scenes/cats.webp","/images/child-mode/sentence-scenes/cup.webp","/images/child-mode/sentence-scenes/cups.webp","/images/child-mode/sentence-scenes/sock.webp","/images/child-mode/vocabulary/sink.webp","/images/child-mode/vocabulary/straw.webp","/images/child-mode/vocabulary/trip.webp","04ba3364dbca068b6365ea7a407730bbee7f5910c187f69189ba0af82ae2a9ed","114377dd1e2605ce4db3cf948f9bc7e10e3173d6ba3a6307db3164eaf4aa20a3","12","174177f59a6d86ed550cbff37b55275fafcbae1c0d923dcfcb365a1a45287d79","309dfa4dc767a8e57db70f97ffa4e78d5a90ca93ba1d30ea09085ddadc3c344f","4adf0ed56645f0c66c80c81a78e3d3cf0fe4edf1ba4b2b5b8a37c9ad8bfc90af","72a60e6b57f5cf94f2a4b9bb54ee2766629e7c4c6d6c68fdb2219c1ff7a46198","77e7c52a219604326913d4dbc011f72533fe2c94482a47a1a52819dbbe986689","A Red Kite","A Turtle’s Food","A backpack pressed several buttons in the crowded lift. That made the lift stop at every floor. Each stop took more time on Priya''s trip upstairs. She reached the dentist after her appointment time. She heard her name as she left the lift. The lift moved at its usual speed between the floors. It had no fault, and the doors opened normally. At each empty landing, Priya still had to wait. The accidental button presses had added these waits to her journey.","A ball rolled into the open doorway. It rested between the door and its frame. Mia pushed the door towards the frame. The ball was in the way, stopping it closing.","A bat caught a moth above the pond.","A bat found shelter in a narrow roof space.","A bat lay across the team’s kit bag.","A bat struck the ball over the fence.","A bat","A bench stands near the ticket machine.","A bicycle can travel down a path.","A bicycle has pedals.","A bicycle is useful for a short trip.","A bicycle needs several checks before a ride. Firm tyres help it roll smoothly. Brakes must stop the wheels when squeezed. A loose chain can slip, so its position matters too. Looking at just one part does not tell you whether the whole bicycle is ready to use.","A bird drank from the bowl in Mara''s garden. Each morning, Mara filled it with fresh water. One day she heard the bird sing nearby. She paused her digging and listened with a smile. Caring for the garden brought her a little joy.","A bird flew away after feeding its chicks.","A bird gathered things to build its nest.","A boat","A book about nests","A book by","A borrowed library book follows four steps on this return route. First, the reader slides it through the return slot. That slot leads to a padded box behind the counter. Next, a librarian checks the book for damage. This book has a loose page that needs attention. A helper then repairs the page with suitable binding tape. Finally, the librarian puts the repaired book back on its shelf. Checking first prevents damaged books returning straight to readers. A loose page could otherwise fall out during the next loan.","A bowl of crushed dark berries tipped onto a pale cloth. Juice spread across it and left purple marks. Sora rinsed the cloth later, making the marks lighter. The cloth had been plain before the juice spilled.","A boy is holding up a huge red jelly.","A bright lamp lit the reading corner. Someone accidentally pulled its plug from the wall while moving a chair. The lamp went out, and the corner became dark. The ceiling light elsewhere in the room stayed on until closing time.","A broken radio arrived at the village repair table. Its owner expected to throw it away and buy another. A volunteer noticed a loose wire near the speaker. She joined the wire safely, and music played again. At the next table, someone sewed a torn school bag. Nearby, a child replaced a missing wheel on a toy. People shared tools, learned new skills and kept useful things. The event left the rubbish bin almost empty that afternoon.","A burst of noisy, unsettled activity.","A bus broke down, leaving fewer buses on the route. More people waited at each stop. The next bus filled up and could not take everyone. Usually, two services shared the passengers during that busy hour. With one missing, everyone depended on the remaining service. Every seat and safe standing space was already taken. The driver followed the limit for that vehicle. The people left at the stop had to wait for more space.","A bus stop gives travellers several kinds of information. A route number identifies the service. A map shows places along its journey. A timetable tells when buses are expected. Reading these together helps a traveller choose a bus and plan when to wait for it.","A butterfly’s wings need drying before flight. The sheltered stem was safe for this slow change. A caterpillar changed into a chrysalis. Later, a butterfly came out. Finally, it flew away on its dry wings.","A care chart named Zara as the person to feed the fish. Zara was away, so Owen asked Priya to take over. Priya measured one small spoonful of food and sprinkled it on the water. Owen checked the filter while the fish ate. Zara returned the following day.","A cat is sleeping curled inside an open umbrella.","A cat","A chick hatched in our classroom.","A class built a robot to collect paper scraps. Its wheels kept getting stuck. The children tested and changed one part each time. After several changes, it rolled along collecting paper.","A class needed a leader for its river-cleaning project. Mina spoke confidently and suggested the biggest collection target. Quiet Ravi asked where younger pupils could work safely. He checked the route and found places for sorting the rubbish. On the day, Mina encouraged everyone and Ravi kept the plan organised. The teacher praised both kinds of contribution to the project. Afterwards, the class chose two leaders for its next event. They had learned that different strengths could support the same goal.","A clear visual cue helped the whole group stop together.","A coat had eight buttons. The tailor found that three were cracked and replaced those with new ones. Two other buttons were loose, so she sewed them on more firmly. She left the remaining buttons alone. When the coat was finished, it still had eight buttons.","A compost heap can turn plant scraps into material for a garden. Fruit peel, fallen leaves and cut grass can go into it. Over time, small living things help break the scraps down. Gardeners then mix the finished compost into soil instead of treating every plant scrap as rubbish.","A cup held the freezer door open during the party. Ice cream melted and dripped onto two bags of peas. Dad removed the cup and shut the door. The freezer grew cold again. The drops froze, sticking the bags together. The bags had been separate before the party began. The sticky drops were the only thing joining their surfaces. Cooling changed those drops from a liquid to something hard. Pulling gently at one bag moved the other bag with it.","A delivery van parked across the entrance to the narrow lane. The school bus could not pass it on the way home. Its driver waited while a helper found the van''s owner. By the time the entrance was clear, ten minutes had passed. The bus reached its final stop later than usual. A teacher called the waiting families to explain the delay. No one on the bus had been hurt or lost. The problem began with where the delivery van had stopped.","A different route helped us arrive with dry equipment.","A dinosaur skeleton arrived at the museum in numbered boxes. Scientists checked photographs before deciding where each fossil belonged. Some bones were too fragile to support the weight above them. Metal supports held those pieces safely in their correct positions. Visitors watched through glass as the body slowly took shape. A missing section was replaced with a clearly marked model. After a month, the complete display stood above the visitors. Its labels explained both the real fossils and the added supports.","A direction sign had turned in the wind. Its arrow pointed away from the picnic ground, so two walkers followed the wrong track. They noticed their mistake at a locked gate. After returning, they used a map and told a ranger about the loose sign.","A display contained fourteen small models. Six were made from clay and stood on a square board. Five wooden models were arranged beside them. Three paper models hung above the table on threads. The labels explained the materials, not the size or age of each model.","A distant ring echoed from the church bell.","A dry sock lies on the ground.","A duck''s feathers help keep its skin dry. It spreads oil over them with its bill. Water rolls off the oily feathers in drops. The duck can swim without getting soaked underneath. It cleans its feathers often.","A fallen branch blocked the narrow garden path. The caretaker cut it into smaller pieces. Two helpers carried those pieces to a pile. Then they swept the path clear of twigs. Families could walk through the garden safely again.","A family tried a small robot cleaner in their living room. At first, it stopped with a sock caught underneath. After the sock was removed, a loose cable blocked its brush. The instructions explained that small objects should be cleared first. The family lifted the cable and collected toys from the floor. This time, the machine travelled around the room without stopping. The machine avoided table legs and returned to its charger. After cleaning, Dad emptied dust from the small removable box.","A fish splashed.","A food show put the bakery on television that week. The other bakery in town was closed for repairs. Saturday was also the first day for its special plum tarts. All these things brought a long line of customers. Some customers wanted to visit the shop they had seen. Others usually bought their bread from the closed business nearby. People hoping for tarts joined both groups in the queue. The baker''s notebook listed all three sources of the unusually busy morning.","A football rolled towards the road through a gap. Hana shut the gate before it reached her. The ball struck the gate and stopped. She picked it up and returned to play. The gate stayed shut throughout the game.","A fox kept taking carrots from Bear’s garden. Bear first built a tall fence, but the fox dug beneath it. Bear then offered the fox a small plot to grow carrots. Soon they both had carrots, and the digging stopped.","A freezer alarm warned that its door had been left open. The warm air softened the frozen food. Staff moved the food to another freezer before it fully thawed. The building itself was much warmer than the freezer''s usual setting. A box beside the hinge had stopped the door closing properly. The alarm was a warning about this change, not its cause. Moving the food restored cold conditions around it. The staff then removed the box from the hinge.","A girl in rain boots jumps over a puddle.","A green stone was set into the ring.","A group was building a model bridge from paper strips. Their first bridge bent in the middle under one small stone. Mara folded the next strips into shapes with straight ridges. She set the folded strips between the same two supports. This time the bridge held three stones before it bent. Mara drew both designs and counted the stones beside each. She asked the group to try another folded shape next. The size of the gap stayed the same for every test.","A gull watched Jo''s sandwich on the bench. Jo went to fetch a drink from Dad. The bird hopped closer and opened its beak. It leaned towards the bread while Jo was away.","A gust blew the picnic cloth into the pond. Dad used a long branch to reach it. He spread the cloth across a sunny bench. The family ate from their lunch boxes instead. The cloth dried there.","A gust of wind broke the kite''s tail. Without a tail, the kite started to spin. That spin wound its string around a flagpole. The kite stayed there until the school cleaner fetched a ladder. Before the tear, the kite had flown straight above the field. Its long tail had helped keep it steady in the air. The pole stood close to the place where it began turning. Nobody on the ground was winding the line around anything.","A gust pulled the kite string from Rosa’s hand. The kite caught in a low branch beyond her reach. Rosa fetched a long broom and asked Dad to come with her. Dad held the broom while Rosa pointed at the branch.","A hamster needs food, water, and a safe home.","A helmet has a hard outer shell. Soft pads fit around your head inside. A strap holds it in place during movement. These parts work together when someone falls. The helmet must fit properly to protect the head.","A kite caught in a low apple tree. Lila shook the branch, but it stayed there. Her friend held the string away from twigs. Lila slowly lifted the kite with a pole. Together they brought it down safely.","A kite caught in a low tree. An adult named Ruth brought a ladder. Dev wanted to climb it, but Ruth explained that she would do the climbing. She asked Dev to hold the bottom steady while Sol watched for people on the path. Ruth reached the kite and brought it down.","A letter begins its journey in a street postbox. A postal worker first empties the box into a collection bag. Letters for many different places are mixed together there. At the sorting centre, workers sort them by their destinations. Each group is ready for a different delivery area. A truck then carries the sorted letters to the local office. Finally, a carrier delivers each letter to the address on it. The address matters during sorting as well as at the end. These are four main stages of the journey.","A letter with her name arrived from a country Safa had never visited. She turned the envelope over, examined the unfamiliar stamp and asked who might have sent it. Before opening it, she fetched an atlas to find the country marked beside the stamp.","A library kept ten maps in its local-history corner. Two old maps were protected behind glass. Five modern maps could be borrowed. Three large maps were available to study at a table but had to stay in the library. Each group had a sign explaining its use.","A library offers more than shelves of books. Clear labels help people find subjects. Staff can suggest a story or show someone how to search. Tables provide room to study, and some libraries hold storytelling events. These services help different people use and enjoy information.","A lighthouse stands where the rocks are dangerous. At night its big lamp turns round and round. Ships out at sea watch for its flashing light. It warns them about rocks in the water. They can steer safely past.","A little shoot grew out of the seed.","A long piece of rope.","A long ring came from the old telephone.","A loose roof tile let rain drip onto the ceiling. The wet patch grew during each storm. After the tile was replaced, the ceiling stayed dry. A roofer found a gap at the edge of that tile. Water could travel from there down to the plaster below. The replacement covered the gap completely. Another storm came after the repair, but no new mark appeared. That comparison showed why the old wet patch had formed indoors.","A loose tile let rain enter the roof of a shed. Water dripped onto a cardboard box holding paper decorations. By morning, the bottom of the box had become soft. When the caretaker lifted it, the bottom split open. The decorations fell out and some were too wet to use. The class spread the dry ones on a table. They repaired the roof before storing anything in the shed again. Replacing only the box would not have stopped the original problem.","A marble rolled smoothly down a sloping wooden track. It reached a strip of thick felt and slowed to a stop. Earlier marbles had rolled farther along the bare wood. The slope had not changed, but the new rough strip resisted the moving marble.","A market stall opens before the nearby shops. Its seller lays out fresh fruit in baskets. Customers choose what they need and pay. The seller replaces empty baskets during the busy morning. At closing time, she packs the remaining fruit away.","A message arrived from the class next door. Our teacher read the note to everyone. The children thought of a reply together. Then one child wrote their answer on paper. At the end, two helpers delivered the finished message.","A message reached the class saying the animal shelter would close tomorrow. Several children wanted to copy it onto their family noticeboard immediately. Uma noticed that the message had no date or named sender. She asked an adult to telephone the shelter before they repeated it. The shelter was only closing its front door during one repair morning. Animals would still receive care, and afternoon visits would continue. Uma replaced the alarming message with a dated notice explaining those details. The children changed their visit time without spreading the wrong story.","A model boat sat still on the water. Mei blew towards its sail, and the boat moved away. When she stopped blowing, it slowed. Her hand never touched the boat.","A moth saw a bright yellow porch light outside. The light drew it toward the house. It flew around the lamp again and again. The dark garden stayed behind it.","A neglected path was covered in litter. One morning’s cleanup barely changed it. The neighbours planned short weekly sessions, sorting rubbish and arranging collection. After many weeks, the path was clear enough for families to use again. They continued checking it so rubbish would not pile up.","A new camera is for sale; please pay in the office.","A new child stood alone at lunch. Sam made room and asked him to join the table. Weeks later, Sam forgot his lunch. The same child offered part of his sandwich without being asked.","A pair of children disagreed about their class mural. One wanted a river; the other preferred a busy market. They each explained what they liked about their own idea. Instead of voting at once, they drew a few plans together. Their final picture showed a market beside a river crossing. Both children could add details they had been excited to paint. Other classmates found space for their ideas as well. Listening had helped the group make something neither had imagined alone.","A paper plane kept turning sharply to the left. One wing was folded much higher than the other. Lina adjusted the wings until they matched, then launched it from the same place. This time it flew straight. She used the same paper and did not throw it harder.","A parcel arrived for Noor.","A period of rest while asleep.","A permission slip was needed to go on the walk.","A puppy waited beside its empty water bowl. Its owner filled a jug at the tap. She carried the jug over and knelt down. The puppy wagged its tail and watched closely. She tipped fresh water into the bowl.","A rectangular floor mat","A red glove lay beside the playground gate. Hassan picked it up before it blew away. He took it to the school office. The helper pinned it on the lost-items board. Its owner saw it there after lunch.","A reusable bottle can be filled again instead of thrown away after one drink. It needs washing between uses. A tight lid helps prevent leaks in a bag. If its parts can be replaced when worn, the same bottle can stay useful for longer.","A rooster crowed as the sky began to brighten. The farmer joked that its crow had lifted the sun. But dawn came on quiet mornings too. The rooster noticed the early light and began crowing. The bird''s coop had a window facing the lightening sky. The farmer could see daylight there before hearing the first call. A recording of yesterday''s crow played at noon changed nothing outside. The passage describes this bird responding to dawn, not controlling the time.","A rope held the little boat beside the bank. It needed releasing before the journey could begin. The sailor untied the rope. She pushed the boat from the bank. Then she began rowing.","A row of six cups stood on the table. Four cups had seeds in them. The other two held only damp soil. The class labelled every cup with a number. They watched the seeds over several days.","A seed bank stores seeds from many kinds of crops. Workers label each packet with its plant and original place. Cold rooms help keep those seeds useful for future years. A few seeds are tested regularly to check they grow. If a flood destroys a local crop, saved seeds may help. Farmers can grow that kind again instead of losing it. Researchers also compare old varieties with crops grown today. The quiet store protects choices that future growers may need.","A seed became a flower.","A seed lay in the soft soil.","A seed library lends small packets at the start of spring. Gardeners choose plants suited to the local soil and weather. They grow them and leave some flowers to produce new seeds. After collecting and drying those seeds, they return a share. Other gardeners can borrow from those packets the following year. Labels explain which plants grew well and how they were cared for. The collection changes as neighbours contribute seeds and useful notes. Gardeners return new seeds, not the ones they first borrowed.","A seed may travel far from its plant. Some have hooks that catch on animal fur. Others have light wings and float on wind. Birds carry some seeds inside juicy fruits. These journeys help plants grow in new places.","A seedling stood in a dark cupboard by mistake. Its stem grew pale and bent towards a crack. The children moved it onto a bright windowsill. New leaves soon looked greener and stronger. They remembered to check its water each morning.","A shark swam behind a thick glass wall. Smaller fish turned together in the blue water. The guide pointed to another tank of jellyfish. Children moved closer to watch its living creatures.","A shy dog hid when Joel visited the shelter. Joel followed the keeper’s advice, sitting quietly and letting the dog choose when to approach. He returned regularly and stayed calm each time. Gradually the dog came closer, and one day it rested beside him without hiding.","A small dog appeared outside the school without its owner. The office kept it safe while an adult checked its collar. The name tag was missing, so pupils could not telephone anyone. A nearby vet offered to check for a tiny identity chip. The chip''s number led to a record with the owner''s details. Soon a relieved neighbour arrived carrying the dog''s empty lead. The office checked those details before allowing her to collect it. The dog returned home without the school guessing who owned it.","A small shoot grew from the bean.","A smell travelling through the air.","A smooth ball of soft clay lay on the board. Hana pressed her thumb into its centre and then lifted her hand. A hollow remained where her thumb had pushed. Later she put a bead beside the clay, without touching the hollow.","A smooth ring covered the mark on her finger.","A snail moved slowly across a dry path. Heavy rain soon covered the path with puddles. The snail slid towards the wet garden soil. It left a shining trail behind its shell. We watched without lifting it from the ground.","A snowman is wearing a pair of sunglasses in sunshine.","A sock is folded on a shelf.","A soft bottle had a small hole near its base. While it rested upright, only a few drops escaped. When Leo squeezed its sides, water squirted through the hole. He stopped squeezing and the strong stream ended, though the hole remained.","A soft brush cleaned it.","A spider web starts with one thin thread. The wind carries the thread across a gap. The spider walks along it, adding more threads. The sticky web can trap flies for dinner.","A storm broke the bridge to Fox’s island. Rabbit had promised to bring Fox a birthday cake. She wrapped the cake, tied it safely inside a little boat and rowed across the calm inlet. Fox met her at the shore. They ate together while watching workers begin to mend the bridge.","A storm shook the windows. When the lights went out, Amir turned on a battery radio to hear the weather report. His sister found a torch but waited to use it until she went upstairs. Their father had switched off the television before the storm began.","A story by","A strange tapping came from the closed box. Luis tilted his head and moved closer. He asked what could be making the sound. Then he checked each side carefully for a clue.","A swing of the bat sent the ball toward the boundary.","A tadpole does not look like a frog. It has a tail and no legs. First the back legs grow. The front legs appear next. The tail gets shorter. At last the small frog can leave the pond.","A tailor helped us mend the class puppet. She put thread through the eye of a needle. Then she stitched the tear in its arm. Last, she tied a knot to hold the thread. The puppet was ready for our next story.","A thin gray kitten kept visiting Priya''s steps. Priya put out water for it each day. Each day the kitten crept closer. On Friday it curled up on her lap. She heard it purr softly.","A timetable hangs beside the door.","A tiny diamond glittered on the gold ring.","A visitor asked Nia where the community garden entrance was. Nia was unsure, but pointed confidently towards a locked gate. The visitor returned after walking a long way without finding it. Nia admitted she had guessed instead of knowing the answer. She asked the caretaker and walked with the visitor to the entrance. Later, someone asked her another question she could not answer. She paused. She said she was unsure and offered to find out. The second visitor thanked her for checking before giving directions.","A visitor dropped a ticket near the gate. Lee saw it fall but could not leave the gate, so he called to Sana. Sana picked up the ticket and handed it to Mo at the information window. Mo called the visitor’s name and returned the ticket when she came over.","A wasp flew through the window toward an open jam jar. Uncle Josh jumped back when he saw it. His elbow hit a flour bag, tipping it over. Flour fell onto the dishes below. He put the jam away before cleaning the dishes. The bag had been standing open near his arm. Its paper sides could not hold the powder once it tipped. The dishes were directly under the edge of the worktop. The insect never touched either the bag or the dishes.","A whistle blows beside the platform.","A wind farm stands on the hill above our town. Its tall towers hold blades that turn when wind pushes them. Equipment inside changes that movement into electricity for nearby homes. On still days, the blades rest and produce no power. Other sources must supply the electricity people need then. Workers check the towers and stop damaged machines for repairs. The town uses the wind farm alongside its other power sources. Nearby homes still need lights during long, calm evenings.","A wooden sign needs four preparation steps in this workshop. First, the maker rubs the rough board with sandpaper. A smooth surface is easier to write and paint on. Next, she draws the planned letters lightly in pencil. Those lines guide the brush during the following stage. She then paints the letters along the pencil shapes. The back needs hooks rather than painted letters. Last, she attaches hanging hooks to the back of the board. The hooks allow the prepared sign to hang from a rail.","A woolly hat helps keep the head warm.","A worker checked our tickets at the tall gate. We found numbered seats around a huge playing field. Two teams ran out as the crowd cheered. A referee walked into the centre with a ball.","A young dragon wanted to visit the village library. Every excited sneeze sent sparks from his nose, so the librarian worried about the books. They arranged a reading place in the stone courtyard. The dragon listened there while children took turns bringing stories outside to share.","Above the quiet town.","Ada’s first seedlings dried out. She learned when to water and checked the next plants each morning. Several weeks later they grew strong enough to plant outside. She kept caring for them even after the first flowers opened.","After eating, we packed the blanket away.","After practice, I stored the bat with my gloves and ball.","After testing and changing it, we had a strong bridge.","After the bread.","After the rain stopped outside.","After the rain stopped, we went outside.","After the rain, the playground was full of puddles. Children splashed in the deep puddle by the slide. Two friends raced leaf boats along the gutter stream. By lunch, the warm sun had dried the puddles.","After washing them, we cooked them for lunch.","Air trapped in the thick fur slows heat loss.","Alex, the person viewing Nina’s pictures","Alf guessed how much salt the soup needed. Everyone found it much too salty to enjoy. Next time, he used the recipe''s small measuring spoon. The new batch tasted right without any extra salt.","Ali Moss","All the beans and the sunflower have already sprouted.","All three buses are delayed by the same amount.","Allowing water to pass through the material.","Along the rope.","Although the hill was steep, we reached the top.","Although the steep hill beyond the village was covered with slippery stones.","Amara''s bike squeaked all the way to the park. Squeak, squeak, squeak. Her uncle helped her put oil on the chain. The bike was quiet on the way home. Amara smiled as she rode.","Amaya planned a lantern for the windy evening parade near the coast. She used a small frame with strong joins and thick covering. Friends chose taller shapes that looked grand beside her plain little lantern. Amaya checked her joins once more instead of adding extra height. Along the route, a gust tore the thin sides of several lanterns. Some large frames bent, and their owners carried them folded home. Amaya''s light stayed covered inside its firm frame throughout the walk. At the finish, she could still hold it upright beside her drawing.","Amir needed a hat to take part.","Amir packed his bag the evening before the trip. He rolled up a raincoat to save space. He added sandwiches and a bottle of water. His notebook and pencil were ready for bird spotting. He put a flashlight at the top of the bag. A pocket inside kept his writing things away from the food. The coat would protect him if the clear sky changed. His torch would help on the shaded path home. The bird guide belonged to the teacher, who would carry it separately.","Amir''s hot shower made the bathroom mirror steam up. He wiped it with a towel. Small towel threads stayed on the damp glass. When the glass dried, he could see those threads. He washed the mirror to remove them. The towel was old, with loose fibres along its edges. Those fibres caught on the wet surface during the first wipe. The steam itself cleared as the room cooled. Even after that, the tiny marks from the cloth were still visible.","Amir''s trumpet practice disturbed his sleeping baby sister. He tried the yard, but neighbours could still hear every note. The bathroom had an echo that made the sound louder. Dad helped him test another room with thick curtains and rugs. Outside that room, the family could hear much less noise. Amir agreed to practise there before his sister''s bedtime. He kept learning while people nearby could rest more easily. At bedtime, his trumpet was back in its case.","Amira found a bookmark beside the mystery books. Daniel''s name was written on it in blue ink. Amira''s own bookmark had her name in green. She gave the lost bookmark to the librarian. The librarian put it in Daniel''s book box. Both bookmarks were strips of yellow card. They also had the same star drawn near one end. Their names and ink colours made them different. The librarian checked the blue writing before choosing a box. Amira kept the green one in her pocket.","An ant can carry things bigger than itself. Ants work in long lines. One ant finds food and leaves a smell trail. The others follow the trail. The ants work together to carry food home.","An artist painted a whale above her own front door. A neighbour asked whether matching waves could cover his bare wall. More households joined after seeing the two pictures connect. They agreed on a sea scene before choosing their individual animals. Volunteers cleaned the walls while the artist marked the outlines. By summer, the paintings stretched along most of the short street. Visitors came to see how separate houses shared one picture. The first whale now joined an octopus several doors away.","An empty swing hung still. Sam gave the seat a gentle push, then stepped back. The swing moved away and returned several times, gradually slowing. A bird landed on the fence only after the swing was already moving.","An envelope lay beside an open ink bottle. When the bottle tipped, ink spread across the address and hid the house number. The letter inside stayed dry. Bea copied the address onto a clean envelope because the first one could no longer be read clearly.","An older gardener’s cart lost a wheel near the gate. Ella called for help and stayed to collect the fallen tools. Later, the gardener saw Ella puzzling over a bare flowerbed. He offered spare seedlings and advice, remembering how willingly she had helped him.","Apple. Listen to the first sound. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Ari checked his jacket pocket for the ticket, but found only a wrapper. Mum looked under his chair without success. She finally saw the ticket between two pages of his book. Ari put it safely in his pocket after she handed it back.","Ari could not find his yellow glove.","Ari followed a trail map while holding it upside down and took a wrong turn. At a signpost, he matched the map’s north arrow with the path directions. He found his way back. On later walks, he checked how the map was facing before choosing a trail.","Ari waved at someone he thought was his uncle. The stranger looked puzzled and pointed at himself. Ari''s cheeks grew hot, and he looked down. He stopped waving and stepped quietly behind Dad.","Ari''s family had a little tradition for a lost tooth. First, his loose tooth fell out during breakfast. It was one of the baby teeth already ready to go. That night, Ari put the tooth beneath his pillow. While he slept, Dad exchanged it for a small coin. In the morning, Ari found the coin in its place. Dad''s exchange explains why the objects were there at different times. The four events begin with losing the tooth and end with discovery.","Aria grabbed a puzzle piece and pushed it hard. Its corner bent because it did not fit. She stopped and looked closely at the picture. Then she turned another piece until it matched. After that, she checked before pressing each piece down.","Arlo carried warm rolls from the bakery counter. His paper bag tore near the door. Two rolls fell onto a clean tray. Baker Tom gave him a stronger bag. Arlo put every roll safely inside it.","Arrows mark every turning.","Arun heard a bird call from the reeds. He stopped talking and slowly lifted his binoculars. His friend pointed to a brown bird on a stem, and Arun turned the binoculars towards that exact spot.","Asha compared her shadow with marks on the playground. At nine, she marked its long outline towards the fence. At noon, she marked a short shape by her feet. The midday Sun was high above the playground. At three, she marked a longer outline on the opposite side. The Sun was lower and in a different direction. At five, she marked the longest evening outline towards the hedge. Four coloured outlines recorded the four checks. Each colour belonged to one time on her chart.","Asha noticed ice covering the path outside school. She told the office and asked for the path to be cleared. Mr Hall brought a bag of grit. A parent, Dan, helped him spread it across the ice. Later the headteacher thanked all three for making the path safer.","Asha tested a bridge made from folded paper. She added paper clips to its middle. Their weight became too much for the paper. The bridge bent down under the extra load.","Asha took the bus without Dad for the first time. His list showed each stop before the one near her school. The back seats were empty, and her friend waved from there. Asha waved back but chose a seat behind the driver. Twice she compared the names outside with Dad''s list. When two stops sounded alike, she leaned forward with a question. The driver pointed to the next street, and she nodded. She relaxed her grip on the list after seeing the school.","Asha volunteered to guide visitors around the school''s small history display. She had learned most labels but was unsure about one old photograph. Someone asked its date; she nearly chose a number that sounded right. Instead, she explained that she needed to check the original record. With the teacher, she found the dated entry and returned to answer. The visitor used that confirmed date in notes for a local article. Asha added a small reference beside the photograph for the next tour. She answered later questions confidently when she knew the source.","Asha wanted to play a fast rhythm at the concert. Each evening, her sticks missed the same beats halfway through. She tried hitting harder, but the mistake stayed the same. Her teacher asked her to play each part very slowly. Asha disliked how plain the slow rhythm sounded at first. Soon she could hear exactly where her hands lost time. She joined the parts, then carefully increased her speed. At the concert, she played the whole rhythm without stumbling.","At first, Dev lost his place whenever the drum pattern changed. He practised one short section each day and gradually joined the sections. After several weeks, he could keep the beat while the group sang. He still practised the parts that sometimes confused him.","At story time, Finn noticed a younger child could not see past the crowd. He moved aside and shared his front space. The next week, Finn arrived carrying a heavy craft box. The same child hurried to hold the door, smiling when he recognized Finn.","At the bus stop.","At the doorbell’s first ring, the dog woke up.","At the repair club, each child brought one broken object. Tomas brought a torch that no longer worked. The helper checked its bulb before opening the battery cover. The bulb was fine, but one battery was facing backwards. Tomas turned that battery around and closed the cover. The torch shone brightly when he pressed its button. He wrote the repair in his notebook to remember it. Then he helped carry the club''s tools to their cupboard.","At the soup garden, carrots follow four stages before cooking. First, children sow the seeds in loose garden soil. The growing roots will need space beneath the surface. Next comes a period of watering the developing carrot plants. Their leaves above ground help the roots grow below. When the carrots are ready, the children pull them from the soil. Finally, they wash the harvested roots in clean water. Soil belongs in the garden, not in the soup pot. Harvest must therefore come before this last cleaning stage.","At this recycling plant, used glass follows four main stages. Workers first sort the glass into groups by colour. Keeping colours separate helps control the colour of new glass. A machine then crushes each group into small pieces. Those smaller pieces are easier to heat evenly. Next, a very hot furnace melts the crushed glass. Finally, the liquid glass is shaped into new containers. Hard pieces cannot fill a container mould like flowing glass. The plant keeps each stage in its own marked area.","Ate it in very small mouthfuls.","Auntie Vee","Auntie, thank you for singing to me.","Auntie, which song did you sing at school?","Auntie, would you like to come to our school concert?","Ava had two bread slices for her lunch. The cheese would go between them, not outside. Ava put bread on a plate. She added cheese to one slice. Then she closed the sandwich.","Ava’s first woven mat had loose, uneven strips. She looked closely at a finished example. She adjusted her weave and tried several small mats. Her next one lay flat and held together.","Aya counted her money and put on her coat. She checked the note Dad had written. They needed honey for their pancakes at breakfast. Aya lifted her shopping bag and opened the door.","Ba visited the shelter, where three small dogs needed homes. Each dog came forward when the keeper opened its gate. A grey terrier rested its nose gently against Ba''s hand. Ba asked about that dog''s walking needs and wrote them down. At home, he checked those notes against his own daily routine. He put the terrier''s photo beside his bed and kept the notes. The shelter''s telephone number was printed below every dog''s picture. His family agreed to discuss whether they could care for a dog.","Baby Ren by himself","Bats rest in dark places during the day. At dusk, some hunt flying insects. They use sounds and echoes to find their way. Their calls help them locate food in the darkness. They return to rest when the night ends.","Bea''s balloon slipped from her hand at the fair. She watched it float higher above the stalls. Her lip wobbled as the string disappeared. She wiped one wet eye with her sleeve.","Beavers build dams from branches and mud. The dam slows water and creates a deeper pond. A pond can protect the entrance to a beaver’s home and give other animals a place to live. A beaver’s building changes its surroundings.","Because the bright morning sun came out from behind the clouds.","Bees make honey through several connected stages in their hive. First, foraging bees collect sweet nectar from flowers. That liquid contains more water than finished honey does. Next, hive bees place the prepared nectar in wax cells. They then fan their wings across those open cells. Moving air helps water leave the nectar as it thickens. Finally, bees seal the ready honey beneath wax covers. The cells need to stay open during the fanning stage. A cover belongs at the end of this four-stage account.","Bees visit many flowers on one trip. They drink a sweet juice called nectar. They pass the nectar to bees inside the hive. Slowly the nectar thickens into honey. One jar of honey takes thousands of flower visits.","Before leaving, the children filled their hamster''s bowl with small pellets. They smoothed the paper bedding and checked the cage door. A camera took pictures inside the cage throughout the night. In the morning, many pellets had gone from the bowl. Fresh tunnels crossed the paper, with loose pieces beside the wheel. Nobody had opened the cage since the children left. One picture showed a blurred shape beside the turning wheel. Now the hamster lay curled in its usual sleeping corner.","Before the little shop closed.","Before the morning lesson, a branch fell onto a power line. The lights in the school went out soon afterwards. The teacher opened the blinds so the room was easier to see. Pupils moved their books closer to the windows. A repair team removed the branch and restored the supply. The lights came back on during lunchtime. Nobody at school had switched off the supply to the building. The open blinds helped the class, but did not repair the line.","Before the shop closed, we bought some milk.","Bel''s first cookies burned. She nearly gave up. Then she checked the recipe and found her mistake. The oven had been too hot. Her next batch cooked well at the right heat.","Ben and his partner painted different parts of a long school mural. While she fetched supplies, he noticed their patterns did not join neatly. Painting over her section would let him finish his own design quickly. Instead, Ben waited and asked what her curved shapes were showing. She explained that they followed the river beside their school. They joined his straight paths to her curves using small painted bridges. Both original patterns remained visible when the wall was finished. The two artists signed beside their separate ideas within the shared scene.","Ben began a model before reading all the instructions. He glued two parts together that were meant to move. He carefully separated them and studied the diagram. When he restarted, he checked each step first. The finished model’s wheels turned freely.","Ben had counted the days until the boat trip. His packed bag waited by the door. When the trip was cancelled because of strong winds, he lowered his head and slowly unpacked the binoculars. He asked whether there might be another trip next month.","Ben needed bus twelve. A bus approached showing twelve above its windscreen. He checked the number once more, closed his book and stood near the boarding point with his travel card ready. The bus slowed beside him.","Beside the basket full of apples.","Beside the bell.","Big fires often have more firefighters than small fires. Ana wondered which event happened first. Her poster showed that a fire grows before extra crews arrive. More crews are called because a fire has become large. A row of pictures showed a call for help between them. The first crew needed more people to handle the spreading flames. The next picture showed those helpers arriving with extra equipment. The pictures did not show the arriving crews making the fire larger.","Bird. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Birds began landing where they could reach the food.","Birds sang outside our classroom window.","Black clouds covered the park during their picnic. Thunder rumbled, and the wind shook their blanket. Mom packed the food while Dad fetched the keys. They lifted their bags and faced the car.","Both bags were without the lunch.","Both bridges are open. Choose either bridge.","Both groups finished their pictures without arguing.","Both the climbing frame and the slide are open again.","Both this week and next week are completely full.","Bread","Brief while still giving the information that is needed.","Bring any red umbrella with a handle.","Bring my towel with yellow-striped edges.","Bring the red umbrella with a curved handle and silver tip.","Bring the red umbrella with a silver handle and curved tip.","Bruno heard the wooden gate creak outside the house. That noise made him bark. Then a delivery worker rang the doorbell. Bruno had barked before the worker reached the door.","Builders were using a loud machine outside the classroom. The teacher shut the window, which made the noise much quieter inside. The children could hear their partner read again. The machine kept working outside, and the building fence stayed in the same place.","Bus 2 is delayed; buses 3 and 4 are on time.","Bus 4 is delayed; buses 2 and 3 are on time.","Busy with movement, voices and activity.","By sharing seeds, we filled all the garden boxes.","Caleb carried a pencil, a ruler and a small brush in his apron. He drew a dot with the pencil, then put it away. He took out the ruler and measured the board. Only after cutting the board did he use the brush to sweep the sawdust from the table.","Can you fix the string on my kite?","Car. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Careful measuring helped us make the costume fit.","Carmen","Carry the full cup to the table.","Cats and dogs","Cats have soft fur.","Ceri kept watching for snow outside the window. Grandma suggested getting ready while she waited. Ceri dried her gloves and checked her old sled. When snow finally came, everything was ready to use.","Changing where we looked from helped us find the glove.","Children can enter after an adult opens the gate.","Children enjoy splashing in a swimming pool.","Children helped cut the paper used for each label.","Children often gathered wherever the school cat sat. A visitor thought their voices called the cat over. The teacher watched more carefully. The cat chose a sunny place first. Only then did children walk over to sit nearby. On one morning, the children were inside until a late break. The cat was already stretched out beside the warm wall. When a cloud shaded that patch, it moved to another corner. Children joined it there only after they came out for playtime.","Children use the front door. Adults wait at the side gate.","Children use the side door. Adults wait at the side gate.","Children use the side gate. Adults wait at the front door.","Choose a photograph that fits the card.","Choose any small bag of beans.","Choose the large bean bag marked with a ladder picture.","Choose the missing letters for __in.","Choose the small bean bag marked with a ladder picture.","Close the cover over the battery.","Cloud Shapes","Cold weather cracked the clay pot on the balcony. Soil fell through the crack during the week. Without enough soil holding it, the rosemary became loose. Wind then tipped the plant and pot over. The crash made pigeons fly away. The cracked container had once held the roots firmly upright. Losing that support left a space around the roots. The rosemary was already leaning before the strong gust arrived. The birds had been resting on a different balcony nearby.","Come and decorate the biscuits we baked this morning.","Come and mix the dough for tomorrow’s biscuits.","Come and watch us eat the biscuits we decorated.","Come for a walk!","Come to chess club; we will only watch other people play.","Come to chess club; we will show you how to play.","Come to chess club; you must already know every rule.","Come to my puppet show!","Come to our play; everyone must wear a costume.","Come to our play; please leave your costume at home.","Come to our play; wear a costume if you wish.","Come to story hour; you do not have to borrow a book.","Come to story hour; you must borrow a book to enter.","Come to story hour; you must bring a book to read aloud.","Completely clean, without dirt or marks.","Corn. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Could I have some water, please?","Could our class plant a tree next year?","Could you carry both things while I walk ahead with nothing?","Could you take the heavy box while I carry the light bag?","Could you take the light bag while I carry this heavy box?","Could you tell me the colour of the door?","Cover the seed with soil.","Cover the table to protect it.","Crabs cover rocks.","Cross immediately, then wait for the helper on the road.","Cross the narrow bridge beside the mill.","Cross the wide bridge beside the mill.","Cross whichever bridge you reach first.","Cut a length of string.","Cut the lemon into halves.","Cut the orange in half.","Dad could smell the soup through the closed door.","Dad flips the calendar to a new month. Everyone adds their days. Swimming badge test for Lena. Dentist for Dad. Grandma''s visit gets a big red circle. The little squares fill up with the family''s plans.","Dad had promised a sponge cake for the afternoon visitors. When they arrived, he met them wearing an oven mitt. A blackened cake sat cooling on a tray by the sink. The windows were open although the afternoon air was chilly. Dad had switched the oven off and checked it was safe. He carried a shopping bag towards the bakery across the road. Clean plates and candles still waited on the table. The visitors offered to put those ready while he went out.","Dad left crayons in the very hot car. Heat softened their wax during the day. After lunch, Mina opened the crayon box. The crayons had melted into one colorful lump.","Dad on the ladder beside the wall.","Dad plans the vegetable patch before the first spring shoots appear. He chooses plants that will be ready at different times. We pick little radishes while the climbing beans are still small. Those beans fill our bowls during the warmer summer months. In autumn, we dig potatoes from beneath their dying leaves. Winter frost covers the paths, but hardy kale remains green. Dad leaves room for each crop instead of planting everything together. After picking one crop, he prepares its space for another.","Dad planted a tree when each child was born. Asha''s tree has cherries, and Ben''s has apples. Baby Mo has the youngest tree, a plum. This year, the apple tree gave the most fruit. Ben helped Dad carry all its full baskets. The cherries ripened first, but there were only two small baskets. The young plum tree gave just a handful of fruit. Dad wrote each child''s name beside the harvest on a chart. The fullest row belonged to the child with the apple tree.","Dad printed a photo from the school concert. Zoe stood beside the choir teacher in it. Dad stood behind them in the back row. Zoe chose a yellow frame for the photo. She put it above her desk.","Dad stepped on a creaky board while fetching Biscuit''s meal. Hearing that familiar noise, the dog came into the kitchen. A visitor thought Dad fetched food because Biscuit had arrived. But Dad had started before the dog left its bed. The clock showed the dog''s usual feeding time. Dad followed that time even when Biscuit was asleep. His footsteps made the sound that drew the dog closer. The visitor had noticed the response without seeing its earlier trigger.","Dad swam in the sea yesterday.","Dad turned on the light to wake me.","Dad wanted the hall for a school meeting. A sign at the corner pointed left. Its arrow was beside the word Hall. Dad followed that direction without asking anyone for help.","Dad wrapped the cup in soft paper and put it into a box. He added a note on top of the cup. Before sealing the parcel, he checked that the note was inside. Then he sealed the lid with tape. Finally, he wrote the address on the outside and carried the parcel to the post office.","Dad, I have finished this puzzle.","Dad, join my puzzle!","Dad, where did you buy this puzzle?","Damaged as the metal slowly rusted away.","Dan drew a chalk outline beside a bucket of clean water. He planned to paint inside it after fetching a brush. When he returned, his little brother was running towards the house. The bucket lay on its side where the boy had passed. Water spread from its open top across the nearest chalk lines. Lines farther away remained dry and clearly visible on the ground. There had been no rain, and the paving beyond was dry. Dan lifted the empty bucket before looking at the missing lines.","Dara promised to water her neighbour''s young tree. She forgot, and its green leaves began to curl. She made a daily reminder and followed it. Soon, the tree began growing healthy new leaves again.","Dara was last in the long school race. She kept running towards the finish line. At the end, she punched the air. She showed everyone her finisher''s ribbon with a smile.","Dark clouds gathered above the school field. The class had planned to eat outside. Miss Green heard thunder beyond the trees. She moved the children into the school hall. Rain began while they ate their lunch.","Dark clouds gathered over our picnic.","Dead leaves fall onto the soil in autumn.","Dee left the bath tap running to answer her phone. Water rose over the bath''s edge during the long call. It ran through a gap beside the pipe. The water then made a mark on the ceiling below. Dee saw the mark after ending the call. The ceiling mark was directly beneath that part of the bathroom. No pipe had burst inside the wall. A dry towel covered the floor on the other side. The open space around the pipe gave the spilled water a route.","Deep snow covered the ground beside the house. The snowman needed a body underneath its head. The boy rolled a large snowball. He added a smaller one for the head. Then he gave the snowman a hat.","Dev dropped his mitten somewhere in the snow. He walked back along his own footprints to look. A red mitten hung on the gate post. Someone had left it there for him to find.","Dev saw his sister at the station after her long trip. He ran towards her, dropped his bag and hugged her tightly. He began asking questions before she could answer the first one. He kept smiling all the way home.","Did a chick hatch at your house?","Did you enjoy my puppet show?","Did you make a kite at home yesterday?","Different people worked together to prepare our play.","Dip the brush into paint.","Disappear from view.","Does your class have a fish?","Drink the water from the cup.","Drop the envelope in the post box.","Drop the sealed envelope into the post box.","During a dry week, Bea offered water from her full tank to a neighbour with thirsty plants. Later, when Bea was away, the neighbour checked Bea’s garden each day and watered the new seedlings. Bea returned to healthy plants and a note welcoming her home.","During an evening sky watch, the group first pointed its telescope at the moon. Clouds then covered the moon, so the leader turned the telescope towards a bright planet. The children took turns looking at the planet. They discussed distant stars, but did not point the telescope at them.","Dylan checked the museum map on the bus. The dinosaur room was beside the stairs. The cafe was beside the gift shop. He showed the map to his partner. They found the dinosaur room after arriving.","Each Friday, Sami borrowed a book from the class shelf. He always chose a thin book and returned it quickly. One week, the teacher offered to read its opening with him. Sami stayed after the others had left to play. He followed the words with his finger and asked about two. The next morning, he chose that same book again. At home time, he asked whether he could keep it longer. The teacher smiled and put a bookmark inside for him.","Each team could choose three tools for the building task. Noor chose scissors, a ruler, and tape. She left the glue and paint on the supply table. Her plan was a tall paper tower with straight sides. Measuring mattered because every side needed to be equally long. Cutting would make the shapes, and tape would hold them together. Only three tools could travel to the workbench. The table stood beside the sink, holding the unused supplies.","Effie''s cousin","Egg. Listen to the first sound. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Ela recognised a paint smell as soon as she came home. The hallway walls were still yellow, without any fresh patches. On the balcony, her old red bicycle stood on spread newspapers. Its bent bell and worn saddle were exactly as before. The frame was silver now, and wet drops marked the paper. Mom had matching spots on her hands beside an open tin. A clean brush rested across the top of that tin. Ela waited before touching the bicycle or rolling it indoors.","Eleven children argued over one long jump rope. Fern suggested two jumps each, then changing places. Everyone agreed and waited for their own turn. This time, each child could join the shared game.","Eli had spent weeks helping paint the school mural. At its opening, he stood a little taller when visitors admired the river he had painted. He pointed out the tiny fish to his family and smiled while explaining how he had mixed the colours.","Eli saw a squirrel near the park bench. It held an acorn between its front paws. A pine cone lay beside the bench. The children stepped back to give it space. Soon the squirrel ran up a tree.","Eli usually chose the seat next to the classroom window. On Tuesday, he moved his chair beside a new pupil. The pupil had arrived that morning and spoke very quietly. Eli pointed to the picture timetable and showed his own work. At break, he waited while the new pupil found a coat. They walked outside together and joined a small group. Eli''s usual friends waved to him from the far wall. He waved back but stayed with his new companion.","Emil wanted to become a better goalkeeper. He practiced catching carefully every evening. Some balls slipped past, but he tried again. In the final game, he saved a difficult shot.","Empty of the things that normally filled them.","Enter through any gate that opens onto the path.","Erin West","Etta found a feathered young bird hopping beneath a thick hedge. She fetched a box, wanting to carry it into the house. A wildlife helper asked her to describe the bird before acting. The helper explained that its parent might still be feeding it. Etta put the box away and kept the cat indoors instead. She watched from a distance without standing beside the bird. Soon an adult bird arrived with food and flew away again. Etta left the youngster sheltered beneath the hedge as advised.","Euan''s boots were muddy before his football match. The evening before it, he cleaned the mud away. Clean studs would grip the field better than clogged ones. At noon on match day, he checked the team list. His name was beside the left-wing position. At one, the whole team began its warm-up together. That exercise prepared their muscles for running across the field. At two, the referee blew the starting whistle. Euan''s schedule put preparation before play across two separate days.","Eva Hill","Eva’s toy cart had a wobbly wheel. A loose screw let it move sideways. Eva tightened that screw with a small screwdriver. The firm screw kept the turning wheel steady.","Every model roof leaks even after all its joins are repaired.","Every morning Mr Pole stands at the school crossing. He raises his round sign to stop the cars. He smiles and waves the children across. He arrives before the bell in any weather.","Every piece was in place and our whole puzzle was complete.","Every tray needed exactly the same amount of water.","Everyone had a job during the garden harvest. The tall cousins picked green beans. The younger cousins dug potatoes with small trowels. Grandma weighed the vegetables on her kitchen scale. The largest pumpkin went home in a wheelbarrow. The beans and potatoes went into baskets before weighing. Beans were high on poles, where longer arms could reach them. Potatoes were hidden below the soil and needed careful digging. The pumpkin was too heavy for a basket, so its wheels helped.","Everyone rose before the match finished.","Evi walked along the village path before most neighbours left home. After windy nights, she checked for small branches across the paving. She moved the light ones and told an adult about heavy ones. When the path was clear, she continued to school as usual. Most walkers arrived later and never saw who had checked it. Once, a neighbour noticed and offered to mention her in the newsletter. Evi accepted thanks but kept doing the checks on unmentioned days too. Pushchairs and walking sticks passed easily along the cleared route.","Extremely bright to look at.","Extremely cold and uncomfortable.","Families helped repair the costumes for our play.","Felix rode to the market and locked his bicycle to a rail by the fountain. He carried his basket to the fruit stall. A broken basket handle meant he had to visit the repair tent too. When shopping was finished, he returned to the fountain and unlocked the bicycle.","Fill the cup with water.","Find the book showing a train.","Finn called hello inside an empty tunnel. His voice hit the hard walls. The sound bounced back toward his ears. Finn heard his own hello again. Only Finn was inside that tunnel.","Finn carried a blanket to the picnic. His aunt carried a basket of bread. They spread the blanket under a tree. Finn’s cousin arrived with apples, and they all sat down to eat.","Finn carried books to the little free library. Its shelf was almost full of small books. He put the large books at the bottom. The small books stood above them in rows. Now every book fitted on the shelf.","Finn helped a new player learn the team''s game. Later, Finn forgot his trainers for practice. The player offered a spare pair that fitted. Finn thanked him, and they joined the practice together.","Fit the poles into the tent.","Flew high while being carried through the air.","Follow a path that goes somewhere.","Follow one of the ways through the park.","Follow the path that passes the windmill.","For forty years, the lighthouse keeper checked the lamp each night. He cleaned the glass even when nobody visited the little island. Most sailors saw the signal without ever learning his name. He sometimes wondered whether anyone noticed the hours spent maintaining it. At retirement, letters arrived from ships that had followed its light. One sailor described finding the harbour safely during a dark storm. The keeper read the letter beside his neatly stored cleaning tools. He left the tools ready for the person taking his place.","For one month we recorded the weather each morning. We drew cloud shapes, noted rain and checked a thermometer. At the end, we grouped the records by week. The notebook showed that one wet week was warmer than a later dry week, which surprised us.","For the performance, the choir wore green scarves over white shirts. Each singer held a small lamp. They stood in two rows on the stage. The lamps ran on batteries, so there were no open flames. The back row stood on a low step behind the front. All the scarves were the same bright shade of green. Their white shirts made those scarves easy to see from far away. These clothes and the soft lights were part of the plan.","Gave them something worthwhile in return for their effort.","Give Grandma one of the things on the table.","Give Grandma something that came in the post.","Give Grandma the string-tied brown parcel.","Giving only a weak amount of light.","Going around and returning to the starting place.","Grace could not find her red pencil. Maya had only a blue pencil nearby. Noah lent Grace his spare red pencil. She used it to finish her drawing. At the end, she gave it back.","Grandad showed us how to make his bean soup. We wrote down the ingredients as he used them and measured amounts he usually guessed. Then we tried the written recipe ourselves. When the soup was too thick, he helped us correct the water amount on the page.","Grandma answered the telephone while a football match filled the room. The caller was explaining which entrance to use at the station. Grandma wrote one word, then asked for the message again. She pressed the phone closer, but the crowd on television cheered. In the hall, she closed the door behind her. Her pen began moving steadily across the little notepad. She repeated the instructions back and thanked the caller. Afterwards, she returned to the same chair and watched the match.","Grandma gave me a silver ring in a tiny box.","Grandma lived far away. Bo drew a birthday picture, while Hana planned a message. Dad showed Hana which button to press on the recorder. Hana then pressed it and spoke the message herself. Dad sent the recording with a photograph of Bo’s picture.","Grandma wrote a short shopping list for Tayo. It said six eggs and two lemons. She also needed one small bag of sugar. Tayo repeated the list while he walked. He brought everything home from the shop.","Grandpa Tan fixed his neighbours'' umbrellas for free. A storm later damaged his roof. Those neighbours arrived with tools and warm food. They helped him until the roof was safe.","Grandpa and a child are flying a red kite.","Grandpa expected a quiet afternoon alone. When he opened the door, his grandchildren stood there with a picnic basket. His eyebrows rose and he said, “You’re here!” Then he laughed and quickly made room for everyone to come inside.","Grandpa folded a paper plane at the kitchen table. He showed Hana where to press each corner firmly. Dad recognised the design from lessons thirty years earlier. His first planes had crashed, just like Hana''s first attempt. Together they checked her uneven wings and adjusted the folds. This time, her plane crossed the kitchen in a straight line. Hana wrote the folding steps in her own picture book. She wanted to show her little cousin when he visited.","Grandpa helped Suvi repair his old toy train. She checked its wheels and cleaned each part. Her first repair did not work. After another careful check, the train ran again.","Grandpa kept his shed tidy. Screwdrivers hung on hooks in size order. Clear jars of screws stood on the window shelf. The lawn mower stayed under its cover. A radio near the door played music while he worked. There was a space between the jars for a small torch. Grandpa could see the smallest screws through the clear glass. The mower''s cover kept dust off its wheels and handle. Hook labels showed where each screwdriver belonged after use.","Grandpa left the garden gnome beside the pond before breakfast. Later, it stood beneath the rose bush wearing a tiny scarf. Grandpa had been indoors, and the gardener was beyond the fence. A neighbour''s young daughter had brought her doll through the gate. Her doll now wore a hat but no matching scarf. Small shoe marks led from the pond towards the bush. When called for lunch, she carefully straightened the gnome''s new scarf. The gardener carried only his usual box of fence tools.","Grandpa watered his cactus much more often than it needed. Its pot had no hole to drain extra water. The roots stayed wet and began to rot. Cold air by the window made the plant weaker too. Water collected at the bottom after each extra drink. The wet soil had no chance to dry between waterings. A plant guide described this cactus as needing warm, dry conditions. The soaked roots and cold window worked against those needs.","Green fields slid past the window beside their seats. Wheels rattled along the metal tracks below them. A voice announced the next stop on their journey. A worker pushed snacks down the aisle.","Hail bounced across the trampoline during the storm.","Han wanted a magnifying glass to study insects. Each week, he saved coins from small jobs. Twice, he chose saving instead of buying sweets. His jar slowly filled until he could buy it.","Hana drew a chalk rocket on the path. Rain fell on it through the night. Water washed away most of the chalk. Only a faint pink mark remained next morning.","Hana sorted games after the class played indoors. The puzzles went on the top shelf. She put card games in the red bin. One puzzle box still had its lid open. Hana checked its pieces and closed it.","Hana wanted to read the plant signs in her grandparents’ garden. Each visit, they helped her learn a few unfamiliar words. She looked for the same words in books and on seed packets. Later she could read a new planting sign and explain it to her younger brother.","Hana''s group each received a different part of a treasure map. Hana kept her piece hidden because it showed the final landing place. Others could see safe paths but not where those paths should end. Their first route stopped at a river with no crossing nearby. Hana unfolded her piece when she saw that nobody could finish alone. Together, they joined the sections and found a bridge upstream. Each child explained a clue missing from the others'' pieces. The group reached the landing place using the map they had assembled.","Harri told his cousin that winning last year''s race hardly mattered. His cousin noticed the medal hanging above Harri''s desk. Nearby stood several other prizes, some already covered with dust. Every Sunday, Harri took down the race medal and polished it. He carefully returned it to the centre of the display afterwards. He had also saved the finishing photograph inside a clear cover. The cousin remembered that rain had fallen throughout that race. Harri put the cloth away beside his ordinary school cleaning supplies.","Having a harsh taste without sweetness.","Having little energy after hard effort.","He blew them away.","He coiled it neatly.","He cut the string.","He dragged it through mud.","He fitted it back on, and the car rolled again.","He found the glove and put it on.","He looked beside the gate where he had dropped it.","He loosened the knot.","He loosened the string.","He tore the paper.","He untied it.","He used a damp cloth.","He used a dry brush.","Heavy rain was falling outside Eli’s front door. His feet and head needed protection from it. Eli put on his boots. Then he opened his umbrella. Last, he walked outside.","Hedgehog arrived at a party carrying a balloon. When he hugged his friends, his prickles burst it. He worried that he would spoil the games too. His friends made a game of rolling apples towards baskets. Hedgehog joined happily, and no balloons were needed for the game.","Hedgehogs often travel through several gardens while looking for food. A small gap below a fence can let them pass. In autumn, a quiet leaf pile offers a sheltered resting place. People should check garden fires for hidden animals before lighting them. Netting left loose on the ground can trap small legs. Rolling it away after use removes that danger. A shallow water dish can help during dry weather. A fence gap must stay clear when neighbours replace old boards.","Helpers prepared the field for sports day. They painted white lanes on the grass. They counted beanbags into buckets beside the track. They tied the finish tape between two posts. Finally, someone tested the loudspeaker before the teams arrived. The buckets had team colours painted on their sides. The lanes needed enough room for runners to pass safely. The finish tape was loose enough to break when touched. The speaker test checked whether distant teams could hear instructions.","Her clothes might get dirty.","Her","Her. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Here is how to play our board game.","Hiding the birthday surprise from her sister was difficult.","Him","How long is the swing’s rope?","I am going to draw a picture now.","I am looking for my spotted scarf.","I am looking for my striped scarf.","I am looking for my striped socks.","I can run faster with my shoes tied.","I carried my bag to school today.","I dried the ball with a towel.","I found a green coat.","I heard rain on the roof last night.","I hung the finished painting to dry.","I lifted the wet ball out.","I like books with bright covers.","I lost a large blue toy frog.","I lost a large brown dog with four white paws.","I lost a small brown dog with one white paw.","I lost a small green toy car.","I lost a small green toy frog.","I lost a small white dog with one brown paw.","I lost a thing that covers my hand.","I lost a warm thing that I wear outside.","I lost my blue bag.","I lost my green bag.","I lost my red hat with a star.","I lost one black rain boot.","I lost one yellow rain boot.","I lost one yellow raincoat.","I painted a blue boat on it.","I poured water into an empty tray.","I pulled off the wrapping.","I put clean paper on the table.","I saw some seeds in the shed.","I still needed to choose a clean brush.","I still needed to take the plant out of its old pot.","I swam ten lengths at the pool today.","I swam without arm bands today.","I was ready to go to sleep.","I was ready to leave for school.","I was ready to start mixing paints.","I went for a walk yesterday.","I will buy new arm bands today.","I will sit near the window.","I will teach you a game tomorrow.","Ice covered the garden path.","If the rain stops before lunch.","If the rain stops, we can play outside.","Imani heard wheels spinning while an adult tightened a chain. Pumps stood beside a rack of helmets. She pointed to her flat tyre, and the adult lifted her bicycle onto a stand to examine it.","Ines could clap the song but missed its ending. She practised just that small part with a friend. Then they tried the whole song again. This time her final clap landed with everyone else''s. She smiled and joined the group for another turn.","Inez watched a group play from the edge of the yard. She wanted to join but had not spoken. A player waved her over and explained the game. Inez’s tight shoulders relaxed, and soon she was laughing and taking her turn with the others.","Insect. Listen to the first sound. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Inside was a red kite.","Iris and Malik sat down to play chess. Ren had taught them the rules and offered to watch. Ren pressed the clock after each turn and announced when their time was nearly over. The club leader wrote the result on a chart once Iris and Malik finished playing.","Iris often helped her brother climb the bus steps. One morning, Iris slipped and dropped her books. Her brother hurried over with his friends. Together they picked up every book for her.","It can look for food in water.","It can rest beside a pond.","It carried a crumb.","It crossed the bridge.","It did not really happen.","It does not fold, and its front wheel is green.","It folds, and its back wheel is green.","It folds, and its front wheel is green.","It happened long ago.","It has a fox-patterned cover with no sticker on the back.","It has a plain cover with a fox sticker on the back.","It has a plain cover with a fox sticker on the front.","It has long ears, a short tail and one button eye.","It has long ears, a short tail and two button eyes.","It has one section, with a red lid across the whole box.","It has short ears, a long tail and one button eye.","It has two sections, with a red lid on the larger one.","It has two sections, with a red lid on the smaller one.","It hurried past the snail.","It is a new rule.","It is always fair.","It is black with a clear strip beside the zip.","It is blue outside with yellow stars inside.","It is blue outside with yellow stars on top.","It is clear with a black strip around the bottom.","It is clear with a black strip beside the zip.","It is not fair.","It is yellow outside with blue stars inside.","It never snows near our home.","It really did happen.","It rolled along the ground.","It slid along the leaf.","It snowed at our home last night.","It stepped along stones.","It swam through deep water.","It was carried by wind.","It was thrown by a bird.","Its body is covered with feathers.","Ivo stood at his new school gate. He held Dad''s hand tightly and swallowed hard. He knew none of the children passing by. He quietly practised saying hello before taking a step.","Ivy found a coin near the classroom door. She gave it to Ms. Lopez. The teacher used the box for lost things. She placed the coin inside that box. Omar came looking for his coin after school.","Jas had moved to a new house with a much larger garden. Her old bedroom was smaller, and its window looked onto shops. She unpacked her clothes quickly and chose where her books would go. However, she saved the old front-door key instead of discarding it. She wore it on a cord and explained where it came from. On quiet evenings, she added captions to photographs of her old room. The new bedroom already held all the furniture she needed. Her family planned to plant flowers in their new garden soon.","Jay made a bright poster for a school fair but copied the date incorrectly. Several families asked about the wrong day. Jay corrected the posters and apologized. For the next event, he asked a partner to check every important fact before making copies, and the notices were accurate.","Jin practiced flipping a paper pancake in a pan. The pan was cold, so he could practice safely. On Sunday he tried using a real pancake. His dad watched from the table. The pancake spun up and landed in the pan.","Jo chose a shiny watering can for the narrow school planters. Its wide spout sent water onto the path instead of the roots. She liked its bright pattern and tried pouring from different angles. The leaves still missed much of the water they needed. An old dented can had a long spout that reached each plant. Jo tested it without spilling more than a few drops. Next morning, both cans waited beside the tap. She passed the shiny one and carried the dented can outside.","Jo won three races while wearing her red socks. She said the socks had made her faster. Her coach showed her the training chart from two months. She had practiced on every marked day. The coach said that work had made her faster. The chart also showed her practice times getting shorter each week. Some of those quicker runs happened in plain white socks. Her first prize came after the improvement was already recorded. The rewards followed her speed; they could not explain the earlier progress.","Jo","Jo, I will come to your house after lunch to paint.","Jo, come to my house after lunch to paint.","Jo, come to my house before lunch to paint.","Join our games indoors; if it rains, we will go outside.","Join our games outside; if it rains, we will cancel them.","Join our games outside; if it rains, we will play indoors.","Join our walk with Mum; please bring a hat.","Join our walk with Mum; please bring a toy.","Join our walk without Mum; please bring a hat.","Join the sides to make the tunnel.","Jonah usually played forward, scoring goals near the other team''s net. During training, the coach asked everyone to try different positions. Jonah caught several difficult shots when his turn in goal came. He also called clear warnings that helped defenders move into place. The usual goalkeeper was present and fit for Saturday''s game. However, the coach placed Jonah in goal for that match. Some parents looked surprised because they remembered his earlier position. The coach pointed towards the notes she had made during training.","Jonah walked on the beach with his aunt. They looked for empty shells to take home. A tiny crab sat inside one shell. Jonah left that shell where it was. He took three empty shells instead.","Jory left Ann''s comic in the rain. The pages stuck together. He wanted to hide it, but told Ann instead. He bought a new copy with his savings. Ann thanked him for being honest.","Jude spotted broken glass on the slide. He warned younger children and called an adult. He missed his game while keeping them away. Later, the children gave him a thank-you note.","Jules borrowed a neighbour''s plant pot for a school display. While carrying it home, he caught its edge against the gate. A crack spread down the side, though it still stood upright. He first placed it where the damaged part faced the wall. Before returning it, he moved it back and told the neighbour. Jules offered his pocket money and helped choose a suitable replacement. The neighbour thanked him for explaining without waiting to be questioned. Jules carried the new pot with both hands on the next visit.","Jules carefully measured the ingredients, but forgot to grease the tin. When the cake cooled, it stuck to the metal. The next cake used the same recipe in a greased tin and came out easily.","Jules was putting on a coat when its lining caught in the zip. The zip stopped halfway, leaving the coat open. Jules’s brother had already found his gloves. Mum gently pulled the lining free, and then Jules could close the coat before they went outside.","June Ray","June","Kai fell many times while learning to skate. He practised stopping on a safe patch each afternoon. A month later he stopped in time when a small child crossed his path. He knew the practice had helped.","Kai was thirsty after a game outside. His cup was empty beside the kitchen tap. Kai filled a cup with water. He drank the water. Then he put the cup in the sink.","Kai''s first paper boat sank in the tray. He unfolded it and looked at the wet corner. He made another with a firmer fold there. This boat floated across the whole tray. Kai kept the old boat to remember his change.","Keep the volume high while putting the headphones on.","Keep walking past the crossing without watching the helper.","Kim Lake","Kip made a ramp that sloped down. He let go of a marble at the top. The slope made it roll toward the floor. It stopped when it hit the wall.","Kit broke the class crayon and hid it. Later, he told Miss May what had happened. She showed him how to tape it together. He wished he had told her sooner.","Kofi bounced a ball in the stairwell after the rain stopped. The echoes were exciting, and he had not noticed the nearby door. Mrs Adjei opened it and spoke to him quietly. She explained that a baby inside had just fallen asleep. Kofi lowered the ball when he understood who could hear it. She showed him a dry outdoor space with room for bouncing. He moved his game there and invited two friends to join. Next day, he checked the stairwell door before deciding where to play.","Kofi studied hard for his spelling test. He hoped to get every word right. His test came back with six mistakes. His smile faded as he looked at the marks.","Labels identify the right drawer.","Labels make classroom routines easier.","Labels tell us where to find our classroom supplies.","Last spring, water entered Auntie Fern''s low garden workshop. Her paper seed packets were soaked on the bottom shelf. The dry packets upstairs produced seedlings, but the soaked batch failed. Before this winter, she moved the new packets much higher. She also placed them inside dry jars with tight lids. The old paper labels stayed inside, where she could read them. When a storm warning arrived, she checked each lid carefully. She left her empty flowerpots on the floor as usual.","Later, we took ice out of the tray.","Lay the paper on the table.","Lea''s neighbour found the bracelet she had lost. Lea held it tightly and smiled at him. She thanked him twice and offered a hug. Then she made him a card showing the bracelet.","Leah rubbed a balloon against her soft woollen sweater. Rubbing gave it a tiny electric charge. She held it near her head. The charge pulled her hair toward the balloon.","Leave borrowed books on a chair near the shelves.","Leave the soil dry; move the seeds into the watering can.","Leaves and branches","Leaves blocked the drain, so rainwater could not flow away. Water gathered on the path and froze overnight. In the morning, the caretaker closed the slippery path. That walkway was the usual route between the gate and classrooms. The drain normally carried puddles away before they became deep. A mat of wet leaves covered its opening this time. Temperatures fell below freezing after sunset. Children used another entrance while the caretaker dealt with the ice.","Leaves covered the path.","Leena’s first few stitches slipped out because she had not secured the thread. She asked her aunt to show her how to begin with a knot. Leena unpicked the loose section and sewed it again. Before starting her next piece, she checked the thread was secure.","Lee’s thick curtains kept daylight outside the bedroom. The room was still dark after breakfast. Lee opened both curtains wide across the window. Sunlight could now enter, making the whole room bright.","Leila packed a gift for her cousin. She placed a blue scarf in the parcel, then picked up a yellow card. The card stayed on the table because she had not written in it. A red ribbon went around the closed parcel.","Leila''s team had a spare ticket for the school match. At lunch, she heard Anya asking what time it began. Anya then counted the coins in her pocket and went quiet. Leila put the spare ticket beside Anya''s lunch box. She said the team had one ticket they could not use. Anya looked up, smiled and folded it carefully into her pocket. After lunch, they planned where to meet before the match. Leila never mentioned the coins she had noticed.","Lena listened to Mr. Hill at the piano. He played three high notes slowly. Lena copied them on a small keyboard. Then Mr. Hill played five low notes. Lena listened carefully to those notes too.","Lena placed two borrowed books into a return slot. Ahead of her, someone asked a worker where to find books about stars. Rows of labelled shelves filled the room, and a sign asked visitors to keep their voices low. Lena waited with her borrowing card in her hand.","Lena raced Sam to finish their drawings first. She rushed and tore her paper in half. Sam worked slowly and made a neat picture. Lena started again, taking more care with each part.","Lena''s cat was at the vet all day. When the phone rang, Lena suddenly froze. Mom listened, smiled, and gave a thumbs up. Lena let out a long breath and relaxed.","Lena’s toy car could not cross the gap between two boxes. Her first paper bridge sagged. She folded the paper into ridges and tried again. This time the car crossed safely. Lena used what she saw to improve her design.","Leo Sun","Leo had the empty tray; Hana had drinks","Leo painted a red fire truck at school. His wide brush made the ladder look messy. Ms. Chen gave him a thinner brush. Leo made neat silver lines on the ladder. He kept the wide brush for the wheels.","Leo wanted to swim the full length of the pool. He usually stopped halfway because he tried to move too quickly. His coach first helped him practise slow breathing beside the edge. Then they worked on steady kicks over a shorter distance. Leo joined the two skills during each lesson that month. Sometimes he still stopped, but he could see his progress. After six weeks, he touched the far wall without standing up. The coach marked the full length on Leo''s progress sheet.","Lia turned on the tap, but little water reached the hose nozzle. She followed the hose and found it sharply bent beneath a chair leg. She put down the nozzle and reached for the chair, keeping her eyes on the trapped section.","Liam was inside with the front door closed. The window showed who was standing outside. Liam heard a knock. He looked through the window. Then he opened the door for Dad.","Lift the leaf to reveal the print.","Lila patiently explained a new board game to Tomas instead of leaving him out. On a later art day, Lila struggled with folding a tricky paper shape. Tomas sat beside her and showed her the steps slowly, just as she had done with the game.","Lily''s lunchbox had opened when it fell into a muddy puddle. At lunchtime, she sat with an empty tray beside Marco. She glanced towards the busy teacher but did not call. Marco had already peeled his orange, his favourite lunch treat. He quietly put half its pieces onto Lily''s tray. When another child turned around, he carried on discussing their game. Lily ate the pieces and gave him a small smile. Marco smiled back, then asked her about the afternoon match.","Lin counted birds visiting the tree outside her classroom each morning. She recorded the same length of time on every school day. Visits fell after noisy building work began beside the tree. The tree still held berries, and the weather stayed similar. Lin continued counting instead of stopping after the first quiet morning. When the building noise ended, many familiar birds visited again. Her chart showed the drop and return across several weeks. She shared the observations without claiming she knew every bird''s reason.","Lina wanted a sunshine picture for her window. Her blank paper had space for one sun. Lina drew a circle. Then she added sun rays. Last, she coloured the sun yellow.","Listen to the word first. Which two letters complete b__n?","Little Boats","Lower the volume first, then put the headphones on.","Luca kept losing his balance during one part of the dance. He managed the earlier steps, then always put a foot down. His teacher suggested short practice periods instead of one exhausting afternoon. Each day, Luca held the difficult position for a little longer. He marked his progress, including days when he wobbled again. After several weeks, he could hold the pose throughout the music. The rest of the dance had not become easier by itself. He had spent time on the part that needed repeated work.","Lucas helped put the food shopping away. The eggs sat above some heavy cans. He lifted the eggs out with both hands. He wanted to keep them safe from breaking. Then he put the cans on the shelf.","Lunch was finished and the plates were clean.","Luz shared bean seeds with her nearby neighbours. Their gardens soon held strong new shoots. Snails later ate Luz''s own young plants. They brought spare shoots, and her garden grew again.","Mae lent spare mittens to a classmate whose hands were cold. When Mae later broke her arm, the classmate offered to carry her books without being asked. Mae realized that the care she had shown was now coming back in a different way.","Make a painted mark.","Make a small hole in the soil.","Make holes in the stacked pages.","Many jobs keep the swimming pool ready for visitors.","Maps by","Mara began explaining her idea for the model. Her partner interrupted again and moved her pieces aside. Mara pressed her lips together and said firmly, “Please let me finish. I have listened to your idea.” She kept her own plan open on the table.","Mara had rehearsed her song all week. Now she stood behind the curtain, twisting the edge of her sleeve. When her name was called, she took a deep breath and checked the opening words again before stepping forward.","Mara needed a bag for a walk where rain was forecast. One bag had bright beads and colours that matched her coat. Its thin straps strained when she added her lunch and spare clothes. A plain bag had strong straps and a cover that shut tightly. Mara tested both with the same things inside before deciding. She chose the plain one, though she still liked the other''s pattern. Rain fell during the walk, but her clothes stayed dry. She opened the bag at the shelter without needing to replace anything.","Mara put cups out for the class picnic. Twenty children needed drinks. At first, she put out eighteen cups. Her friend saw that two children needed cups. Mara added two more before lunch began.","Mara put on her goggles. Swimmers splashed along marked lanes under a high roof. A coach blew a whistle from the edge. Mara climbed down the ladder into the water.","Marisol got off the bus one stop before her intended destination. Rain began while she checked how far she would need to walk. A small museum nearby offered somewhere dry until the shower passed. She expected to wait near the entrance without looking around. A display of old ship models caught her attention instead. She asked the guide about a tiny boat from her grandparents'' town. The rain stopped, but she stayed until the tour had finished. Next week, she returned deliberately with questions for the same guide.","Mark where the car comes out.","Marta placed her recorder in its blue case before going upstairs. The next morning, she heard uneven notes from her brother''s room. He had been practising finger movements without an instrument yesterday. Marta hurried to school with the blue case in her bag. When music began, she opened it and found only the cloth. Her teacher said no instruments had been collected that morning. At home, her brother stopped practising when she asked about it. His new sheet of recorder music lay on his desk.","Max Pine","May I borrow your book about sharks?","May I borrow your coat today?","May I borrow your eraser, please?","May I borrow your other shoe?","May I borrow your ruler and return it after the lesson?","May I borrow your ruler and return it next week?","May I have a turn on the swing?","May I have ten seeds for the two spaces in my pot?","May I have two pots for all the seeds in your packet?","May I have two seeds for the two spaces in my pot?","May I have your ruler to keep after the lesson?","May I take a photo after we take the model apart?","May I take a photo before we take the model apart?","May I take the model home instead of taking a photo?","May I use your colouring pencils?","May I visit my teacher at home?","May came home from a muddy walk. She left her boots on the porch. Then she went inside in her socks. Dad put a mat beside the boots to catch the drops of water.","May we close the window and leave the door wide open?","May we open both the window and the door as far as possible?","May we open the window slightly and leave the door shut?","Maya planted sunflower seeds in a small pot. She added a label with her name. Then she watered the dry soil. She put the pot on the sunny windowsill. A green shoot appeared there on Friday.","Maya tried to join the same pieces five times. Each time, her small model fell apart. She sighed loudly and pushed the pieces away. Her forehead wrinkled as she stared at the pile.","Maya watched the moon from the same window each evening. She drew its shape beside that day''s date. On cloudy evenings, she marked the box hidden instead. At first, each drawing seemed like a separate little picture. After four weeks, she laid the pages in a row. The bright shape grew wider, then became narrow again. Her cousin could see that pattern without watching every night. Her cousin pointed to matching shapes several pages apart.","Maya''s brother","Meet me at school before lunch to watch ducks.","Meet me at the pond after school to watch ducks.","Meet me at the pond before school to watch ducks.","Mei walked between rows of living plants in pots. Labels explained how much light each plant needed. A worker helped her choose one that suited a sunny window and added a bag of potting soil. Mei paid for it at a counter near stacks of empty flowerpots.","Mended a damaged place by attaching another piece over it.","Mia offered to teach a new club member how chess works. He said he had watched games but never joined this club. Mia opened the box and began looking for the instruction sheet. Before she found it, he placed every piece correctly. He explained which moves would leave Mia''s king in danger. When she moved a bishop wrongly, he described its allowed path. The board stood on a small table beneath the clock. Other players nearby were still choosing partners for their first game.","Mia stopped her brother entering the garden den after they argued. Her collection stayed untouched, and every cushion remained where she put it. However, the pretend shop was dull when she played both customers herself. Her brother missed their game but disliked being blamed for every mess. They agreed that special models needed permission before anyone moved them. Other toys could still be used together and put away afterwards. Mia opened the den again without removing every rule. Two customers soon waited at the shop while the models stayed safe.","Mia swung the bat to hit the ball.","Mika filled a plant pot right to the rim with soil. When she watered it, muddy water spilled over the edge. She removed some soil and left room for the water to soak in. When potting another plant, she remembered to leave the same useful space at the top.","Mika wanted to take the class photograph without accepting any advice. In her first picture, faces disappeared in shadows beside the window. She tried again from the same place and got the same result. A neighbour who enjoyed photography offered to explain the light. Mika finally listened and moved so the window lit the children''s faces. She checked the new picture before asking everyone to smile again. The finished photograph clearly showed each child in the group. Mika wrote the neighbour''s helpful lighting tip beside her own picture notes.","Mika’s simple wooden flute made a weak, rough sound. A small piece of paper was stuck across the opening where air passed. Her teacher carefully removed it. Mika blew in the same way as before, and a clear note sounded. The flute had not been replaced.","Miles and Grandpa raked leaves from the path. The wind kept blowing them across the garden. Grandpa held a large bag open. Miles pushed the loose leaves into it. They tied it shut before more wind came.","Milly carried ice cream on a warm walk. The heat softened it until it started dripping. She licked fast to catch the drops. White drops still ran down onto her fingers.","Milo disliked the dark corner beside his bed at night. Dad listened instead of laughing and left a torch within reach. At first, Milo checked every shadow before settling under his blanket. Dad reminded him that he could call whenever he needed help. After several nights, Milo left the torch beside the pillow unused. He still knew where it was and could reach it easily. One night, Dad found him sleeping with the room completely dark. The torch remained ready on the bedside shelf the next morning.","Milo guessed how much string his model needed and cut it too short. Instead of throwing the model away, he measured each side, joined a fresh length and tried again. On the next model he measured before cutting.","Milo''s tooth had wobbled for a week. He wiggled it at breakfast and at bath time. He laughed at dinner, and the tooth popped out. He caught it in his hand. Milo put it under his pillow that night.","Min gave the fish extra food every day. Uneaten bits made the tank''s clear water cloudy. An adult helped Min measure the right amount. With less waste, the clean water stayed clear again.","Mina Fox","Mina chose a library book.","Mina opened a box from her old friend. Inside was the scarf she had lost. She pressed it to her cheek and smiled. Then she hurried to get a thank-you card. She kept the scarf beside her all evening.","Mina''s kitten often slipped behind furniture without making any noise. Its grey fur was difficult to see in the dark gaps. Once, Mina searched several rooms before finding it behind the sofa. Her mother fitted a light bell to its safe cat collar. Later, Mina heard a jingle beneath the armchair before seeing anything. She knelt beside that chair instead of checking every room. The kitten came out when she called, just as it usually did. Mina left the other furniture alone and fetched its dinner.","Mina’s large jigsaw seemed impossible on the first evening. She sorted the pieces by edges and colours and worked on a little section after school. Some sections took many attempts. Over two weeks the empty spaces shrank, until she placed the final piece and saw the complete picture.","Mira usually watered the class plant. On Friday she was absent, so Ben asked Lila to do the job. Lila filled the jug and watered the dry soil. Ben wiped up the water that spilled on the table.","Mo left his bike outside without shelter all winter. Rain kept wetting the bare metal. The wet metal slowly grew orange rust. By spring, the chain was stiff and rusty.","Mo wanted to remember a long message for assembly. He learned one short part each day. Then he practiced joining the parts together. At assembly, he remembered the whole message.","Mole wanted to join the woodland concert, but his singing was very quiet. The others could not hear him over the drums. Mole tried tapping smooth stones together instead. Their clear clicks fitted the song. At the concert he kept the beat, and the singers followed him.","Mom had promised a picnic beside the lake after breakfast. The basket was ready, and nobody had lost their swimming things. While packing the blanket, she looked towards the hills. Dark clouds covered them, and distant thunder interrupted the birds. She checked the weather map, then put the basket away. The children fetched their shoes while Mom found two umbrellas. She suggested the indoor museum, where their tickets were still valid. They could have their sandwiches at its covered cafe instead.","Most museum objects stood behind glass that visitors could not touch. A visitor who could not see asked about a carved bowl. Spoken descriptions helped, but did not explain every curve clearly. Staff made a strong copy that people could explore by hand. They added copies of several other objects with different shapes. Labels and recordings explained which details matched the originals. Sighted visitors also noticed things while holding the carefully made copies. The original objects remained protected behind their glass display cases.","Move an event to a later time or date.","Move the seeds first, then pour water onto the soil.","Moved up and down repeatedly on the water.","Moving the feeder made it easier for the birds to use.","Mr Okafor needed to paint the patch above his front window. His ladder reached it easily from the place he first chose. However, one ladder foot rested on a loose, uneven stone. When he pressed the lowest rung, the ladder shifted sideways. He moved it, checked both feet, and tested the rung again. This time, nothing moved beneath his weight. He asked his helper to hold the bottom before climbing. The paint pot stayed beside the same window throughout these checks.","Mr Price could not move his wheelbarrow from mud. The children fetched an adult to help him. Together, they pushed it onto firm ground. He thanked them with apples from his orchard.","Mr. Pole could not find the race whistle. He checked both pockets in his coat. Sana had a spare whistle on her bag. She lent it to him for the race. He blew it and the children started running.","Mud covered my hat.","Mum handed me a wrapped box.","Mum made a simple bird feeder for our garden. She spread soft seed butter on a cone. Next, she rolled the sticky cone in seeds. Finally, she tied it to a tree branch. We watched from our window for visiting birds.","My bag was a present last year.","My bicycle has a black basket on it.","My bicycle has a white basket on it.","My boat has a red hull, one white sail and a flag.","My boat has a red hull, two white sails and no flag.","My boat has a white hull, two red sails and no flag.","My coat has a very long zip.","My coat is green, like the other coat beside it.","My cousin won the game yesterday.","My cousins are staying with us.","My cousins left before I saw them.","My crayons are in a round tin.","My crayons are in a square box.","My first tooth fell out today.","My friend lost a tooth last week.","My glove has a torn thumb.","My green bag has two pockets.","My green coat has a black pocket and torn buttons.","My green coat has black buttons and a torn pocket.","My kite flew high this morning.","My lost bag has a fish-shaped tag.","My lost key has a fish-shaped tag.","My lost key has a star-shaped tag.","My lunch is in a round tin.","My new boots were red.","My painting session was over and everything was put away.","My pet fish, Dot, has bright colours.","My plant needs water every day.","My purple bag has no pockets.","My purple bag has two pockets.","My scarf covered my chin.","My school bag has useful parts.","My scooter has a white basket on it.","Nadia thought the tide pool was empty when she first arrived. Her moving shadow passed over the shallow water as she leaned. A guide asked her to sit still beside one dry rock. After a while, a tiny crab came out from hiding. Small arms opened on a sea creature fixed to a stone. Nadia noticed movement she had missed during her quick first look. She left every creature where it was and drew pictures. Her picture now showed creatures around the once empty-looking rock.","Nadia took photographs for the class newsletter. The chess club held its board in their photo. The garden team held fresh carrots in theirs. The teacher chose the picture with the carrots. Nadia placed that picture on the front page. Pictures of the same groups working were saved for another page. One showed a chess game halfway through. Another showed hands planting seeds in a narrow bed. The front picture needed people holding things they had used or grown.","Nan had searched the kitchen twice for her ring. She kept looking at her bare finger. When it appeared in the bottom of a shopping bag, she let out a long breath and smiled. She put it on and finally sat down with her untouched cup of tea.","Near the fish.","Ned rushed to carry three cups at once. One tipped and spilled water on the floor. He fetched a cloth and wiped it up. Then he carried the cups one at a time. Everyone reached the table with a full drink.","Neighbours collected twelve towels. Six clean towels were packed for the animal shelter. Four torn towels became cleaning cloths in the workshop. Two damp towels were hung up to dry before anyone decided where to send them. A volunteer drove the packed towels to the shelter.","Neighbours planned a surprise for Chen''s hundredth birthday. The cafe offered a cake, but one shop could not organise everything. Children painted a long banner while adults hung flags safely. A musician practised the song Chen loved as a boy. People who could not attend wrote messages for a shared book. When Chen stepped outside, the street began singing together. He read the messages while neighbours passed slices of cake. He kept the shared book beside his chair after everyone left.","Nell had finished decorating her cake before the contest opened. Zeke arrived with his own cake, then dropped its box. The pieces were safe inside, but his careful design had collapsed. Nell''s name was called while she helped him collect clean cups. They layered the cake pieces with fruit into small desserts. By the time they finished, Nell''s contest turn had passed. She received no ribbon, though her untouched cake was still ready. She stayed beside Zeke as visitors enjoyed the desserts they shared.","Nell had just paid for apples when her basket handle broke. She caught the basket against her knee, but two apples rolled onto the ground. The seller brought a strong bag for the fruit. A dog was sleeping beside the stall throughout the accident.","Nell wanted everyone to see her puppet show. The low stage hid the puppets from some children. She tried a taller box behind the curtain. Now the children at the back could see too. Nell began the show again for everyone.","Nell''s baby brother had just fallen asleep. Her music box suddenly played loudly. Mom pointed at the baby and raised one finger. Nell reached into her pocket for the box.","New books arrived at the library in boxes beside the lift. Before the reading event, the librarian put them on a low table near the window. Children looked through them there. At closing time, helpers moved them to a shelf by the door, ready for borrowing the next day.","New pupils came to school after the holiday ended.","Nia dropped her last coin under a table. A boy crawled underneath and found it. Nia bought two small apples with the coin. She gave one to the boy to thank him.","Nia kept answering before her partner finished speaking, and they built the wrong shape. She apologized and listened to the whole plan. Together they rebuilt it. During the next task she waited until her partner had finished.","Nia was about to join a running game when she felt a lace brush her ankle. She looked down and saw that one shoe was untied. She stepped out of the players’ way and bent towards the shoe, keeping its loose lace in her hand.","Night Sounds","Niko interrupted whenever someone shared an idea. Soon, his group stopped trying to speak at all. He began waiting and asking what others thought. Their next plan included useful ideas from every member.","Nina rode her bike along the park path. Its loose chain slipped as she pedalled. She walked the bike to the repair bench. Her dad tightened its chain with a tool. Then she rode along the path again.","Nita used twenty red blocks for her tower. Ten blue blocks formed the middle. Four yellow blocks balanced at the top. The tower stood for one whole minute. Then the cat came to look at it.","No one had reached the end yet.","No one watered the plant during school break. The break lasted two hot weeks. Its soil became hard and dry. Without water, the leaves began to droop. Children saw the bent leaves when they returned.","No shoe escaped the puppy''s chewing.","No tickets were left by lunch.","Noah noticed that a new classmate had no pencil, so he quietly offered a spare. A month later, Noah missed several lessons. The classmate made clear notes about the shared project and saved them for him. Noah found it much easier to join in when he returned.","Nobody used the muddy corner of the school garden. Water gathered there, and wrappers caught against its broken fence. A class first cleared the rubbish and repaired the fence. They mixed compost into the ground so water drained better. Then they planted sunflowers where children could watch them grow. By summer, yellow flowers stood above the old muddy patch. The class added a bench for anyone passing through. People now stopped there instead of hurrying past the mess.","Noor built the walls of the model house. Eli made the roof, but one corner came loose. Tessa held the walls steady while Noor glued the roof back together. Eli painted the door after the glue dried.","Noor opened the parcel.","Noor showed her finished birthday cake to her little sister. The cherries on its top looked bright against the white icing. Just before showing it, Noor had placed those cherries carefully. The icing beneath them had gone on earlier. Under that soft layer was a sponge she had baked first. The cake could not have icing while still raw batter. Each part needed the part beneath it to be ready. Her sister saw only the finished cake, not its earlier stages. The sponge formed the base beneath both kinds of decoration.","Nora decorated a box for her brother’s long model train. At the end she discovered the train would not fit. She made a longer box and measured before decorating it. When asked to wrap a different gift later, she checked its size before choosing the wrapping.","Nora looked after the class calendar. She crossed off Monday after the class meeting. Their next trip would be on Friday. Nora drew a circle around that day. Her friends counted the days until the trip.","Now we had both shoes and could go out.","Octopus. Listen to the first sound. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Ola could reach only one high monkey bar. She tried again during each school break. After weeks, she could reach halfway along the row. By summer, she could cross from end to end.","Ola filled the bird feeder with seeds at breakfast. At lunchtime, half the seeds had gone. After school, she found only dust in the feeder. She had not added more seeds during the day. A pigeon rested on the nearby fence. At each check, she looked through the clear sides. She wrote full, half full, or empty in her notebook. The lunch entry was different from both other entries. Her teacher asked for the two checks showing the whole change. No check was made the next morning.","Old paper does not have to be trash. Trucks take it to a special factory. Water and paper are mashed into soft pulp. The pulp is rolled flat and dried. The new paper is ready to use again.","Omar Page","Omar forgot the lid on the popcorn pot. The hot corn started to pop and jump. With no lid, pieces flew out. They landed on the counter and kitchen floor.","Omar grew impatient while waiting for the bus.","Omar leaned back in a wide chair. A bright lamp shone towards his mouth. The person beside him wore gloves and asked him to open wide while she checked each tooth with a small mirror. Omar’s coat hung by the door, and a cup stood near the chair.","Omar planted several kinds of seeds without labels. Later he could not remember which tiny shoots were which. He compared them with pictures and sorted out the names with his teacher. The next time he planted seeds, he wrote and placed labels before covering the pots.","Omar wanted a simple telescope. Instead of spending every small amount he received, he put some into a labelled tin each week. The amount grew slowly. Months later, he counted enough to buy the telescope and felt pleased that he had kept to his plan.","Omar wanted a tower taller than his knee. The flat carpet was clear of other toys. Omar set down his blocks. He stacked them into a tower. Then he smiled at the tall tower.","Omar''s class built a model town from cardboard. He wanted his tower in the centre because it was tallest. The group explained that it hid the smaller buildings behind. Omar moved it to the edge and looked from different sides. Now visitors could see every part of the town clearly. At the exhibition, a younger child admired both tower and houses. Omar felt proud that his change had helped the whole display. He showed the visitor how the group had arranged it together.","On a long train ride, Mina grew bored with her puzzle. Her grandfather suggested drawing things outside. Mina drew a crooked tree, a striped tent and a river bend. She joined the pictures into a map. When they arrived, she used the map to tell her cousin about the journey.","One neighbour kept all the seeds for a single box.","One piece was missing from Ash''s nearly finished puzzle. He looked under the sofa and behind the chair. Neither place held the small blue piece. He kept searching and found it beneath the rug.","One red sock is falling from the hanging clothesline.","One tree can provide many useful places for wildlife. Birds may build nests among its branches. Beetles can hide under loose bark. A hollow trunk may shelter a small animal, while fallen leaves protect creatures near the ground. Different parts of the same tree support different lives.","Open the battery cover.","Otter delivered letters along the river. One morning the addresses washed off his bag of envelopes. He visited the animals and asked who was expecting news. With their help he matched every letter to its owner. Afterward, he made a waterproof cover for his delivery bag.","Otto was small enough for the barber''s special high chair. First, the barber fastened a gown around his shoulders. Its smooth cloth would keep loose hair off his shirt. Then the barber cut Otto''s hair with scissors. The hand mirror beside the chair could show the back. After the haircut, the barber held up that mirror. Otto could see the short, even shape all around. Last, the barber offered him a wrapped lollipop. The treat was for after the check, not during cutting.","Our bean pods grew long and green.","Our bell helps us know the times of the school day.","Our bus arrived.","Our class cut down a tree today.","Our class finds ways to waste less paper.","Our class has a new fish.","Our class learned the names of the new pupils.","Our class made a feeder for the birds.","Our class made a timeline about the school garden. The beds had been dug before the winter holidays began. Seeds were planted when the class returned in spring. Several weeks later, small shoots rose through the soil. The gardener fitted supports before the climbing plants grew tall. By summer, the children could pick beans from the vines. They cooked some and saved others for next year''s seeds. Their timeline included drawings from each visit to the garden.","Our class made the cloakroom easier to use. We drew a picture above each child''s hook. We put a low box under the scarves. Wet boots went on a tray by the door. Everyone could find their things quickly.","Our class makes labels from small pieces of paper.","Our class planned a small market. We wrote signs, counted the things for sale, and decided fair prices. During the market, we took turns helping visitors and recording money. Afterwards, everyone checked the totals together.","Our class planted a tree today.","Our class took steps to help new pupils feel welcome.","Our class wanted a quieter place to read. We moved a shelf away from the door and put cushions behind it. We agreed to speak softly there and return books neatly. After trying the space for a week, we added a lamp so the pages were easier to see.","Our class wants to get a fish.","Our class wrote rules for visiting the school pond. Everyone must stay on the path beside the water. Quiet voices help us hear the birds and frogs. We may sketch a creature without picking it up. An adult carries the pond net and sampling tray. After observing creatures, the adult returns them to the water. The rules also say to wash hands before eating. Each pupil reads the rules with a partner before leaving.","Our classroom labels are printed on white paper.","Our club collected food for a community cupboard. We checked which unopened foods were needed before bringing donations. At the hall, we sorted tins, rice and other packets. We checked the dates and packed boxes so the cupboard volunteers could find each kind easily.","Our day at the beach was over.","Our family planned a walk to the park. We chose a route with safe crossings and checked how long it would take. We packed water and a map. During the walk, a closed path meant we used the map to choose a different way and still reached the park.","Our first design held ten coins without bending.","Our first roof kept the water out without any repairs.","Our footprints crossed the sand.","Our library had a box for borrowed books. It stood just inside the main door. Tia put her finished book in that box. Then she chose another from the animal shelf. She carried it to the desk.","Our missing rabbit has brown ears and one white paw.","Our missing rabbit has brown ears like the other rabbit.","Our missing rabbit has white ears and one brown paw.","Our paper roof sagged under the first coin.","Our plants are near the back door.","Our puddle froze in the cold.","Our roof protects us from weather.","Our rubbish stayed beside the picnic blanket.","Our sandwiches were still waiting on the plates.","Our seats are in the back row beside the left aisle.","Our seats are in the front row beside the left aisle.","Our seats are somewhere beside an aisle.","Our seed trays stood on two shelves in the classroom. The top shelf was sunny but sometimes too dry. Plants there wilted whenever we forgot to water them. The lower shelf stayed damp but received very little light. Its seedlings grew long, pale stems reaching towards the window. We moved both trays where they could receive light. A rota reminded us to check the soil every morning. Extra water alone would not have solved both problems.","Our shed holds all our garden tools.","Our sheets of paper come in different colours.","Our swimming pool has several useful areas.","Our teacher has a pet bird at home.","Our town has a small garden beside the bus station. Herbs grow in old sinks that would otherwise be thrown away. A rain barrel catches water from the roof of the shelter. Fallen leaves become compost in a corner bin. Volunteers use the compost to feed next year''s plants. Wooden pallets form seats where people can rest and talk. Labels explain what each reused object used to be. The garden shows how unwanted materials can become useful again.","Our trip is now on Tuesday. We will visit a new place.","Our trip is now on Tuesday. We will visit the same place.","Our trip is still on Monday. We will visit a new place.","Our walk took us past a field with a wooden fence.","Our wet clothes dried by the heater.","Owen needed a book for his weather project. He searched the science shelf without finding one. A helper showed him books about storms. Owen chose the one with lightning photographs. He took it to the reading table.","Owen stirred the soup and added carrots. After it cooked, he tasted a spoonful. It needed more flavour, so he added a little salt. He put the pepper back without using it, then served the soup.","Owls hunt mice.","Paint the picture on the paper.","Paper bridges always break when coins are added.","Paper can be made into large sheets or small cards.","People in our building began sharing garden tools. They listed what each person could lend and set a return day. A notebook recorded who had each tool. When a handle broke, the borrower reported it, and the group arranged a repair before anyone used it again.","People read quietly at the long wooden tables. A worker scanned the books Mira had chosen. Mira could borrow them for two whole weeks. She left without paying and planned to return them.","Pia and Jules carried different boxes into the hall. Pia’s box was lighter, but Jules’s was wider. The wide box contained the folding display stand. The smaller box held paper maps for the visitors. The other box was awkward because it stretched past both handles. Each child carried just one box all the way. The helper needed the stand before she could hang the maps. She asked for the wider box, rather than the lighter load.","Pia saw her new neighbour struggling with several bags and held the door for him. She did not expect anything back. Days later, Pia’s wheelbarrow tipped in the garden. The neighbour came over to help collect the soil. They began stopping to help each other with small jobs.","Pia usually rode her scooter down Hill Lane after school. Today, loose stones covered the steep section below the bend. A bicycle ahead skidded there before its rider stopped safely. Pia tested her scooter''s brake on the flat pavement. It worked, and both wheels turned freely without making a sound. Even so, she got off before reaching the steep part. She walked down slowly, keeping one hand on the brake. On the clear, level road below, she began riding again.","Pia''s shoes fitted at the start of summer. Her feet grew during the following weeks. Now her toes pressed against the shoe ends. The tight shoes made her feet ache.","Picture for ant","Picture for feather","Picture for frog","Picture for gate","Picture for key","Picture for octopus","Picture for seal","Picture for spoon","Picture for star","Pip and Dad were preparing bread in the kitchen before breakfast. Dad left briefly to bring a clean tray from the cupboard. When he returned, the open flour bag leaned against Pip''s stool. A white pile lay below it, with small footprints leading away. Pip''s socks had the same powder pressed into their soles. His hands were sticky, and half-mixed dough waited in his bowl. The paint box was closed on a high shelf in another room. Pip looked down at the prints and fetched the brush.","Pip could not find his new library card. He looked inside his coat and lunch bag. He checked the pocket of yesterday''s trousers. There it was, beside a small pebble. Now he could borrow the book he wanted.","Pip''s seed stayed hidden while other seeds sprouted. Pip checked the soil and watered when needed. At last, a small shoot appeared. With steady care, it grew into a tall sunflower.","Please add a ramp so I can wheel my trolley inside.","Please bring one of the metal things.","Please bring one of the things in the bathroom.","Please bring some maps and show us how to read them.","Please bring something that opens a lock.","Please bring something to dry with.","Please bring the key with the round red tag.","Please bring the seedlings in before they start to grow.","Please buy new shoes before entering.","Please buy some plants for our garden.","Please carry this box outside.","Please close the gate and lock it behind you.","Please close the gate, but leave it unlocked.","Please come to our class show on Friday.","Please could you open the door for me?","Please could you say that again?","Please do not say anything else.","Please explain the instructions so I can do the answers myself.","Please feed the cat tomorrow and keep it indoors.","Please feed the cat tonight and keep it indoors.","Please feed the cat tonight and let it outside.","Please fill these bottles with water.","Please find any book with a cover and several pages.","Please find my glove and bring it back to me.","Please find something from the bookshelf.","Please finish the sheet while I put away my pencils.","Please give me all your cards.","Please give my kite back to me.","Please give my place to someone else while I fetch my coat.","Please help me find my blue socks.","Please help me learn my lines before the school play.","Please help me look for my lost dog.","Please help me mend this puppet.","Please help me open this jar.","Please help me reach that book.","Please help me tie my shoelace.","Please help me tie this lace.","Please join our picnic at home on Sunday.","Please join our picnic in the park on Saturday.","Please join our picnic in the park on Sunday.","Please join our picnic!","Please keep my place while I go back for my coat.","Please keep this gate shut.","Please keep to the path.","Please keep your shoes on the shelf.","Please leave muddy shoes on your feet.","Please leave my bag behind the door.","Please leave the gate open for the rabbit.","Please leave the line now.","Please leave the line with me while I look for my coat.","Please leave the path and cross the soil.","Please leave the tap running.","Please lend me a book from home.","Please lend me your bike. I will bring it back tonight.","Please line up here.","Please look closely at the pots without touching them.","Please make lunch, cooks, and please serve it, helpers.","Please move to a different seat.","Please paint the dry seats before sitting on the bench.","Please pass any blue cup from the table.","Please pass something from near the door.","Please pass that thing from over there.","Please pass the blue cup with a white star.","Please pass the blue cup with a white stripe inside its rim.","Please pass the blue cup with a white stripe on its handle.","Please pass the tall green cup beside the sink.","Please pass the yellow scarf beside the door.","Please pick the plants by the path.","Please pick up the pots so you can look closely.","Please place finished books on this trolley.","Please put empty bottles in this box.","Please put my book on another shelf.","Please put my shoes in the box.","Please put that book on a higher shelf.","Please put your bags here.","Please put your coats on the trolley.","Please remove all the handles from our cupboard.","Please share your book; I want to read the tale about the moon.","Please speak quietly during the recording; you can talk afterwards.","Please stay completely silent for the rest of the day.","Please take off muddy shoes here.","Please take out our cupboard and buy a new one.","Please take this trolley home.","Please take your bag when you leave.","Please talk loudly during the recording and be quiet afterwards.","Please tell me every answer so I can copy them down.","Please tell me where you walked.","Please tell me which book you read.","Please tell us your favourite song.","Please tighten this loose handle on our cupboard.","Please turn off the tap after use.","Please untie both of your laces.","Please use the dry seats; this bench has wet paint.","Please use this bench; the seats beside it have wet paint.","Please wait for the bus over there.","Please wait while our teacher counts us before we leave.","Please wake the baby now.","Please walk past the pots without looking at them.","Please wash the outside of the tap.","Please water my plant tomorrow.","Please water the seeds I already planted.","Please wipe your feet before coming in.","Post the sealed envelope.","Posy sang softly in the quiet school library. A boy kept losing his place while reading. Posy noticed him cover both of his ears. She stopped singing, and he began reading comfortably again.","Pour the collected juice into a cup.","Pour the juice into a cup.","Preheat","Preparing lunch for a school takes more than cooking. Ingredients must arrive and be stored safely. Workers prepare enough portions and keep equipment clean. Before serving, they check which meals belong to children with different food needs. Careful preparation connects all these jobs.","Press the painted leaf onto paper.","Press the photograph onto the card.","Priti watched a storm through the window with her grandmother. At each thunder crash, she stepped farther back into the room. Soon she asked Grandma to sit between her and the glass. She held Grandma''s hand until the flashes and crashes stopped. Then she loosened her grip and returned to the window. Her book remained open on the sofa throughout the storm. Grandma had finished cooking before joining her in the sitting room. Outside, the rain continued quietly after the last sound of thunder.","Priw chose the easiest jobs during the class woodwork project. He carried boards while Tup and Lin practised making strong joins. At first, he enjoyed finishing quickly while they corrected their mistakes. Later, each child needed to build a small frame alone. Priw could measure wood but did not know how to join it. Tup and Lin remembered the problems they had already worked through. Priw asked to practise beside them before starting his frame again. This time, he chose to try the part he had avoided.","Priya carried her rabbit in a box with air holes. In the waiting room, a dog sat beside its owner. A worker called the rabbit’s name and led Priya to a room with scales and an examination table. The rabbit’s health card lay open beside the scales.","Priya found tomorrow''s answer sheet beside the school copier. The heading was visible, and nobody else stood in the room. She knew reading it might give her a higher quiz score. Instead, she turned it over and returned it to the teacher. During the quiz, she answered only what she understood herself. Her seven points did not earn the highest score in class. A friend offered sympathy, but Priya smiled at her own work. She marked two difficult questions to study before the next quiz.","Priya mixed a green she loved but forgot how she had made it. When the paint ran out, she could not match the colour easily. She tested small amounts and wrote down the mixture that worked. Later she used her notes to make the same green again.","Published by","Pull up the zip of your coat.","Pulled tight rather than hanging loosely.","Push pegs into the ground.","Put another slice of bread on top.","Put the card into an envelope.","Put the headphones on first, then turn the volume up.","Put the letter inside the envelope.","Put the new battery inside.","Put the seed in the hole.","Put your arms into the coat.","Put your shoes on your feet.","Put your socks on your feet.","Quick and full of purposeful energy.","Rae handed the map to the older children before their woodland walk. She gave the youngest child only the empty snack bag to carry. At a fork, the older walkers disagreed because both paths looked similar. The youngest child pointed out a small carved leaf on one post. She remembered the same leaf beside the correct route on the map. The group followed her clue and soon found the marked bridge. At the next turning, Rae held the map where everyone could see. She asked the youngest child what she noticed first.","Rafi and his partner were close to finishing the treasure hunt. They had solved the last clue before the nearest competing team. Then his partner tripped and could not comfortably stand on her ankle. Rafi could see the final marker a short distance away. He left it untouched, called an adult and stayed beside her. Another team reached the marker while they waited for help. Rafi later collected their unfinished clue sheet without asking for a prize. He walked beside his partner when she was ready to leave safely.","Rafi performed his part in the finished school play. The performance had come after several weeks of rehearsals. Those practices were possible once the teacher had chosen the cast. Rafi''s audition had happened before that choice of actors. During the audition, every child had a chance to read. The teacher needed to know who suited each role. The rehearsal weeks were for learning the chosen roles together. The audience saw the finished performance, not that earlier work. Four stages led from trying out to the final play.","Rain continued all morning, and wet shoes left marks in the entrance hall. Before lunch, the caretaker put a large absorbent mat inside the door. People wiped their shoes on it. After lunch there were fewer wet marks, although rain still fell outside.","Rain falling on a roof can flow through gutters into a tank. A cover keeps leaves out of the stored water. A tap near the bottom lets a gardener fill a watering can. The tank links collecting, protecting and using rainwater in one simple system.","Rain fell all night.","Rain fell, but the food stayed dry.","Rain flooded the field, so the game moved to the yard. The yard''s hard ground made the balls bounce very high. Those high bounces carried balls over the fence. Seven balls landed among Mr. Njoku''s tomato plants that afternoon. The school used soft grass for games on dry days. Balls dropped there usually stayed close to the ground. In the yard, the same balls sprang up much farther. The fence had not changed, and the children used ordinary throws.","Rain kept our class indoors at break. We first crowded around one box of blocks. To make room for everyone, we set up a drawing table, a puzzle area and a building space. Children chose an activity and changed places when they were ready, without pushing.","Rain made a puddle by the school door. Someone mopped it before the bell each week. Early one morning, Priya saw who helped. Bill, the bus driver, was holding the mop. She waved to him through the window.","Rain usually ran down the clear drain. This time wet leaves covered the opening. Water collected on the path until a caretaker removed the leaves. Then the water flowed away again.","Rainwater washed a worm onto the busy school path. A shoe nearly stepped on it. Lina used a leaf to move it away. This kept it safe from passing feet.","Raj''s torch grew dim and then went out. Pressing its switch again did not help. Spare batteries were kept in the kitchen drawer. Raj walked there and pulled the drawer open carefully.","Ramps give people another way past steps. Wide doors give wheelchairs room to move through. Clear paths help people travel around safely. Low shelves put useful things within easy reach. These changes let more people use a building.","Ran towards the shelter very quickly.","Rana planted three bean seeds in a paper cup. She watered them every morning before school. For days, nothing happened. Then one green stem pushed up through the soil. Rana cheered so loudly that her dog barked.","Ravi can find his way there easily.","Ravi put his bowl on a thin paper mat. Some soup spilled over the side. The paper became soggy and tore when lifted. Ravi used a cloth to wipe the table. Next time, he chose a washable mat.","Ravi put his towel on a bench and pulled goggles over his eyes. A rope divided the water into lanes. His instructor waited beside a ladder and reminded him to breathe out under the water. A lifeguard watched the people from a tall chair.","Ravi stepped off the low balance beam many times. With the instructor nearby, he practised looking ahead and moving slowly. He returned to the safe low beam at each lesson. Eventually he could cross its length without stepping off and was ready to try the next challenge.","Ravi would not sit beside the new boy. Then Ravi''s bag broke and his books fell out. The new boy helped gather and carry them. Next day, Ravi chose to sit beside his helper.","Ray watched a younger child struggle with a large picture puzzle. She had pushed several pieces together although their edges did not match. Ray nearly took over so the table could be cleared quickly. Instead, he showed her how to find the straight edge pieces. He joined one corner while she watched, then moved his hands away. The child tried another corner and began building the outer frame. When she asked, Ray gave a hint without choosing the next piece. She completed the middle herself and called her family over to look.","Ren''s sister got a robot for her birthday. It was the very one Ren had wanted. Ren clapped slowly while everyone else cheered loudly. He stared at her new robot and sighed.","Repairing the join made our model roof keep the water out.","Return borrowed books straight to any empty shelf.","Return borrowed books to the desk for checking.","Rio''s shoe was safe on the pier after the rescue. The lifeguard had lifted it from the water with a pole. Before lifting it, she had fetched that pole from her hut. Rio''s call for help had sent her to the hut. He had called because his shoe had fallen into the water. The pole was long enough to reach below the pier. Rio stayed well back from the edge throughout the rescue. No one needed to enter the water to retrieve the shoe.","Robin wanted the class fund spent on new goal nets. Most classmates voted for a party, so that plan went ahead. Robin felt disappointed but helped serve food and joined the games. Afterwards, he checked the old nets and photographed their torn places. At the next meeting, he explained why they still needed replacing. He waited while others asked questions instead of interrupting their answers. Classmates listened carefully and agreed to save for repairs next. Robin helped list costs beside children who had voted differently before.","Roll the car through the finished tunnel.","Roots do more than hold a plant in place. They take in water from the soil. Some roots also store food for later growth. Their jobs happen underground, so we may not notice how much the plant depends on them.","Rosa brought her beach ball to the picnic. Wind pushed it toward the pond. Her cousin caught it before it reached water. Rosa thanked him for saving the ball. She put it under their picnic blanket.","Rosa built a sandcastle near the calm blue sea. Later, the tide rose up the beach. Waves washed over the castle walls. Water flattened them into a smooth little hill.","Rosa chose a loaf from a warm shelf. Trays of rolls stood behind the glass. A worker wore flour on her white apron. She wrapped Rosa''s loaf in a paper bag. Rosa paid and carried it home for lunch.","Rosa wanted to mend her jacket. She cut a star from blue cloth and a circle from red cloth. The circle covered the hole in her sleeve, so she sewed it there. She kept the star for a bag. A yellow button was already on the jacket.","Rowing home was hard that afternoon. The tide moved against the little boat. Wind from the shore pushed against it too. Both rowers had tired arms after a long swim. They rested before trying the last part of the trip. Each pull moved the oars through water pushing the other way. The breeze acted on the boat above the water as well. Their muscles had already worked hard before the return journey. Together, these difficulties explain why their progress was so slow.","Rows of seats faced a wide red curtain. Ben found the number printed on his ticket. A bell rang, and people stopped chatting. The room grew dark as the curtain rose. Someone stepped forward beneath a bright lamp.","Rows of tomato plants grew under a glass roof. It felt warm, though frost covered the ground outside. A gardener opened a vent in the roof. The plants stayed sheltered behind the clear walls.","Rui stayed awake late reading his comic. His phone battery ran out, so its alarm did not ring. Thick curtains also kept his room dark after sunrise. Those things helped him sleep past his usual waking time. Normally, daylight through the thin summer curtains helped wake him. These heavier curtains were new for the colder months. His charger was still in his school bag overnight. With both usual wake-up signals missing, his late night mattered even more.","Ruth cleared snow from her elderly neighbour’s path. She did it again after the next storm without asking for anything. When Ruth’s bike broke, the neighbour showed her how to mend it. They began helping each other often.","Safa found that others could not read the labels she wrote. She practised a few letter shapes slowly each day, paying attention to spaces. Her first changes were small. By the time the class made an exhibition, visitors could read her labels without asking her to explain them.","Sali took the wrong bag home from school. She found a name tag different from hers. She called the owner and arranged a swap. Next day, she checked her bag before leaving.","Sam and Jo wanted to share a small desk. Their papers kept mixing while they worked. They made two trays and cleared a space between. Each put loose papers into their own tray. Now both had room to finish their drawings.","Sam was ready to clean his teeth. The brush was beside the bathroom sink. Sam put toothpaste on his brush. He brushed his teeth. Then he spat out the toothpaste.","Sam''s pencil","Sami waited behind the curtain with his recorder. All the seats were full of waiting families. He wiped his damp hands on his shirt. He checked his music for the third time.","Sana usually watched the stars alone through her new telescope. She worried that waiting for neighbours would reduce her own viewing time. During comet week, a child nearby asked for a quick look. Sana agreed, then invited the child''s family to take turns. One person noticed a pale tail she had missed herself. Another shared a drawing showing where the comet had moved. Sana spent less time at the eyepiece but talked excitedly for longer. She left an invitation beside the telescope for the following evening.","Saturday is market day. Grandma gives Ade the shopping list. He finds the oranges, and Grandma picks the fish. The vendor always adds one free plum for Ade. They carry their food home for dinner.","Seal the envelope closed.","Seal the finished card inside the envelope.","Serve","Several children had colds when they came to school. Rain kept the class crowded indoors all week. The windows stayed shut, so little fresh air came inside. Germs could spread easily in the crowded room. By Friday, more children had colds. The room had enough seats but little space between groups. Children shared the same indoor air throughout each wet day. An open window would have helped replace that air. These conditions helped germs pass between children sharing the room.","Several walkers arrived late because a fallen tree blocked the path. Heavy rain also slowed their progress. Their map was correct, and everyone had left on time. The group needed to go around the branches through thick grass. The wet ground also made each step slower and more careful. Landmarks still matched the path shown on their map. Their watches agreed with the planned starting time. The delay happened along the route, rather than before they set off.","Shadows are not the same all day. Morning shadows are long when the sun is low. At midday the sun is high. Shadows become shorter. Evening shadows stretch out the other way.","Shaky because its parts were not firmly held together.","She carried it overhead.","She carried the ring in a small cloth pouch.","She climbed under it.","She held the gate.","She jumped over it.","She poured slowly.","She pulled with her hands.","She pushed it uphill.","She pushed with her foot.","She read the book at home.","She returned it to the library.","She rode it uphill.","She scooped with the cup.","She searched the grass for the ring lost from her finger.","She splashed with her fingers.","She squeezed the lemons.","She squeezed through it.","She tiptoed past the dog.","She twisted the silver ring around her finger.","She used her elbow.","Shy when speaking to unfamiliar people.","Slide beads onto the cut string.","Small brown dog with one white ear.","Smoke rose from the toaster in the kitchen. Dad sniffed and put down his newspaper. The toast inside had turned black at the edges. Dad hurried over and reached for the stop button.","Snow fell so deeply that roads became unsafe. The school could not open for lessons. Amini heard its name on the radio. Her school was listed among the closed schools.","Sofia played her piano song before the school concert. She began well and played the middle without stopping. She kept missing only the final note. Her teacher asked her to practice the ending slowly. Three careful tries helped her play that note correctly. The song had a loud opening and a quieter middle. Neither section needed more work that day. The ending had just one sound after a short pause. Sofia marked that place on her music with a circle. At the concert, she remembered to slow down there.","Sol saw a younger child drop her crayons. He helped find every colour under the table. Later, Sol could not reach the glue shelf. The same child fetched the teacher to help. Sol thanked her, and they finished their pictures together.","Some birds leave when winter comes.","Some leaves had smooth edges and others had pointed edges.","Some tools have wooden handles and others have plastic ones.","Some visitors arrive at the centre before lunchtime.","Some warm bread.","Speak in a low voice with unclear words.","Speaking the same cue more quietly solved the problem.","Spread glue on the back of the chosen photograph.","Spread paint on the leaf.","Squeeze juice from the orange.","Squeeze the halves over a bowl.","Stars shone above us.","Stiff and difficult to bend into another shape.","Story and pictures by","Straw covered the floor beside a wide wooden door. A horse stood in its stall beside Tia. She brushed its mane with long, gentle strokes. Fresh hay hung in a net nearby.","Strong wind tugged at the tent all night. Its pegs pulled loose from the soft ground. Without those anchors, one side of the tent fell. The other side still had firm pegs.","Substitutes a for the medial short e spelling in bench.","Substitutes a for the medial short e spelling in hen.","Substitutes a for the medial short e spelling in melt.","Substitutes a for the medial short e spelling in peg.","Substitutes a for the medial short e spelling in pet.","Substitutes a for the medial short e spelling in step.","Substitutes a for the medial short e spelling in tent.","Substitutes a for the medial short i spelling in click.","Substitutes a for the medial short i spelling in fin.","Substitutes a for the medial short i spelling in kit.","Substitutes a for the medial short i spelling in lid.","Substitutes a for the medial short i spelling in list.","Substitutes a for the medial short i spelling in milk.","Substitutes a for the medial short i spelling in mist.","Substitutes a for the medial short i spelling in slip.","Substitutes a for the medial short o spelling in drop.","Substitutes a for the medial short o spelling in fox.","Substitutes a for the medial short o spelling in hop.","Substitutes a for the medial short o spelling in log.","Substitutes a for the medial short o spelling in plot.","Substitutes a for the medial short o spelling in pond.","Substitutes a for the medial short o spelling in rod.","Substitutes a for the medial short o spelling in stop.","Substitutes a for the medial short o spelling in top.","Substitutes a for the medial short u spelling in clump.","Substitutes a for the medial short u spelling in crust.","Substitutes a for the medial short u spelling in cub.","Substitutes a for the medial short u spelling in cut.","Substitutes a for the medial short u spelling in jump.","Substitutes a for the medial short u spelling in lump.","Substitutes a for the medial short u spelling in rug.","Substitutes a for the medial short u spelling in truck.","Substitutes a for the medial short u spelling in tub.","Substitutes e for the medial short a spelling in band.","Substitutes e for the medial short a spelling in camp.","Substitutes e for the medial short a spelling in cap.","Substitutes e for the medial short a spelling in crab.","Substitutes e for the medial short a spelling in flap.","Substitutes e for the medial short a spelling in jam.","Substitutes e for the medial short a spelling in rat.","Substitutes e for the medial short a spelling in sand.","Substitutes e for the medial short i spelling in click.","Substitutes e for the medial short i spelling in fin.","Substitutes e for the medial short i spelling in kit.","Substitutes e for the medial short i spelling in lid.","Substitutes e for the medial short i spelling in list.","Substitutes e for the medial short i spelling in milk.","Substitutes e for the medial short i spelling in mist.","Substitutes e for the medial short i spelling in slip.","Substitutes e for the medial short o spelling in drop.","Substitutes e for the medial short o spelling in fox.","Substitutes e for the medial short o spelling in hop.","Substitutes e for the medial short o spelling in log.","Substitutes e for the medial short o spelling in plot.","Substitutes e for the medial short o spelling in pond.","Substitutes e for the medial short o spelling in rod.","Substitutes e for the medial short o spelling in stop.","Substitutes e for the medial short o spelling in top.","Substitutes e for the medial short u spelling in clump.","Substitutes e for the medial short u spelling in crust.","Substitutes e for the medial short u spelling in cub.","Substitutes e for the medial short u spelling in cut.","Substitutes e for the medial short u spelling in jump.","Substitutes e for the medial short u spelling in lump.","Substitutes e for the medial short u spelling in rug.","Substitutes e for the medial short u spelling in truck.","Substitutes e for the medial short u spelling in tub.","Substitutes i for the medial short a spelling in band.","Substitutes i for the medial short a spelling in camp.","Substitutes i for the medial short a spelling in cap.","Substitutes i for the medial short a spelling in crab.","Substitutes i for the medial short a spelling in flap.","Substitutes i for the medial short a spelling in jam.","Substitutes i for the medial short a spelling in rat.","Substitutes i for the medial short a spelling in sand.","Substitutes i for the medial short e spelling in bench.","Substitutes i for the medial short e spelling in hen.","Substitutes i for the medial short e spelling in melt.","Substitutes i for the medial short e spelling in peg.","Substitutes i for the medial short e spelling in pet.","Substitutes i for the medial short e spelling in step.","Substitutes i for the medial short e spelling in tent.","Suki forgot a step during dance practice and stopped moving. Her partner showed her how to follow the drumbeats. They practised the tricky part slowly, then joined the rest of the dance. At the show, Suki heard the familiar beats and completed the step without stopping.","Suki had a calm and quiet bedtime routine. Papa dimmed the lamp and read a story. The quiet room helped her relax. Soon her eyes closed and she fell asleep.","Sunlight reached the books on our table.","Suri looked through glass at warm loaves and trays of rolls. Behind the counter, a worker dusted flour from an apron and placed dough into an oven. Suri chose a seeded loaf, which the worker sliced and wrapped before taking payment.","Suri noticed an extra point beside her name during the final game. The scorer had counted one of her missed throws as a success. Nobody else appeared to have noticed the small error on the board. Correcting it would place another player just ahead of her. Suri waited for the pause, then explained which throw had missed. The scorer checked and changed the total before announcing the winner. Suri clapped with the others and collected her own score sheet. The mark beside that throw now matched what had really happened.","Take any grey toolbox with yellow parts.","Take the grey toolbox with a yellow handle and no wheels.","Take the grey toolbox with yellow wheels and no handle.","Tali wrote a short announcement about the plant sale. Her teacher checked the date, and Niko copied the corrected words onto a card. At assembly, the microphone was passed to Tali, who read the card to the school. Niko stood beside the poster showing the prices.","Talia helped sort rolls before the bakery opened. One tray held plain rolls with soft tops. The other held rolls covered in seeds. Baker Tom chose the seeded rolls for the front counter. Talia put the plain rolls on the back shelf. Customers could see both trays through the glass. A small card named each kind of roll. The seeds were scattered over the crust, not inside. Tom wanted those dotted tops facing the waiting customers.","Tam felt hungry when the ice cream music played. He thought the tune caused his hunger. Mom asked him to notice days without the truck. He was hungry at four on those days too. Lunch was early, and his body needed food again. The truck followed its route even when nobody bought a cone. Tam noticed his empty stomach before hearing it on Tuesday. On Wednesday, the truck never came down their road. He still wanted his usual afternoon snack at about the same time.","Tam wanted to fold one hundred paper birds. He made a few after school each day. He kept the finished birds in a large box. At last, he counted one hundred birds to hang.","Tao’s paper boat sank in the bath. He folded another with higher sides. It floated but tipped when he added stones. Tao tried a wider base on his next boat. This one stayed upright with the small stones inside.","Tariq found the wheel under a chair.","Tessa promised to water a neighbour''s plants during a holiday. On the second morning, she forgot and went out to play. When she returned, she saw one pot beginning to dry. She watered it, told the neighbour what had happened, and set reminders. For the rest of the week, she checked every pot carefully. The neighbour thanked her for being honest and making a better plan. Tessa understood that repairing a mistake meant changing what came next. A promise needed actions that could be relied on each day.","Thank you for buying me a new pair of gloves.","Thank you for counting us while our teacher drove the bus.","Thank you for finding my dog.","Thank you for finding my glove and bringing it back.","Thank you for fixing my bike. I will ride it home tonight.","Thank you for fixing the swing.","Thank you for giving me a lovely dog.","Thank you for hearing my story.","Thank you for helping me find my dog.","Thank you for helping me learn my lines for the play.","Thank you for lending your bike. I will bring it back tonight.","Thank you for looking after our plants.","Thank you for making my costume for the school play.","Thank you for planting new seedlings; they are growing well.","Thank you for sharing your book; I liked the tale about the moon.","Thank you for showing me where to find it.","Thank you for teaching me that game.","Thank you for the book, Gran.","Thank you for the maps; we will show you how to read them.","Thank you for the picnic basket.","Thank you for the ramp; now I can wheel my trolley inside.","Thank you for the trolley; now I can carry it up the steps.","Thank you for waiting for me.","Thank you for waiting until our teacher had counted everyone.","Thank you for watering my plant.","Thank you for writing your book; I liked the tale about the moon.","Thank you, cooks, for making lunch, and helpers, for serving it.","Thank you, helpers, for making lunch, and cooks, for serving it.","Thanks for fixing my zip!","Thanks for making room!","Thanks for sheltering our seedlings; they are thriving.","Thanks for the maps and map-reading lesson.","Thanks for the picture!","That","The Hidden Gate","The Lost Sock","The address label was stuck on the top of the box.","The alarm’s ring brought everyone to the door.","The apartment went dark just as supper was ready. A message said the electricity would return later that evening. Mom used battery lamps instead of waiting to serve the food. Without television, everyone began telling stories around the small table. Grandma taught a clapping game she remembered from childhood. Even after the lights returned, nobody reached for the remote. They finished their game before clearing the bowls away together. The family decided to spend another evening like that next week.","The aquarium''s otters kept climbing out of their small pool. Cameras showed them stacking stones against the glass each night. The keepers could have removed every stone from the pool. Instead, they watched how much the animals enjoyed moving things. They built a larger space with tunnels and deeper water. Safe stones remained, but the outer walls were much higher. The otters explored, carried stones, and swam through the tunnels. They stopped escaping while keeping activities that interested them.","The audience was quiet in the small hall. The musician’s flute was still beside her chair. The musician lifted her flute. She played a tune. Then she bowed to the listeners.","The bag was beside Leo’s untidy picnic spot. Its opening needed closing before the final lift. Leo filled a bag with rubbish. He tied the bag shut. Then he put it in the bin.","The bake sale opened at ten o''clock. All the oat bars sold by ten thirty. The principal arrived at eleven o''clock. She bought the last brownie on the plate. Then the helpers packed the table away.","The baker took four loaves from the oven. A tray on the counter held dough that had not been baked. She placed the hot loaves on a wire rack beside the back window. Later, when they were cool, she arranged them in the glass display by the till.","The bakery opens before the sun is up. Trays of rolls slide into the big oven. The smell of warm bread drifts down the street. At eight, people line up outside for fresh bread.","The ball rolled into a puddle.","The band announced a place for one new drummer next month. Applicants would play a short rhythm chosen by the music teacher. Noel copied that rhythm and borrowed the practice recording after school. He could already play other tunes, but repeated this one each day. He asked what time applicants needed to arrive at the hall. The band usually rehearsed on Tuesdays beside the school office. Several friends planned to watch, though they were not applying themselves. Noel marked the arrival time beside the rhythm in his notebook.","The basket full of apples fell over.","The basket full of fresh apples.","The bat broke when the ball hit it.","The bat chased insects in the evening air.","The bat clung to the roof of the cave.","The bat folded its wings against its body.","The bat hung upside down from a branch.","The bat opened its mouth to catch a moth.","The bat returned to its colony before dawn.","The bat slept until the light began to fade.","The bat slept upside down in the cave.","The bat slipped from the player’s hands.","The bat spread its wings and flew out.","The bat swooped between two trees after sunset.","The batter gripped the bat with both hands.","The bear has strong legs for swimming.","The bear’s pale fur helps it blend into snow.","The bee flew to the rose.","The bell is made from shiny metal.","The bell rang.","The bell was cleaned during the holiday.","The bell’s ring told us that the break had ended.","The berries were ready for jam, but still dusty. First, Hana washed them in a bowl of clean water. Only the fruit belonged in the cooking pot. She then boiled the berries with sugar until thick. A cold plate would show whether the mixture was ready. Next, she tested a spoonful on that plate. The small sample held its shape instead of running. Only after that test did she fill the clean jars. Runny jam would have needed more cooking before this final step.","The big red bus.","The bird feeder stayed empty of visitors behind the fence.","The birds stopped visiting after we moved the feeder.","The blue card alone had no decoration.","The blue crate held books, and the red crate held games. Every book had a yellow return label. The games had plain white labels. The library helper needed to send only the books back today. Games would stay for the rainy-day club next week. Both crates were full, so their contents were hidden beneath lids. The coloured crates and labels were listed together on the collection sheet. No empty boxes were waiting beside them.","The book fair filled the school hall for a day. Tables held picture books and joke books. An author signed books at a special table. Reading club tokens counted double at the fair. The librarian wore a dress with a book pattern. Classes visited one at a time after morning lessons. Children could browse the picture tables or hear a short reading. The signing table stood near the hall''s open doors. Books borrowed with tokens still needed returning to the library later.","The bookcase was too heavy to shift while full. First, Ren removed every book from its shelves. The empty wooden frame was much easier to move. Next, he moved the bookcase away from the wall. A thick line of dust lay behind its old position. He then vacuumed that newly uncovered strip of floor. Last, he put the books onto the shelves again. The bookcase now stood a little farther from the wall. Cleaning the hidden floor required the earlier moving step.","The box can hold books, games and drawing paper.","The box was beside a sunny window. Its lid had a soft cushion on top. The cat jumped onto the box. Then it curled into a ball. Last, it fell asleep.","The bread stayed uncovered on the board overnight. No bag protected it from the dry air. Air dried out its soft edges. By morning, the slices felt hard. Dad wrapped the next loaf after breakfast.","The bread was soft and pale at breakfast. Dad liked his butter on hot, crisp toast. Dad put bread in the toaster. The toast popped up. Then he spread butter on it.","The bright stars.","The bus and van","The bus stop was hard to see at dusk. Neighbours asked for a lamp beside the sign. They cleared bushes that hid the waiting area. A new bench gave older people somewhere to sit. More people could use it comfortably.","The bus stopped at our stop.","The café tables were in hot sunlight. A worker opened the awning above them, and the tables became shaded. Later, a breeze moved the leaves nearby. Customers sat under the awning with their drinks.","The cake sank because the oven door opened too soon. The baker had also used too little flour. She kept the correct cooking time and used fresh eggs. The written recipe gave an amount larger than she had measured. The door should also have stayed closed while the mixture rose. Her kitchen clock matched the time in the recipe. The egg carton showed yesterday''s delivery date. Checking these details separated two mistakes from two things done properly.","The candle flame stayed still in the hall. Dad opened the door and let air blow through. The moving air made the flame bend. It flickered until he shut the door.","The card had a yellow border and rounded corners.","The card showed whose turn it was, so each pupil could speak.","The caretaker found the school cat waiting outside his store room. Winter mornings made the concrete floor there feel especially cold. He folded an old blanket into a dry box for it. Each morning, he checked its water before making his own tea. If he was absent, he asked another worker to check instead. Children sometimes stopped near the doorway to watch the animal. His tools remained on high shelves, away from the box. The cat usually slept there while the caretaker cleaned nearby rooms.","The cat is hiding under the bed.","The cat is sitting beside the bed.","The centre of our puzzle was still empty.","The child opened a parcel.","The child wanted something that could glide. A flat sheet would not fly like a plane. The child found some paper. She folded it into a plane. Then she flew it across the room.","The child who found the shell showed it to us.","The child who found the shiny shell.","The children entered the museum through the garden door. They walked past the gift shop to lockers beside the stairs. Their bags stayed in the lockers while they visited the dinosaur room. At lunch, they collected their bags and carried them outside.","The children on the list played while everyone else waited all week.","The children outside after the rain.","The children were sharing water after their walk. When the jug was almost empty, their teacher refilled it from the tap. The cups had been washed before the walk. After the refill, there was enough water for the children still waiting in line.","The choir had one last practice before the show. First the singing was too quiet. Then it was too fast. Miss Obi clapped a steady beat for them. Soon their voices joined in time.","The choir travelled to a concert in two groups. Seven singers took the train with two teachers. Five other singers came in a minibus. Everyone met at the hall, where three musicians were already waiting. The train group arrived first and carried in the music folders.","The class attached paper to a kite frame and tied on a short tail. They took it outside for a first test flight. The kite kept spinning, so they brought it down and lengthened the tail. During the second flight, it stayed steady. Only then did they decorate the paper with coloured stripes.","The class brought nine jars to the pond. Four jars were filled with pond water for a science activity. Three held clean water for washing hands, and two stayed empty as spares. The teacher placed all nine in a crate for the walk back to school.","The class built a tall tower of blocks. Its bottom row had only two small pieces. Jo placed a heavy block on the top. The tower began to lean towards the rug. Everyone stepped away from the wobbling blocks.","The class chose to sell soup at the winter fair. Different teams washed vegetables, measured spices, and set out bowls. While Priya chopped, Sam stirred the pot to prevent sticking. The first taste seemed plain, so they added some ginger. A teacher helped serve the hot soup safely to visitors. Soon the queue stretched past the classroom''s open door. Every bowl sold, raising money for the shared garden. They counted enough money to buy the garden''s new hose.","The class cleaned its aquarium on Friday. They rinsed the gravel through a strainer. They wiped the glass inside and outside. The plastic castle was washed until it shone. The fish waited in a bucket during all this work. The teacher kept the fish safely away from the cleaning tools. Fresh water was ready beside the tank before work began. Each helper had a different task written on a card. The class checked those cards to make sure nothing was missed.","The class counted animals during a nature walk. They saw five snails on a wall. Three white butterflies flew beside the hedge. One turtle crossed the path in front. Everyone waited until it reached the other side.","The class found litter beside their pond. They wore gloves and collected it safely with adults. They sorted rubbish from things that could be recycled. A helper took the full bags away. Soon, the water’s edge was clear again.","The class had a cleanup race before home time. One team stacked the chairs. Another team collected the pencils. The last team wiped the tables. Soon the room was neat. Each child earned a sticker for helping.","The class had four jobs before its concert. On Monday, the children chose a song with a repeated chorus. Its words were short enough for everyone to remember. On Tuesday, they learned those words with their music teacher. On Wednesday, they added actions to go with the chorus. Each movement matched something named in the song. On Friday, they performed the song for their families. The words and actions were both part of that performance. Their wall chart showed the four stages in separate boxes.","The class kept seedlings beside a sunny window during the day. Last week, some leaves touched the glass through a freezing night. Those leaves turned brown, while plants on an inside shelf stayed green. Both groups had received the same water during the daytime. Tonight''s forecast warned that the temperature would fall just as low. The children carried the window plants to that inside shelf. They planned to return them to the sunny place after breakfast. Labels stayed on the pots so their growth could still be compared.","The class left its cardboard model outside overnight. Rain soaked the roof and made it soft, so the roof sagged into the model. A plastic flag on top stayed bright. The next morning, the class brought the model inside and began making a waterproof cover.","The class made one shelter with sticks and another with card. They tested both using the same small fan. Only the stick shelter stayed standing during the test. Both shelters stood on the same table at equal distances. The children used the fan''s lowest setting for each trial. They kept its direction the same so the comparison was fair. After the test, one shelter lay flat against its base. The other still had a roof held up by its walls.","The class moved large shells from a tray to a glass case. A small striped shell was too fragile to move, so it stayed in its padded box on the teacher’s desk. Its label was copied onto the class poster. The poster hung outside the classroom for visitors.","The class packed snacks for the field trip. Apples and cheese stayed in the fridge until morning. Crackers stayed in a sealed box on the counter. Ms. Reed checked both places before loading the cooler. She packed the apples and cheese last. The cooler already held ice packs beneath an empty tray. The crackers did not need to stay cold before departure. Their sealed box kept them dry instead. All snacks would travel together despite being stored separately overnight.","The class pet needed a school-break home. Four children offered to care for it. Miss Adu picked a name from a cup. The folded paper had Femi''s name on it. Femi carried the pet''s cage to the car.","The class placed a bowl beneath a dripping tap. By lunchtime, the bowl was nearly full. The caretaker tightened a loose part inside the tap. The dripping stopped, and no more water collected. The class emptied the bowl onto thirsty plants.","The class planned a small exhibition about local birds. Each group chose one kind to study for a week. The robin group drew its bird with coloured pencils. They made a clay nest and wrote a short label. Their display also included a map of the school garden. Small red dots marked places where robins had been seen. Blue dots marked feeding places, not sightings. Visitors could compare the two sets of marks at the exhibition.","The class planned races and games for a picnic beside the river. A forecast warned of heavy rain before the agreed afternoon start. Some children wanted to continue because their posters already named the place. Others suggested keeping the games but using the school hall. They changed races into safe indoor challenges and moved the food. When rain arrived, the outdoor tables stood empty under dripping trees. Inside, the children enjoyed the same shared afternoon they had hoped for. The posters gained a new room number beside the original date.","The class prepared apples for their snack. First, they washed the fruit under cool water. Next, an adult cut it into small slices. Last, the children shared the slices on plates. Everyone sat down at the clean table to eat.","The class put a rain gauge beside their vegetable patch. Each morning, they measured the water and emptied the tube. They added that amount to a chart covering the whole term. Several dry days made a row of low marks. After one storm, the day''s mark rose much higher. At term''s end, they compared the chart with garden notes. Plants needed extra watering during the long dry spell. Their simple daily measurements helped explain changes across the season.","The class reached its bus stop on time. A fallen branch blocked the usual road, so the driver used a longer route. No one got off at the wrong stop, and the bus worked normally. They arrived late at the museum because of the extra distance.","The class returned to find the book corner neatly arranged. Nobody had watched the helper work during the lunch break. Femi usually grouped things by colour before sorting them by size. His pencil tray showed short blue pencils beside long blue ones. The books now formed coloured groups, each running small to large. Other children usually grouped books only by subject or height. The teacher had left the labels and shelves unchanged that morning. Three children had stayed indoors, including Femi, while the others played.","The class sat in a circle on the carpet near the window.","The class sorted twelve books. Five stories went on the bottom shelf. Four nature books fitted on the top shelf. The remaining three books stayed on the table until the class found another shelf for them.","The class tested why their paper boats kept sinking. One boat had a small tear along its folded bottom. Another carried several heavy pebbles instead of one light bead. Water entered the first boat through the torn paper. The second boat sat so low that water spilled over. Both boats sank, but the routes for water were different. The class made a fresh boat with sound folds. They loaded it lightly and watched it float across the tray.","The class visited a workshop where a potter shaped clay. A wheel turned while her hands pressed the soft sides. When a pot leaned, she slowed the wheel and adjusted it. She did not throw away the uneven pot or hurry. She tried a small change, paused and looked again. Soon the rim stood level all the way around. The children watched without interrupting while she worked. Afterwards, one child praised her patience with the difficult task.","The class waited for the bell, but heard nothing. Its battery had run out. A loose sign beside the bell rattled in the wind. When a new battery was fitted, the bell rang loudly again.","The class wanted to compare shadows at different times of day. They placed a stick upright in a pot of sand. Its base stayed in one marked place on the playground. At each visit, a child marked the shadow''s tip with chalk. The group wrote the time beside every new chalk mark. Only after the final visit did they join the marks. Then they could compare both the direction and the length. Moving the pot between visits would have spoiled their comparison.","The class was on a trip by bus. The children were safely seated near the doors. The driver was at the front. The bus stopped. Its doors opened. Then the children stepped off.","The class watched a snail move across clear plastic. Ivo held a leaf near the end to encourage it forward. Asha followed the path with a marker on the other side of the plastic. The teacher took a photograph of the children when they had finished. The marker line showed every turn.","The class watched one egg in a warm incubator. On the first day, Ms Vale placed the egg inside. Its shell was smooth and whole at that point. On day nineteen, a small crack appeared in the shell. A day after the crack, children heard a quiet cheep. The chick was still hidden, although they could hear it. On day twenty-one, the chick finally hatched from the shell. Their chart had four pictures showing these changes. Each picture had the day written beneath it.","The classroom clock stopped during reading time. Ella saw both hands pointing at twelve. Mr. Reed changed its battery after lunch. The clock began ticking before home time. Ella watched its long hand move again.","The classroom window was open during a windy afternoon. A gust swept across the teacher''s desk. The loose papers scattered over the floor. Two children collected them and closed the window. They put a heavy book on the pile.","The clay felt too dry to shape.","The climbing frame is open again; the slide is still closed.","The coach replaced the bat because its wood had cracked.","The coach usually kept a whistle in a coat pocket. Before the match, the pocket was empty. She checked the equipment bag and found cones, but no whistle. A player spotted it hanging from a hook behind the changing-room door. The coach put it around her neck for the match.","The completed model was made during an art lesson.","The concert is now in the hall and starts at seven.","The concert is now in the hall and still starts at six.","The concert stays outside but will now start at seven.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word bird uses ir in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word burn uses ur in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word car uses ar in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word curl uses ur in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word dirt uses ir in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word far uses ar in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word fern uses er in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word first uses ir in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word germ uses er in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word girl uses ir in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word herd uses er in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word hurt uses ur in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word perch uses er in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word purse uses ur in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word serve uses er in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word shirt uses ir in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word stern uses er in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word term uses er in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word third uses ir in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word turn uses ur in the blank.","The conventional spelling of the exact spoken word verb uses er in the blank.","The costume had fitted perfectly before we shortened it.","The covered soil lost water more slowly than bare soil.","The crowd sang loudly, encouraging the band to play harder. Hearing the louder band, the crowd sang even louder. The band responded by playing harder again. Both groups kept encouraging each other through the final song. The extra volume did not start with both groups at once. At the first chorus, the audience''s strong singing changed the playing. During the next chorus, that stronger playing changed the singing. Looking at only one chorus would miss part of the whole pattern.","The cup had a small hole underneath. Jo poured water into it at the table. Water escaped through the hole onto the wood. The cup could not keep the water inside.","The damaged bat was replaced before the match.","The dog beside the gate and the fence.","The dog beside the gate barked at us.","The dog came in wearing Dad''s large woolly hat. Ivy saw the hat and started laughing. Her family looked at the dog too. They also laughed at its funny hat.","The dog dug up a bone.","The dog had dirt stuck in its coat. Its clean fur needed care before brushing. Nia washed the muddy dog. She dried its fur. Then she brushed its fur smooth.","The dog loved fetching things in the park. Noah had one tennis ball for their game. Noah threw the ball. The dog chased it. Then the dog brought it back.","The door made a loud noise when opened. Grandma added oil to its stiff hinge. She moved the door to spread the oil. The door then opened quietly without waking Baby.","The doorbell’s ring sent the puppy running.","The dough was for one long bread loaf. A tray was ready beside the warm oven. The baker mixed some dough. She shaped it into a loaf. Then she put it in the oven.","The drawers were built into a cupboard beside the window.","The fair made more money than usual. Warm, dry weather kept visitors there all afternoon. A new cake stand was very popular and sold out. More families also came because it was a local holiday. The helpers counted the money after closing. Families had time to try games instead of rushing home. The cake stall brought extra spending as well as extra visitors. Helpers compared the total with last year''s smaller crowd. Their notes named the weather, holiday and new stall as helpful changes.","The family found every plugged-in clock blinking when they arrived home. Dad''s battery clock still showed the correct time beside the stairs. The freezer door was shut, but its ice cream felt soft. A lamp came on while nobody was touching its switch. Next door, a neighbour called out that her lights had returned. The family had been away since breakfast, with all doors locked. Mum checked which food needed throwing away before resetting the clocks. Outside, workers were packing tools beside the street''s electricity box.","The family moved slowly between paintings hung on plain walls. Small labels gave the artists’ names and dates. A guide explained how one artist had used light and shadow. The children were asked to look closely without touching the pictures.","The family returned with their folded picnic blanket still perfectly dry. Their coat sleeves, however, dripped onto the mat by the door. Dad shook water from an umbrella before bringing it inside. Small leaves stuck to boots that had been clean that morning. Outside, water rushed along the gutter beneath a dark sky. Mum put the unopened food back into the kitchen fridge. The bathroom was dry, and nobody carried swimming towels. They hung their coats apart instead of leaving them in a heap.","The feeder had been full when Dad locked the garden gate. By morning, its metal pole leaned sharply towards the trees. The wooden tray lay cracked beneath it, with seeds scattered nearby. Deep paw prints crossed the soft ground behind the pole. Dad placed his boot beside one print; the print was wider. There were no shoe marks along the feeder''s usual path. The night had been calm, and other tall plants stood straight. A trail of matching prints continued through a gap towards the woods.","The finished class poster hung beside the library door. Its photos had been glued down before it could hang there. Before gluing, Bo had arranged those pictures on the painted background. That background had dried before any photos touched it. Wet paint could have stuck to the pictures in unwanted places. Dry paint let Bo compare different spaces without a mess. The glue made the final arrangement stay in place. The last stage required photos that would stay attached when upright.","The fire station invited families inside on Saturday morning. Children tried heavy helmets while a firefighter explained their purpose. Another firefighter unfolded the ladder used for reaching high windows. Visitors sprayed a practice hose and felt how strongly water pushed. Inside the truck, they saw tools for rescuing people from cars. The crew also explained why equipment must be checked every day. Some children had thought the job meant only putting out flames. One child asked whether the crew trained for river rescues.","The first choir arrived early and used the hall for rehearsal. Another choir''s bus broke down, delaying singers who had prepared equally carefully. The programme could start on time only by leaving their performance out. The early choir agreed to shorten its extra practice and wait. Some singers worried that less rehearsal might reduce their own advantage. Both groups eventually performed before an audience including the late arrivals’ families. The early choir still tried its best when its turn came. Afterwards, members applauded the other singers without asking for extra points.","The first tray grew best because its soil stayed soaked.","The fish in the pond.","The floor dried quickly because the sun came out.","The flower bed was wet when Aisha put the watering can away. She had just watered the new marigolds with it. Earlier, she had planted those small flowers in the cleared soil. Clearing the bed had meant pulling out its crowded weeds first. Those weeds would have competed with the new plants for space. The marigolds now had room around each little stem. Water could reach their roots through the loose bare soil. The empty can belonged on a shelf beside the back door.","The framed photograph had a blue prize ribbon beside it. Before framing it, Malik had entered its paper print in a contest. Before entering, he had printed his chosen photograph at home. The picture itself came from an earlier trip to the harbour. He had taken it while gulls rested on a fishing boat. The camera image needed to become a print for this contest. Its rules required paper entries instead of files on a screen. The frame protected that same print after the contest was over.","The garden chart said to water on Mondays and Thursdays. Another rule said to skip watering after heavy rain. Rain poured all Thursday morning. Meena checked the chart and looked at the wet garden. She left the watering can under the sink. The rule was there to stop roots sitting in soaked soil. Monday had been dry, so watering had gone ahead then. Thursday''s rain was heavy enough to fill the shallow dishes outside. The chart stayed on the wall; its usual days had not changed.","The garden club checked their plants after a hot weekend. Lettuce leaves were limp, but the tomatoes still stood tall. Mr. Hayes asked them to water the limp plants first. They watered the lettuce before the tomatoes. Then they wrote both jobs in their notebook. The watering can held enough for only one row. A second trip to the tap would supply the other row. The leaves, rather than the fruits, showed which plants needed help. Their notebook recorded the order as well as both plant names.","The gardener brought seedlings to our class. Our teacher showed us how deep to dig. The children put each seedling into the soil. The caretaker fetched water when our jug ran dry. Everyone helped tidy the tools.","The group hoped to take the short path beside the stream. Heavy rain had covered it with water, so the leader chose the longer path over the hill. The group had packed lunch before leaving home. They ate it at the viewpoint and continued towards the village.","The group washed apples and cut them into thin slices. They spread the slices on trays and left them to dry. Each day they turned the slices. When the fruit was dry, they packed it into jars. They washed and dried the empty trays, then put the trays away in the cupboard.","The guide brought twenty children to the beach in the morning. Each wore a blue coat, making faces hard to distinguish from behind. Before leaving, she checked their names beside the picnic shelter. One child had wandered behind a rock earlier while looking for shells. At the bus, several children changed places while bags were loaded. The guide waited for everyone to sit, then checked each name again. She compared the final list with the morning list before departure. Only after both lists matched did she signal to the driver.","The guide turned off the cave lights briefly. Noor gripped her torch tightly in the darkness. She counted slowly, waiting for the light. She stayed still, though she wanted to run outside.","The hand signal made it harder for the group to stop together.","The handles are painted the same blue as the lid.","The heater warmed the room, and then the ice sculpture melted. Turning the heater off slowed the melting. The sculpture had stayed solid before the room became warm. The display stood well away from the switch on the wall. Staff recorded the room temperature beside a picture of the sculpture. The temperature rose before the first drop appeared under the base. At the cooler setting, drops formed less quickly. The order and the changed setting supported the same explanation.","The heavy rain filled our small bucket completely.","The heavy rain.","The helpers arranged chairs in rows for the concert. Next, they checked that the paths between rows were clear. They opened the doors and welcomed the audience. When everyone had a seat, the lights dimmed and the curtain rose. The singers stepped forward only after the curtain was fully open.","The helpers painted a banner on the playground floor and waited for it to dry. Rain was expected, so they carried it indoors. They hung it above the entrance to the hall. Small paper flags stayed beneath the covered walkway, while a map was pinned to the noticeboard.","The hidden feeder had been easier for birds to reach.","The icy path was too slippery to walk on. Salt had melted the ice there before. Grandpa fetched a full salt bag and a scoop. He opened the bag beside the frozen path.","The island ferry carries different passengers throughout the day. Children board in the morning to reach the mainland school. At noon, boxes of food travel back to the island shops. Workers return home on a later crossing after their shifts. When strong storms arrive, the captain keeps the boat tied safely. Islanders then use stored supplies while waiting for calmer water. They plan important journeys around the ferry''s published times. The captain checks the water again before restarting the service.","The jeweler measured the ring before repairing it.","The laundromat on our street hums all morning. Round windows spin with socks and shirts. Mrs Kaur folds warm towels into tall piles. People chat while they wait for clean clothes.","The leaves on the path.","The leaves were collected from several kinds of tree.","The lettuce seeds began life in a tray indoors. Maya sowed them there during the cold weeks of March. A warm windowsill protected them from frost outside. In April, she moved the young plants into the garden. The nights were mild enough for outdoor growing by then. After the move, she watered the growing lettuces throughout May. Their roots needed moisture while the leaves became bigger. In June, she cut the leafy heads for lunch. Her calendar showed one main stage in each month.","The library printer stopped halfway through making the club''s posters. Its power light stayed on, and the screen showed no paper warning. Jo had just filled the tray, which still held many sheets. She opened the back cover and found one sheet tightly folded. Its edge blocked the rollers that normally pulled each page through. A helper carefully removed it according to the printed instructions. Jo closed the cover, and the remaining posters began coming out. Nothing else was replaced or plugged in during the repair.","The library wanted more families to visit. It opened on Saturday mornings and added story sessions. Volunteers made a quiet corner for adults and a low shelf for children. More families began staying to read together.","The light bag was easy to lift.","The list meant that no one could use the game any more.","The little frog rested on a smooth log.","The loose ring slipped off into the sink.","The lunch bell rang across the busy school. Omar slid his tray along the serving counter. A worker gave him warm peas and rice. He sat at a long table with his class.","The mail cart comes up our road at nine. Letters slide through mail slots, flap, flap, flap. The family at number twelve signs for a package. Our dog waits by the mail slot each morning.","The map has a green border and a picture of our logo.","The metal bowl gave a clear ring when tapped.","The milk in the shop by the road.","The missing grey mitten has a red thumb and torn cuff.","The missing red mitten has a grey thumb and new cuff.","The missing red mitten has a grey thumb and torn cuff.","The model walls were cut from thick cardboard.","The moon does not really change its shape. The moon circles the Earth. Sunlight lights up one side of it. Some nights we see the whole bright side. Other nights we see only a thin piece. That is why the moon looks different.","The morning ferry left at nine and took thirty minutes. Its afternoon return left at three and took forty minutes. Heavy wind slowed the return journey. The ticket listed a departure time for each trip separately. The outward crossing ended at the island before the shops opened. On the way home, waves pushed against the ferry''s bow. The crew still followed the usual route past the lighthouse. The longer trip took ten extra minutes, rather than several hours.","The mother bat carried her baby beneath a wing.","The museum lent our school a box about old kitchens. It contained a wooden spoon and a heavy iron pot. There was also a cloth used to cover rising dough. A card explained how families once baked their bread. The teacher placed each object on a low display table. Pupils could handle the spoon while sitting with an adult. They could look at the heavy pot but not lift it. At lunch, every object went back into the box.","The museum put three rules beside the dinosaur room. Visitors had to walk on its smooth floor. They had to whisper so others could listen. They could take photos but could not use flash. A helper showed each group the rules before entering. The first rule kept people from slipping on the polished floor. The quiet voices helped visitors hear the short dinosaur recording. The photo rule protected the experience without banning cameras altogether. The notice listed those three rules together in large, clear writing.","The museum trip had four parts on the teacher''s plan. At the entrance, children left their bags in numbered lockers. Large bags were not allowed beside the delicate displays. Next came their visit to the dinosaur gallery upstairs. Lunch was the next part of the plan. The class ate in the courtyard beyond that gallery. Before leaving, they collected their bags from the lockers. Each child still had the same numbered key. The plan put collecting bags after lunch, not before the exhibition.","The nature club kept a record of the pond. On Monday, its members saw frogspawn near the reeds. They drew the clear jelly and the dark dots inside. On Friday, tiny swimmers had appeared in the same place. They had tails but no legs yet. The children used a guide to name this new stage. Their drawings showed how the creatures changed during the week. Nobody took any creatures home; all stayed in the pond.","The nest was empty after the summer.","The new bat had tape around its handle.","The new girl always ate her lunch alone. Bo sat beside her and shared his grapes. Later, Bo broke his arm and needed help. She cut his lunch and stayed beside him chatting.","The new library is open now.","The new playground was planned with several families'' advice. Firm paths let wheels move between different parts of the site. Ramps offer a route where steps would stop some visitors. Certain swings have extra support around the seat and back. Children who find busy noise difficult can use quiet corners. Signs use clear pictures as well as written words. Families tested the plans before builders finished the equipment. Two friends now reach the same climbing platform by different routes.","The nights stayed below freezing all week. The pond water turned to solid ice. On Saturday, ducks stood on its frozen top. Their flat feet slid as they tried walking.","The notice said to bring a hat and a water bottle. Lunch would be provided at the park. Children also needed shoes suitable for walking. The outing involved a long path with very little shade. A drink refill point would be available beside the picnic area. The school kitchen would send enough sandwiches for every child. Teachers wanted families to read the whole notice before packing. Bringing the listed items would leave less to carry during the walk.","The nut had a shell too hard for biting. The solid path could help break it open. A crow picked up a nut. It dropped the nut onto a hard path. Then it ate from the broken shell.","The old footbridge closed while workers replaced its damaged boards. Families had to follow a longer path beside the stream. At first, everyone complained about the extra walk to school. Then children noticed blackberries growing beside a hollow tree. A heron stood in the shallows, waiting for a fish. They began leaving early so they could watch it hunting. When the repaired bridge opened, the short route was safe. Some families still chose the longer path on quiet mornings.","The old library will close next year.","The old phone box stood empty after its telephone was removed. Instead of taking it away, the town repaired its door. Volunteers fitted shelves and brought books they had finished reading. Anyone could choose a book and leave another in return. A neighbour checked that the shelves stayed clean and dry. New titles arrived as different readers used the little box. People began stopping to chat about stories they had enjoyed. By Friday, a returned comic had three new borrowers waiting.","The old swing rope was worn after years of weather. Its knot rubbed against a rough branch on every swing. On Sunday, two children sat on the seat together. The worn rope broke under their weight. The rubbing point was thinner than the rest of the rope. A single rider usually placed less strain on that weak spot. This time the extra weight pulled on the damaged fibres together. An adult removed the broken swing so nobody else could use it.","The orchard team filled eleven baskets with apples. Five baskets were carried straight to the kitchen for making juice. Three were placed on a stall to sell. The remaining three were left in a cool shed for another day. Empty baskets were stacked beside the trees.","The oven window was so greasy that the baker could not see inside. When the oven was cool, she cleaned the glass thoroughly. During the next bake she could see the cake through the same window. The kitchen light and the oven lamp were unchanged.","The page tore as Tess turned it too quickly. She found clear tape and some small scissors. She placed the torn edges neatly together. She cut a strip slightly longer than the tear.","The paint dried overnight.","The painter rolled a patch of blue onto the wall, but the colour grew faint. The paint tray was nearly empty. She put the roller down, removed the lid from the blue paint tin and lifted the tin towards the tray.","The paper and ribbon were for a birthday. Ana wanted her friend’s present to be covered. Ana wrapped the gift. She tied a bow around it. Then she gave it to her friend.","The paper chain broke between two loops. Eva picked up a short strip of sticky tape and brought the torn edges together. She held them still with one hand while peeling the tape from her other hand.","The parcel for Aunt Mara already had an address on top. Ffion had written it after closing the box with tape. Before taping it, she had packed three wrapped jam jars inside. Earlier still, she had filled those jars with homemade jam. Paper around the jars kept their glass sides apart. The closed box left no room for another jar inside. The address belonged on the outside, where the post office could see. Each of these four jobs depended on the previous one being finished.","The park had a sign asking visitors to protect new grass. A narrow path ran around the area marked with rope. On Saturday, fresh footprints crossed straight through the middle. A football lay beside the far fence, beyond the rope. The groundskeeper noticed flattened blades following the footprints towards the ball. Other parts of the new lawn were still upright. A child suggested someone had crossed the grass to collect it. The groundskeeper agreed, but nobody had actually seen the person cross.","The path beside the river was closed after heavy rain. Fast water had washed soil away beneath part of it. A deep hollow made the surface unsafe for walkers. Workers put barriers at both ends of the damaged stretch. People used a longer route across the park instead. Some complained that the barriers made their journeys slower. The workers explained that the barriers were a response to danger. Once the ground was repaired, they could reopen the short route safely.","The phone rang while the family made lunch. A loud blender mixed a drink. The radio played music at full volume. Cushions also covered the phone, making its sound quieter. Nobody heard it above the other noises. The phone lay on the sofa near the kitchen doorway. Its screen lit up under a fold in the cloth. Sound from the kitchen reached the sofa much more loudly. The missed-call mark remained there after the music and blender stopped.","The phone’s ring interrupted our quiet game.","The picnic place was tidy when we left.","The plant was settled in its new pot.","The play took place on a stage with a red curtain.","The player wrapped fresh tape around the bat’s handle.","The playground after the heavy rain.","The playground ball rolled under a bench. Ava picked it up during the break. She passed it to Coach Lee. Her friend Mina brought back a skipping rope. Coach Lee put both in the equipment basket.","The playground needed a shady place for summer. The young tree had bare roots for planting. The class dug a hole. They planted a tree in it. Then they watered its roots.","The pool opened during my last school year.","The power went off along the street. Neighbors put candles in glass jars on their steps. Mr. Okoye brought his guitar outside and played songs. The food truck could cook using its own gas. It kept serving warm meals while people listened. The streetlamps were dark, but the small flames marked each doorstep. Adults kept the jars away from children and loose coats. People brought bowls to the truck rather than cooking at home. The music made the wait for repairs feel shorter for everyone.","The price depended on the parcel’s weight. Sera was at the counter inside the post office. Sera weighed the parcel. She paid for a stamp. Then she posted the parcel.","The printer ran out of paper, so the notices were delayed. Without notices, families did not know the new meeting time. Only a few arrived when the meeting began. The meeting had moved from afternoon to morning that week. The school usually sent paper messages home before any change. Staff had prepared them, but the empty tray stopped printing. Families were still following the old time on their calendars. A few already knew because they had spoken to staff.","The puddle was liquid at bedtime. Overnight the temperature dropped below freezing. By morning, a thin layer of ice covered it. The sun rose after the ice had already formed.","The puppet’s arm fell off during rehearsal. Mina suggested using glue, but the teacher explained that the arm needed to swing. The children joined it with a loose split pin. They wrapped tape around a crack in the puppet’s stand, then tried the moving arm again.","The puzzle was missing ten pieces. A search of the box uncovered three. Another four were beneath the mat. Two had slipped into a book, and one was inside a slipper. The children put every piece back before packing the completed puzzle away.","The race had finished, and everyone celebrated the runners.","The race had one clear turning point. A cone stood across the field from the start. The runner heard the signal. She ran to the cone. Then she touched it.","The rain stopped.","The ramp was painted to match the classroom door.","The reading corner felt cold beside the window. Max fetched two cushions from the shelf. He put one on the blue chair. He put the other on the rug. His group sat down for story time.","The recipe said to add one cup of oats. Uma found the measuring cup, opened the oat jar and placed the mixing bowl beside her. She held the cup over the jar so any spilled oats would fall back inside.","The recycling bin was full, and its lid would not close. The caretaker brought an empty bag, opened it beside the bin and put on work gloves. He checked the label to make sure the contents belonged in the recycling collection.","The red lunchbox has a silver lid and a handle.","The red lunchbox has a silver lid and no handle.","The repaired vase stood on display beside the classroom window. Before displaying it, Soraya had glued its two pieces together. The pieces had needed cleaning before the glue could hold. She had found them in a dusty cupboard before that cleaning. Fine dust can stop glue touching the surfaces firmly. Both pieces belonged to the same small clay vase. Its matching edges showed where the join needed to be. Cleaning was important because the dusty edges needed a firm bond.","The rescue crew had a call from an island. A visitor there needed a safe way back to shore. First, the crew launched its boat from the slipway. Next, it crossed the narrow channel to the island. The water between the island and mainland was too deep for walking. At the island, the waiting visitor came aboard. Only then did the crew return to the mainland. The boat had an empty passenger seat on the outward crossing. That seat was occupied during the final part of the rescue.","The rescue station has four main actions after an emergency call. First, the alarm sounds to summon the waiting crew. The crew then puts on waterproof suits and safety equipment. Those clothes protect them during work on cold, rough water. Next, they launch the rescue boat from its covered slipway. Only once afloat do they follow directions towards the reported location. The caller''s position guides this final journey. Dressing belongs before launch because the crew must be protected already. The training chart keeps these four actions in order.","The reviewed common u_e short-to-long pairs keep the other consonant phonemes unchanged. Us/use and hug/huge change consonants; rare words are not introduced to manufacture ten examples.","The ridged roof held several coins without sagging.","The ring fitted neatly inside the velvet box.","The ring of the telephone stopped suddenly.","The ring was polished until its stone shone.","The ring was too tight to slide past her knuckle.","The rock pool looked still, but many creatures lived there. A crab hid beneath a ledge until a bird''s shadow passed. A limpet held tightly to the rock as waves splashed over. Small fish darted between strands of seaweed to escape a bird. Each animal used a different way to stay safe. The class watched from the edge and kept their hands out. They recorded the shelter or action each creature used. Their notes showed several solutions to the same problem.","The rolled map showed the whole town. Ada needed to mark the place for catching trains. Ada unrolled the map. She found the station. Then she circled it with a pencil.","The roof keeps rain off people beneath it.","The roof was painted red before it was tested.","The room grew too dark for reading.","The room held art supplies and sports equipment.","The room was still until Anya opened the window. A breeze blew in and lifted the curtain away from the wall. When she shut the window again, the curtain settled. No one had touched the curtain while it was moving.","The roots were left lying on the table.","The rope snapped.","The runners lined up to start.","The school bell rang during art time. Mei left her painting on the drying rack. She washed the green paint from her hands. Then she joined her friends for lunch. Her painting stayed in the classroom.","The school bell’s ring carried across the field.","The school bus normally reached Leo''s stop before the bakery opened. Today, the bakery lights were on and customers already waited inside. Leo checked the timetable; the printed arrival time had passed. A message reported roadworks on the usual bus route. Two other families were waiting at the same marked stop. Their children wore different school coats and carried their ordinary bags. A baker wheeled fresh trays towards the shop''s front window. Leo could see cars moving on the clear road beside him.","The school newspaper lay in a neat pile by lunchtime. Inside was a photograph of yesterday''s winning relay team. The editor had chosen it from several pictures after breakfast. A caption beneath it named the runners in their race order. Before printing, the team captain checked those names for mistakes. Two volunteers then carried copies to each classroom door. By the final bell, pupils were discussing the photograph together. The remaining papers went on a shelf for families to take home.","The school orchestra held a concert in the hall. Before the families arrived, musicians checked their instruments in small groups. The teacher gave a signal when everyone was ready. The players walked onto the stage and took their places. After the audience became quiet, the first song began. Between songs, a pupil explained the next piece of music. At the end, all the musicians stood for a bow. They packed away only after the families had left the hall.","The school play had a large painted moon for its set. During rehearsal, it fell and tore across the middle. The children could not make another before the evening show. They joined the pieces with a sheet of card behind. One child covered the torn line with silver stars. From the audience seats, the repair looked like part of the design. The moon stayed in place throughout the performance. The group saved their set by adapting what they already had.","The school play needed a crown, sword and throne. Raj brought things made from a recycling box. Nell lent the clothes from her costume box. The sword was made of cardboard from the recycling box. Two gold chairs from the staff room became the throne. The crown had silver foil wrapped around a cardboard band. Nell''s box held a cape and a long red robe. The chairs needed no cutting or folding to become royal seats. Raj used blunt scissors to shape the sword before covering its edge.","The school''s vegetable bed was too dry during a warm week. A group measured how much water each row received. They found most water ran off the hard surface. They loosened the soil and spread straw around the plants. Then they watered slowly instead of emptying a full bucket. More water soaked in, and less escaped down the path. The leaves looked firmer the next morning. The group wrote down the changes so other classes could use them.","The seed needed water and soil to grow. Its pot had little holes underneath for drainage. Mia planted a seed in soil. She watered it each day. Later, a green shoot grew.","The seedling leaned towards the sunny window. Its pot was dry and light when Max lifted it. Max brought a jug of water and placed a saucer beneath the pot. He checked that the hole underneath was clear.","The sentence expresses strong feeling, so it needs an exclamation mark.","The sharp fork and shiny plate","The sharp ring of the timer ended the game.","The shed was built before I was born.","The shell could not go into the pan. The egg needed mixing for a soft omelette. Mom cracked an egg into a bowl. She whisked it with a fork. Then she cooked it in a pan.","The shelter stands near a path behind the school.","The shoot grew its first leaves.","The shortest route kept our equipment dry through the flood.","The silver bell.","The silver lunchbox has a red lid and no handle.","The silver ring fit her thumb perfectly.","The sleeve still dragged because no one measured it.","The slide is open again; the climbing frame is still closed.","The slope directs rain into the tray.","The small boat and raft","The soft lining stopped the model hitting the hard sides.","The softened clay could be formed into a bowl.","The soup stall had bread rolls and bowls of soup. A customer bought the last roll before noon. The next customer asked for soup, and the cook filled a bowl for her. After she left, the cook washed a cup that someone had returned. No more customers arrived.","The spaces separate 4 words. Punctuation attached to a word does not add another word.","The spaces separate 6 words. Punctuation attached to a word does not add another word.","The spoken word band has the short a vowel, conventionally spelled a here.","The spoken word bench has the short e vowel, conventionally spelled e here.","The spoken word camp has the short a vowel, conventionally spelled a here.","The spoken word cap has the short a vowel, conventionally spelled a here.","The spoken word click has the short i vowel, conventionally spelled i here.","The spoken word clump has the short u vowel, conventionally spelled u here.","The spoken word crab has the short a vowel, conventionally spelled a here.","The spoken word crust has the short u vowel, conventionally spelled u here.","The spoken word cub has the short u vowel, conventionally spelled u here.","The spoken word cut has the short u vowel, conventionally spelled u here.","The spoken word drop has the short o vowel, conventionally spelled o here.","The spoken word fin has the short i vowel, conventionally spelled i here.","The spoken word flap has the short a vowel, conventionally spelled a here.","The spoken word fox has the short o vowel, conventionally spelled o here.","The spoken word hen has the short e vowel, conventionally spelled e here.","The spoken word hop has the short o vowel, conventionally spelled o here.","The spoken word jam has the short a vowel, conventionally spelled a here.","The spoken word jump has the short u vowel, conventionally spelled u here.","The spoken word kit has the short i vowel, conventionally spelled i here.","The spoken word lid has the short i vowel, conventionally spelled i here.","The spoken word list has the short i vowel, conventionally spelled i here.","The spoken word log has the short o vowel, conventionally spelled o here.","The spoken word lump has the short u vowel, conventionally spelled u here.","The spoken word melt has the short e vowel, conventionally spelled e here.","The spoken word milk has the short i vowel, conventionally spelled i here.","The spoken word mist has the short i vowel, conventionally spelled i here.","The spoken word peg has the short e vowel, conventionally spelled e here.","The spoken word pet has the short e vowel, conventionally spelled e here.","The spoken word plot has the short o vowel, conventionally spelled o here.","The spoken word pond has the short o vowel, conventionally spelled o here.","The spoken word rat has the short a vowel, conventionally spelled a here.","The spoken word rod has the short o vowel, conventionally spelled o here.","The spoken word rug has the short u vowel, conventionally spelled u here.","The spoken word sand has the short a vowel, conventionally spelled a here.","The spoken word slip has the short i vowel, conventionally spelled i here.","The spoken word step has the short e vowel, conventionally spelled e here.","The spoken word stop has the short o vowel, conventionally spelled o here.","The spoken word tent has the short e vowel, conventionally spelled e here.","The spoken word top has the short o vowel, conventionally spelled o here.","The spoken word truck has the short u vowel, conventionally spelled u here.","The spoken word tub has the short u vowel, conventionally spelled u here.","The station escalator stopped working at the start of the week. People sighed when they saw the stairs they must climb. A musician asked permission to play beside the bottom step. His cheerful tunes gave tired passengers something pleasant to hear. Some people counted steps in time with the music. Others paused safely nearby and thanked him after a song. Workers repaired the escalator by Friday, ending the difficult climb. Passengers still remembered the unexpected pleasure of those extra steps.","The steep hill above the village.","The story was too long for one sitting. A bookmark would keep her place until tomorrow. The girl opened her book. She read a page. Then she put a bookmark inside.","The student newspaper asked pupils what needed changing at school. Many wanted somewhere quiet to read during the busy lunch break. The editors printed those requests and invited other suggestions too. They checked which spare rooms were safe and available then. A meeting with the headteacher settled how the space would run. Volunteers agreed to put books away after each lunch session. The following month, pupils began using a small former meeting room. Their newspaper later reported how readers and helpers were finding it.","The swimming lesson had three groups. Beginners stayed in water where they could stand. The middle group swam across with a kickboard. The top group swam full lengths without one. Keya moved from the middle group to the top group today. She handed her kickboard to a beginner. Each group had its own lane and teacher. Today''s move followed a check of Keya''s swimming earlier that morning. Her new teacher pointed to the far end of the pool. That group''s task was longer than the middle group''s crossing.","The team made a sign for their stall. They wrote the prices in dark blue paint. Then they left the card flat to dry. Finally, they fixed it to the front table. Now everyone could see what the fruit cost.","The teams ate orange slices during their half-time break. The referee''s half-time whistle had sounded just before the break. Elin had scored shortly before that whistle. Jo''s goal was earlier than Elin''s, in the opening minutes. Both goals were marked in order on the score sheet. The orange slices belonged to the rest period, not play. Four moments stood out in the coach''s account of the half. The whistle separated playing time from time for food and rest.","The teams had the same score before this. One more goal would put his team ahead. The boy kicked the ball. It went into the goal. Then his team cheered.","The tent leaned towards the path even though its poles were straight. A rope on the opposite side had slipped loose from its peg. Kim pulled the rope tight and secured it again. The tent stood upright without changing the poles or moving to another spot.","The top of the wormery had a layer of dead leaves. Jude had added them after placing worms on the soil. Before the worms went in, he had made damp soil layers. The empty clear jar had been set on a tray first. The tray protected the table from loose soil and water. Clear sides made the layers easy to see from outside. The leaves were food above the worms'' damp home. Soil supported the worms, while leaves rested above their new home.","The torch would not light, even with new batteries. Tao noticed that the cap was loose and the batteries were not held firmly against the metal contact. He tightened the cap. The torch immediately lit, although he had not changed the batteries or bulb again.","The town library has shelves of books for different readers. Behind the desk, visitors can also borrow simple musical instruments. A map drawer helps walkers plan routes without buying new guides. Families take puzzles home and return them when finished. Staff run small computer classes for people needing help online. A quiet room gives groups space to learn together. These services are available even to visitors who borrow no books. Last week, a new reader returned a drum and borrowed a map.","The town prepared for several days of very hot weather. Drinking water was placed where people usually gathered outside. Staff checked that shaded resting places were open and usable. Helpers visited residents who lived alone and might need support. Sports clubs moved outdoor practice into cooler morning hours. Signs reminded families where to find water and shade nearby. Different groups shared their plans so important places were covered. An afternoon match took place early, before the empty field grew hot.","The toy car stopped because a wheel fell off.","The tree had ripe fruit within Ivy’s reach. Dust covered the skin of the lowest apples. Ivy picked an apple. She washed it under the tap. Then she took a bite.","The trolley rolls over the step.","The turn list helped every child get a chance to play.","The twins drew a chalk line across their shared attic room. Each kept books and models on one side after several arguments. For a few days, neither touched anything that belonged to the other. The room was peaceful, but their half-finished model village stayed untouched. Each missed the ideas and stories they used to share. They talked about asking before borrowing instead of staying completely apart. Then they erased the line and finished one village building together. Their separate shelves remained, with a shared workspace between them.","The twins sorted paper cups after the fair. They put half in the classroom cabinet. The remaining cups were shared between two art tables. Each table needed the same number of cups. The twins checked that both stacks matched before leaving. There were twelve cups in all before the sorting began. Six were saved for classroom drinks on the next outing. The other six would hold paint for two groups of children. Each art table had three brushes ready beside its empty tray.","The uses the capital letter needed here.","The visitors entered the theatre through the side door. Signs directed them past a room full of costumes and into a hall with numbered hooks. They hung their coats on the hooks before taking their seats. Bags had to stay beneath the seats, away from the aisle.","The waiting children beside the gate.","The walking tour started at the old town''s stone castle. The guide showed the group its tall gate and towers. After the castle visit, the group walked down to the harbour. Fishing boats filled the water beside the narrow path. Next, everyone visited the little shops by the harbour wall. The shops sold postcards showing both boats and the castle. Finally, the group returned to its hotel beyond the square. The guide''s map used four numbered stops for the day. Those numbers matched the route they had followed.","The wet floor near the sunny window.","The wheel stayed straight when we tried the cart again.","The wheel wobbled each time the cart moved.","The wind blew Mai''s kite into a low branch. She could not reach its dangling string. Dad fetched a long pole and walked over. Below the branch, he raised the long pole carefully.","The wooden bat rested beside the ball.","Them","Theo came inside wearing wet gloves after an afternoon outdoors. Just before that, he had been pulling his sled uphill. He started sledding only after finishing a snowman near the gate. That snowman was possible because snow had fallen during the night. The fresh snow was deep enough for both winter activities. His gloves were wool, with no waterproof cover over them. The final place in this account is the warm house. The overnight snowfall had provided material for the later snowman.","Theo filled a low tub with warm water. He fetched dog shampoo and an old towel. His muddy dog waited patiently beside the open door. Theo called the dog over to the tub.","Theo had practised tying his shoes for days. Today both bows stayed firm all morning. He showed them to everyone at the door. At playtime, he offered to help his friend. He grinned as he pointed to the bows.","Theo knew his song well at home. Now he waited outside the audition room, rubbing his palms on his trousers. Each time a name was called, he looked up quickly and checked that his music was still in his hand. He had not yet heard anyone’s decision.","Theo wanted a place to sit in the park. A red label warned of wet paint. The paint could mark clothes touching the bench. Theo chose another bench to keep his trousers clean.","There are 1 complete sentences, separated by their ending punctuation.","There was mud on both of his hands. The soap and tap were beside the sink. Ben wet his hands. Then he rubbed soap over them. Last, he rinsed the bubbles away.","There were five chicks beside the hen. Three chicks followed her to the feeder. Two stayed near the warm lamp. The farmer put fresh water beside them. All five chicks came together again at sunset.","They can also borrow information books.","They can ask the librarian a question.","They can sit on a chair by the window.","This chocolate workshop begins with cocoa beans ready for processing. Workers first dry the beans on wide trays in sunshine. Moisture needs to leave before the next stage. Then the workers roast those dried beans using heat. Roasting develops the flavour used in the finished chocolate. Next, a machine grinds the roasted beans into a smooth paste. Finally, workers mix the paste with the recipe''s sugar and milk. Grinding makes small pieces suitable for mixing with those ingredients. The workshop diagram shows these four stages in order.","This classroom bottle already contains water and a little sand. Its lid is tight enough to prevent any leaks. First, Tessa shakes the sealed bottle until the water looks cloudy. Next, she sets it upright on the table. During the third stage, the grains settle towards the bottom. Gravity pulls the heavier sand out of the cloudy mixture. Finally, Tessa records the clear water above the settled layer. The still bottle makes that layer easier to observe. Her recording belongs after settling, not while the grains are moving.","This is how I get ready for a wet day.","This week has spaces; next week’s club is full.","This week is full; there are spaces in next week’s club.","Those large baskets and boxes","Thread string through the holes.","Three beans have sprouted; the sunflower has not yet sprouted.","Three benches in the park remembered people from the town. The newest one was for Mr. Alam. He had fed sparrows here for forty years. The carpenter added a seed dish to this bench. The other benches had flowers beside them instead. A small metal plate on each seat carried a different name. The plate on the newest seat also showed two little sparrows. Families could rest there while watching birds visit the dish. The flowers beside the older seats marked other people''s favourite plants.","Three chicks hatched from the eggs today.","Three ducks wait in line at the ice cream truck.","Three friends made signs for the garden. Ellis cut the wood and sanded its edges. Noor drew the letter outlines in pencil. Ben carefully filled the outlines with green paint. When the paint dried, Ellis fixed each sign to a stake beside the plants.","Three groups tested paper for making a shopping bag. One group used a single sheet of thin paper. Their bag tore when they added the second apple. Another group folded thick paper around a card base. That bag held all four apples without tearing. The third group used thin paper with no folded bottom. Its apples rolled out before they could carry it. All groups used the same apples and walked the same distance.","Three lost things stayed in a box on Friday. Jun took his striped scarf at morning break. Ivy took her water bottle after lunch. The only thing left was a dinosaur glove. The box stayed locked in school until Monday. No one could collect anything while the school was closed. The caretaker kept the key in the office over the weekend. A notice said the next collection time was Monday morning. Each owner would need to describe the pattern on their missing thing.","Three pets arrived at the clinic. The rabbit came before the cat, and the dog arrived last. Only the first pet needed its claws trimmed. The receptionist wrote their jobs beside the order of arrival. The second pet was there for a check of its ears. The last pet needed a bandage changed on its paw. None of those two was booked for claws. Their owners waited on separate chairs while the nurse checked the list.","Three sunflowers have sprouted; the beans have not yet sprouted.","Tie the filled string into a loop.","Tie the laces of your shoes.","Tie the string ends together.","Tilly could not join the school trip because her ankle needed rest. She smiled when her friends described the coach and packed lunches. At art time, most children drew places they hoped to visit. Tilly drew herself beside those friends on the trip''s coach. She rubbed out her figure, paused, then put it back. When the others discussed the trip again, she turned her paper over. Her teacher placed some coloured pencils beside her drawing book. Tilly chose a blue one and began colouring the wheels.","To make a clear leaf print, choose a fallen leaf. Put it flat on a firm table with veins upwards. Cover it with thin paper and hold the paper still. Rub the side of a crayon gently over the surface. The raised veins will show as lines on the paper. If the leaf moves, the pattern may blur or double. When the picture is finished, lift the paper carefully. Label the print with the tree''s name before putting it away.","Toby brought a striped towel to swimming class. He left it on the bench before swimming. After his lesson, he dried his wet hair. Then he put it in his bag. The towel fitted into the bag''s side pocket.","Today we have rice instead of soup, with fruit as usual.","Today we have soup instead of rice, with fruit as usual.","Today we have soup instead of rice, without any fruit.","Tom could not find his library book anywhere. He looked under his bed and behind the sofa. He even checked the fridge. At last he checked inside his pillowcase. The book was where he read it last.","Tom swung his bag inside and knocked Grandma''s teapot from its stand. The handle broke, and he first hoped she would not notice. Then he called her and described exactly what had happened. He offered his saved coins towards a repair instead of leaving immediately. Grandma arranged for a repairer to mend the special pot safely. Tom helped pack the pieces and carried the box beside her. Afterwards, he moved his bag hook away from the display shelf. Grandma put the repaired pot back while he hung his bag carefully.","Tom waited beside trays of warm rolls. An adult behind the counter slid a loaf into a paper bag. Through an open doorway, Tom saw someone taking another tray from a large oven. He counted coins for his turn.","Tomas spilled water over the picture he was painting. The blue sky spread down into the green field. At first he wanted to throw the picture away. Then he turned the spreading colour into a pond and added reeds. He liked his changed picture enough to put it on the wall.","Tomorrow the library closes at nine, earlier than usual.","Tomorrow the library opens at eight, as usual.","Tomorrow the library opens at nine, later than usual.","Touch the food first to check whether your hands are clean.","Travellers watched a board showing track numbers and departure times. An announcement said their train would leave from track four. They pulled their cases towards the platform, staying behind the marked line until it was time to board. A ticket inspector checked the route on their tickets.","Treats an exclamation as a calm telling sentence.","Treats an exclamation as a question.","Turn. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Twenty children needed a lift to the farm. Twelve boarded the first bus. Eight waited for the second bus with their teacher. Each bus had two adults aboard when it left, but the teachers were counted separately from the children.","Two blue curtains hung in two different windows. Sun shone on one for many years. The light slowly made its color fade. The curtain in the shade stayed dark blue.","Two blue lunchboxes had the same rocket sticker. Cal''s box had a dent in one corner. Robi''s box had a smooth corner and smelled of orange. Cal looked at the corners before taking his lunchbox. He left the smooth box on the bench for Robi. Neither box had a written name because the ink had faded. Their handles were the same size and colour too. The dent came from Cal dropping his box last week. Robi had kept his own box safely inside his school bag.","Two boys are carrying a ladder past the bakery.","Two cups in the science corner held the same soil. The cup with a red label had been watered. The cup with a blue label had stayed dry. Priya pressed a finger into each cup. The soil with the red label felt softer. Both cups stood on the same sunny shelf. Each held the same amount of soil from one bag. The labels helped her remember which treatment each cup received. She recorded the feeling before adding water to either cup.","Two groups needed the same paintbrushes at once.","Two groups were growing beans for the school display. One group hid its watering notes, hoping to have the tallest plants. The other group shared a useful way to check damp soil. When a dry weekend harmed both sets, the groups met together. They compared notes and made a shared plan for care. Most plants recovered, though neither group won a special prize. At the display, they explained what they had learned from working together. Their strongest result was knowledge everyone could use next time.","Two handles let people share the load.","Two leaves opened on the new shoot.","Two trays of the same seedlings stood beside each other. Both received the same light. The class watered one tray but missed the other during a holiday. The missed tray dried out and its plants wilted. After watering, several of those plants slowly stood upright again.","Umbrella. Listen to the first sound. Which word has the same vowel sound?","Umbrellas opened all along the street as rain began. It might look as if opening umbrellas brought the rain. But the first drops landed before any umbrella opened. People opened them to keep those drops off their clothes. One person without an umbrella sheltered beneath a shop roof. Another person waited indoors until the shower became lighter. The drops reached the street in places with no people too. The weather affected the people wherever they chose to shelter.","Uncertain and careful because he was not yet confident.","Uncle Dip burned his toast.","Under a log, the class found beetles and tiny worms. The ground there felt cooler than the sunny path. Damp leaves covered the soil and gave the creatures shelter. The teacher lifted the log just enough for everyone to look. Pupils drew what they saw without touching the small animals. Afterwards, they put the log back in its original place. This kept the dark, damp home ready for its residents. Next week they would compare it with a sunny patch.","Under the dark cloud.","Under the fallen leaves.","Under the wrapping was a box of paints.","Unfold the tent on the ground.","Until the heavy garden gate finally opened for all of us.","Us","Use any cloth with spots of any size.","Use any locker with a clock nearby.","Use the first opening you see in the fence.","Use the green cloth with large white spots.","Use the large bridge. The small bridge is closed.","Use the locker above the clock with a blue star on its door.","Use the locker below the clock with a blue star on its door.","Use the remaining reviewed short-to-long pair, then gather comparable independent evidence outside this finite bank. Do not lower the shared threshold or treat new distractor orders as fresh targets.","Use the small bridge. The large bridge is closed.","Use the white cloth with large green spots.","Use the wooden gate by the pond.","Very careful about small details of the work.","Very wet throughout.","Visit our art show on Friday evening; the pictures stay here.","Visit our art show on Friday morning; take the pictures home.","Visit our art show on Friday morning; the pictures stay here.","Visitors can see water plants growing beside the pond.","Wait at the crossing until the helper tells you to cross.","Walk outside in your coat.","Warm bread filled the air with a sweet smell. Buns sat behind the long glass counter. The worker took more fresh loaves from an oven. She put them out for customers to buy.","Wash your hands after touching food, only if they look dirty.","Wash your hands before touching food, even when they look clean.","Wash your hands with soap and water.","Water boiled inside the kettle on the stove. Its lid was firmly in place. Steam pushed through the whistle in its lid. The whistle made a loud sound. Auntie took the kettle off the heat.","Water spilled inside a picnic bag. One sandwich was wrapped in a waterproof container, while an unwrapped napkin beside it became wet. When the bag was opened, the sandwich inside the closed container was dry. The bag itself had not kept the water out.","Water the soil gently; leave the seeds where they are.","Waves washed up near Zoe''s large sandcastle. Gulls called overhead as Dad rubbed on sunscreen. Zoe collected shells beside the salty sea water. Each wave left a wet line across the sand.","We added signs showing safe routes near the pond.","We agreed on a turn for each group.","We also grow sweet strawberries.","We also hang a swing from the tree.","We also keep a rake in the shed.","We baked bread.","We began laying out food for the picnic.","We began looking for the first corner piece.","We carried our food into a shelter.","We counted the puddles on the path.","We folded ridges into a new piece of paper.","We found a loose nut and tightened it.","We found the glove as soon as we first entered the field.","We had finished the puzzle together.","We have a new puppy at home.","We have left the farm and are travelling back to school.","We have reached school after finishing our farm visit.","We have reached the farm and will begin our visit now.","We have routines for keeping our tools safe and easy to find.","We heard the ring of the doorbell.","We keep new paper in a cupboard beside the door.","We made changes to help visitors stay safe beside the pond.","We moved it into an open space near a tree.","We never reached the centre after changing our route.","We packed a bag for school.","We painted the shed green last week.","We picked the ripe pods into a bowl.","We plan to buy eggs tomorrow.","We plant beans in spring.","We prepared for our walk.","We pulled back the thick curtains.","We put a bean in damp soil.","We put every tool back in its marked space.","We put the full tray in the freezer.","We put the game back in its box.","We sat on the blanket to eat our picnic.","We spread a blanket on the grass.","We started looking for the corner pieces.","We stepped off the bus onto the pavement.","We still needed to ask for seeds for every box.","We stood under a wide shelter.","We stopped looking because the glove had no bright lining.","We studied a map to choose the route for our walk.","We used what went wrong to improve how we watered.","We waited for the bell’s final ring.","We waited until the gate opened.","We wanted visitors to understand our class models. Each group wrote a short label explaining its model. We put related models together and drew arrows showing a route around the room. During the exhibition, we answered questions and noticed which labels needed clearer words.","We watched drops land on the window.","We waved as the bus drove away.","We were about to choose bread for lunch.","We were ready to choose a puzzle.","We worked a few drops of water into it.","We write on both sides before taking a new sheet.","Wet boots crowded the school drying room after each rainy walk. Children hurried away, leaving several pairs lying with openings pressed together. Ede noticed that those boots were often still damp after lunch. She began standing the pairs apart on the shared rack. She also showed younger children where to leave space around each pair. By afternoon, more children could put on comfortable dry boots. Nobody had asked Ede to earn points or win a prize. She kept a gap on the rack for the last arriving pair.","Wet the clean brush.","Whale. Which word has the same starting sound?","What did you eat at your picnic?","When the child found the shell.","When the class reached the campsite, their tents were already standing. The older pupils had arrived early to put them up. Sleeping bags still lay rolled beside the entrance to each tent. After choosing places, the children unrolled their bags inside. Soon the cook rang a bell beside the outdoor shelter. Everyone left their bags and carried a bowl to supper. By the time the stars appeared, the bowls were washed. A lantern glowed inside every tent before bedtime that night.","When will your first tooth fall out?","When wind scattered a stallholder’s paper bags, Arun stopped to gather them. He was only passing through the market. Weeks later, Arun lost a small parcel there. The stallholder recognized him, helped search and found it under a bench. Arun thanked her for taking the time.","Where did you put my ruler?","Where does this river begin?","Which letters complete __l?","Which letters complete b__st?","Which letters complete b__t?","Which letters complete bl__?","Which letters complete cr__?","Which letters complete d__t?","Which letters complete f__l?","Which letters complete f__m?","Which letters complete f__n?","Which letters complete f__st?","Which letters complete fl__t?","Which letters complete g__l?","Which letters complete g__m?","Which letters complete gr__n?","Which letters complete h__t?","Which letters complete m__n?","Which letters complete n__?","Which letters complete p__nt?","Which letters complete p__se?","Which letters complete s__ve?","Which letters complete st__n?","Which letters complete str__?","Which letters complete th__d?","Which letters complete v__b?","Which vowel completes tr_p?","Which vowel pattern completes c_n_?","Which vowel pattern completes r_p_?","While I held the ladder, Dad climbed up.","While I held the tall wooden ladder against the garden wall.","While the dog beside the gate barked.","Whole existing Leda recording preserves the authored word; no splice or new synthesis.","Will it snow at your home tonight?","Winter days can be shorter than summer days.","Wolfs","Woods","Words by","Workers keep the water in the pool clean.","Worms help improve the soil in two ways.","Worms loosen soil as they move through it.","Would you like to borrow my eraser?","Would you like to fly my kite with me?","Would you like to make a kite with me?","Would you like to paint with water?","Would you like to plant these seeds together?","Would you like to play at my house?","Wren shook dry bean pods over a tray. Small stones that had been on the tray fell into a cup. Wren picked up the beans and placed them in paper envelopes for next spring. She left the empty pods in a basket to carry to the compost heap.","Wren was afraid to sing for the choir. Ana practised quietly beside her each week. Wren slowly began singing with a clearer voice. At the concert, they stood together and sang confidently.","Write the news on the card.","Write your message on the card.","Written and illustrated by","Yara carried a camera to the pond in a shoulder bag. To collect a water sample, she took the camera out and set it on a dry bench. She used both hands to fill the jar. Her friend waited on the bridge with the bag. Yara collected the camera before leaving.","You","Your camera is safe in the office; please collect it there.","Your camera is still missing; please ask in the office.","Yusuf thought his loud trumpet would be the concert''s most impressive sound. He told Amal that nobody would notice her quiet harp tune. Both children had practised carefully and could play their music well. During Amal''s piece, the audience stopped rustling and leaned forward. After the last gentle note, they stayed silent before applauding warmly. Yusuf''s cheerful piece received warm applause too. Afterwards, he asked Amal to show him her softest passage again. This time, he listened without trying to play over it.","Zack tipped a seed packet too quickly. Seeds fell onto the path beside the flowers. Pigeons saw the seeds and flew down. They pecked at the food on the path.","Zara was getting ready for a woodland walk. Her shoes had laces instead of straps. Zara pulled on a sock. Then she put on its shoe. Last, she tied the laces.","[\"long_vowels\",\"phonics\",\"identify_read_split_vowel_sound:u_e\",\"recognition||||visible\"]","___ birds cannot fly.","___ can hop and run.","___ cat sat on the mat.","___ close the lid.","___ dog can dig.","___ drifted away.","___ hen is in the pen.","___ lay together.","___ lunch we rest.","___ passed slowly.","___ played together.","___ ship sails on the sea.","___ shook wildly.","___ the oven so it is hot before baking.","___ went to the park.","___ were carried inside.","___ you find my sock?","a basket holding fruit","a beetle","a book to read","a building on a farm","a car being driven","a climbing vine","a covering stretched overhead","a current of cool air","a deep crack in rock","a dry towel","a fallen branch","a fantasy story","a feeling of hunger","a finished printed book","a first version of writing","a floor for dancing","a happy mood","a large and special meal","a little cold","a little wet","a loose pile of stones","a lot of pain","a machine that cleans streets","a make-believe story","a mess of unwanted objects","a narrow pipe","a narro