r completing both visible pages.","738576f9df3159dc172f45c6e9186a7d90d09d59e0d8bf425b8292b04af15a95","7419e2bb69775f41720cfed7f63ea901b852f452ca224edd5835a0362cac33a9","750d480ffe77e5c852132b3a7d54826d693a49acdd7999b928382ec4a2741cfe","7c1ee0a738d9798e85742f581cb2434a8f6080132feffc252f1bb77a3b9740dc","8 identifies a different printed role or position. Page 10 is the required place to continue. Use a printed part reference instead of detouring through the intervening activity pages.","8 identifies a different printed role or position. Page 12 is the required place to continue. Follow an explicit continuation reference instead of automatically choosing the next number.","810679aaa5fd8b24a0ba341afcce220f399e8ffe7cb447eae5a6945b83ac0bc2","838d55f0a1e6106cf4b60ec82b638d01147216d3859e8bddc173af80ecf720c6","85030d2af799280c54a490c77607e55432057508101a03277f32c11d05e5dc17","8618f81fa04af708e552c99cf2a0759c8747f063ceebf0dcbeac2366b90b753a","86c407b77ea2b5d1f6623ec537cf9477e8c326821b222bbab9e9dacf5c6fe1fb","8bc414a5f6531e127880060675f88243ac3eadd808928cfd888621d1d10b2f4c","8c060954ac10a93a8885a1f4da862e9bb4ca2512c1328948c2c90171b5cff7df","8c4a2a711cc909fa396033e9e35f9a139f8d6559eddbf84cd33331d625bf3fa1","8c5a0d86966251452c737415529261d573afbc14380c46ca0f2a5919a45e7037","8ef41fb0d6d8d13dc3bcd112c2e73e3fff90f79e0ee4d151effcc09d45c73aa3","9 identifies a different printed role or position. Page 10 is the required place to continue. Use a printed part reference instead of detouring through the intervening activity pages.","9 identifies a different printed role or position. Page 12 is the required place to continue. Follow an explicit continuation reference instead of automatically choosing the next number.","90b3804a71fae1988bbd04ca90dff8ef33a54f621f6b2a254b47d5185fdffed7","912aff5725c5cd6fef0dfa5148af88dc69fdfae7f4f5252d89301077ff1ea215","9230d51dcbcece42cb832f1b7c877a8d4ef7151e5c137468841c24da56af1174","92d656093be31c44d1fbe7668c080bd9412e9dd966319f0b42c5e5ffebac3f68","97cb6ccadd77030ec6af6e0cd4e206a7e8ea24d7ae9641ea31345e34fdac25ae","9804b1fc1432f26f410103c0327480fc735f1d2c09ab6fac49df39e7fcf79a5b","996cb2cd679d7fc7ce34539d6a96deb5c66a1cdb7f8031d4f19d57b90bde76a6","99b0701c4e3944b9b2acc5ca086ad2e3ae8e8d27b22141ecccdee70668d758fc","9a2370331e75d2a71a47d63864436b06a3f0772d52936dea10e562683321c8a4","9f2071f47c4510cf31308bf35bc0d0e4818ecd4074e3f1406120a8150af80de6","9fd7460a64502ebaa0d8a7c88c26fbed87838c3eb0eba26f6de98249e688338a","A Basket of Rhymes","A Basket of Rhymes\nPoems by Nell Dove\nIllustrations by Otis Ray\nBook design by Hana Green","A Fox for Faye","A Fox for Faye\nPictures by Omar Lane\nFor Grandad, with love.","A Gift for Gran","A Gift for Gran\nWritten by Esme Ray\nFor Toby, who loves surprises.","A Home for Every Bird","A Home for Every Bird\nWritten and illustrated by Sofia Lake\nMaps by Arlo Reed\nFor bird lovers","A Pocket of Sand identifies a different printed role or position. Mila Snow is the writer identified by this cover.","A Pocket of Sand\nWords by Mila Snow\nDrawings by Noah Reed","A Rainy Walk\nWritten by Mina Fox\nPictures by Leo Sun","A Red Kite\nWritten by Ali Moss\nPictures by Jo Bell","A River Journey","A River Journey\nby Sol West\nMaps by Lily Brook\nIllustrated by Nico Hill","A Trail of Shells identifies a different printed role or position. Amir Hill is the picture maker identified by this cover.","A Trail of Shells\nWords by Ida Fox\nDrawings by Amir Hill","A Turtle’s Body","A Turtle’s Home","A Turtle’s Shell","A ___ breathes air through its blowhole.","A ___ buzzed by my ear.","A ___ carried its spiral shell across the leaf.","A ___ crept past the hens.","A ___ flew home to the hive.","A ___ gave us warm wool.","A ___ guided juice into the narrow bottle opening.","A ___ lid keeps the water in.","A ___ morning is best for kites.","A ___ rolled off the shelf.","A ___ showed how warm the water was.","A ___ swam with a tail before growing frog legs.","A ___ untangles my hair.","A bakery makes and sells bread; all choices name places.","A barn owl often hunts after dark. Its soft feathers help it fly quietly. It listens carefully for small animals moving below.","A bat found shelter in a narrow roof space.: Storing it with a ball and gloves identifies the sports bat; shelter and colony concern the animal.","A bat lay across the team’s kit bag.: Catching a moth as food identifies the animal; the other sentences describe equipment.","A bat struck the ball over the fence.: Catching a moth as food identifies the animal; the other sentences describe equipment.","A bear driving a bus is make-believe.","A bear wore a coat. It bought a ticket and drove a bus.","A bee gathers nectar; be is a verb and bean names a plant seed.","A bee lives in a hive; be and bead do not name the flying insect.","A beetle can crawl beneath branches.","A beginning about how to play introduces the rules.","A bell and speaker serve the same described problem, not an instrument list.","A bell announcing a time produces a sound; the other rings are worn objects.","A bell can make a loud ringing sound.","A bell rang above a door.","A bell rang above us.","A bell warns us that break is over. A sign tells us where to wait. A map shows us the route.","A belongs to the visiting group; B describes the group.","A belongs to the winning team; B tells about the team.","A bicycle has ___ wheels.","A bicycle has two wheels and pedals.","A bicycle has two wheels. Listen. Pick the missing word.","A bicycle leaned against a wheelbarrow. It had a flat tire. The wheelbarrow’s tire was firm, but the bicycle’s rear rim touched the ground.","A bicycle leaned against a wheelbarrow. The bicycle had a flat tire.","A bicycle leaned against a wheelbarrow. The wheelbarrow had a flat tire.","A bicycle leaned against a wheelbarrow. They both had flat tires.","A bike has ___ wheels.","A biography can supply the author’s birth date.","A bird may fly from branch to branch. It can also hop along the ground.","A bitter taste lingered from my drink. The bench was smooth and cold. An announcement crackled through the station speakers before the train arrived.","A boat rested in the cove. Gentle ripples rocked it softly while a warm breeze moved the sail. A rope tied it to a wooden post.","A bowl can sit on a table.","A breeze crossed the empty garden. The leaves danced along the path, turning and lifting before settling again.","A broken drawer was repaired to hold our tools.","A broken tool has no use. It is ___.","A broken tool has no use. It is ….","A broom cannot wake and choose to sweep on its own.","A brush","A buddy could help them learn where things belong.","A buddy system would help new pupils. Someone could show them the rooms. They would have a partner to join at playtime.","A buddy would help new pupils find their way and feel included.","A bus is bigger ___ a car.","A bus’s colour does not show temperature.","A buzzing ___ gathered pollen from the flower.","A buzzing ___ landed on the flower.","A calendar shows the days of each month.","A castle shrank until it fitted inside a teacup. The king stared from its stone window.","A cat and a bed.","A cat steps lightly down the stair,\nAnd leaves no footfall hanging there.\nIts shadow slips across the mat,\nA silent twin beside the cat.","A caterpillar changed into a chrysalis. Later, a butterfly came out. It waited, then flew away on dry wings. What happened last?","A chair stood in the room.","A child in a play needs to know when to enter the stage.","A child sailed to the moon in a paper boat. From the window, Dad watched the boat rise.","A child untied the rainbow and wore it as a ribbon. Wherever she walked, colours dripped onto the path.","A class moved the same load along the same flat path. Without wheels, two children struggled to drag it. On a wheeled cart, one child moved it steadily. They used a blue rope in both trials.","A class plant would help us learn and share jobs.","A clean feeder helps prevent food from becoming dirty.","A clock can strike the hour.","A clock could hang in a quiet café too.","A clock face has no watching eyes.","A clock hung on the wall.","A cloud cannot be picked up and kept in a pocket.","A cloud hid the ___, making the playground darker.","A cloud hid the …, making the playground darker. Which spelling fits?","A coin dropped into the empty tin. Plink! It landed at the bottom.","A colour does not show reuse.","A comb’s projecting points are teeth; tooth is singular and mouths are not comb parts.","A comma follows the greeting in a friendly letter.","A commotion began in the yard during the quiet reading lesson. Bangs, voices and loud squawks mixed into a sudden uproar. Children turned towards the windows while a teacher went to investigate. A delivery box had fallen beside a group of startled geese. The birds flapped around as two adults moved the scattered boxes. One person called directions over the noise of the others. After the geese settled and the boxes were stacked, calm returned. The children could hear their teacher reading softly once more.","A comparison does not say it is literally made of feathers.","A compass indicates direction; the other instruments measure temperature or time.","A compass shows the ___.","A completion date does not show who can use the path.","A compost bin is something our class should make.","A covered space offers protection from rain.","A crow ignored the scarecrow until the farmer clapped and the dog barked.","A crow picked up a nut. It dropped the nut onto a hard path. Then it ate from the broken shell. What happened last?","A cupboard near the door holds all the equipment.","A current route timetable gives departure times.","A cyclist lent a helmet to a skater. She put it on.","A cyclist lent a helmet to a skater. She wore it. The skater fastened the helmet strap under her own chin.","A cyclist lent a helmet to a skater. The cyclist wore the helmet.","A cyclist lent a helmet to a skater. The skater wore the helmet.","A damp cloth gathered the crumbs; brushing was rejected and blowing was not stated.","A delivery driver needs to know where to leave a parcel after the office closes.","A described washing event is not a set of rules.","A describes Evan; B tells its own cooking experience.","A describes Lily’s memory; B shares its own memory.","A describes Luca; B is inside the tent telling its own experience.","A describes Zed speaking; B tells its own attempt.","A describes a few early shoppers; B describes crowds later at the same market.","A describes the sailors; B is one of the people hauling the boat.","A dictionary defines the word.","A display board has space for many sheets of paper.","A distant ring echoed from the church bell.: Fit around a knuckle identifies jewelry; alarms and bells make sounds.","A dog is ___ after its ball right now.","A dog is … after its ball right now.","A draft, or first version of a piece of writing, lay on Mina’s desk. She crossed out one sentence and added a better ending. Her final copy would come later. The open window moved the paper, but did not change the words.","A dragon knitting smoke for a cloud makes an imagined narrative.","A drawing can fade in strong sunlight. Damp can damage its paper, and rough handling can tear an edge. A protective frame, a dry place and careful handling reduce these problems. Keeping artwork safe involves more than choosing where it looks attractive.","A drink spilled over a book with a wipe-clean cover. The cover became wet, but the pages beneath stayed dry. A matching uncovered booklet beside it had wet pages. Both books had pictures on their first pages.","A drop of ___ loosened the wheel.","A drop of oil loosened the wheel. Listen. Pick the missing word.","A drummer names the person making music; loud and quickly describe sound or manner.","A duck dived into the pond. Splash! Water flew over the stones.","A duck rests by the pond!","A duck rests by the pond.","A duck rests by the pond?","A duck rests by the pond___","A duck spreads oil over its feathers. Rain runs off the oily surface. Dry feathers trap warm air next to its body.","A duck swims quietly.","A faint light glowed at the far end of the tunnel.","A fallen tree blocked the road to school.","A feather drifts beside the wall,\nIt turns and twirls before its fall.\nThe bird has gone beyond my view,\nIts tiny gift still holds the blue.","A finger-worn ring can be lost in grass; timers and doorbells produce sounds.","A fish uses gills to take oxygen from water. Its fins help it move and keep its balance.","A fish wrote a letter to the sea. It tucked the paper under a rock and swam away.","A flat painting cannot become a working doorway by drying.","A flock ___ birds flew overhead.","A flock of ___ honked beside the pond.","A flock of birds flew overhead. Listen. Pick the missing word.","A fox found a magic key. It opened a door in the moon.","A frog jumps.","A frozen puddle adds another detail about the cold weather.","A funnel channels liquid into a narrow neck; a sieve strains and a tray supports objects.","A garden helps insects and lets us observe.","A gardener hid all the plants under a white sheet.","A gardener’s story identifies a different printed role or position. Hazel Day is the writer identified by this cover.","A giant moving hills by sneezing is an imagined event.","A giant sneezed so hard that all the hills rolled into the sea. He spent the afternoon fishing them out.","A giraffe opened a bakery on the moon. Each night it sent warm buns down on silver ribbons.","A girl held the gate while her puppy ran through.","A guide raised her green flag. “Follow me,” she called. The walkers crossed the bridge behind her.","A guide to pond animals does not satisfy the complete evidence. Small Pond, Big World is the title. The guide line describes its topic and the studio is credited for art.","A gust caught the door. Bang! The handle shook against the wall.","A gust tugged at the paper map. Leo clutched it, pressing his fingers tightly around both edges. Even when the wind blew harder, he did not let it slip away.","A harbor provides protected mooring for boats; a pond or waterfall does not fit the marine refuge.","A harbour shelters boats; the alternatives serve other kinds of transport.","A hard shell covers the head.","A hat cannot magically cause sweets to rain.","A hat is another item of clothing serving the same topic.","A hedgehog curls up with exposed spines; the other animals have different protection.","A hedgehog searches for food mainly after dark. Its strong sense of smell helps it find small animals among leaves. When threatened, it can curl into a ball so its spines point outwards.","A helmet helps protect a cyclist. Its hard shell covers the head. Its soft lining cushions a bump. Some helmets have blue stripes.","A hidden palace and bubble queen create make-believe events.","A hinge is the turning joint; a handle and a lock do not form that joint.","A hoped-for event not happening brings disappointment; the alternatives imply welcome news.","A howl echoed through the cave. A low growl came closer.","A includes the storyteller; B describes the children.","A is inside the tent telling its own experience; B describes Luca.","A is one of the sailors; B watches them from outside.","A journey through caves identifies a different printed role or position. The Hidden Harbour is the title identified by this cover.","A kangaroo carries its young in a pouch; the other mammals do not.","A key turned very slowly in the lock. We held our breath as footsteps stopped outside. The clock above the shelf showed half past five.","A knot held the rope tightly. I pulled it in short, sharp movements; I ___ it.","A knot held the rope tightly. I pulled it in short, sharp movements; I … it.","A label gave a date.","A ladder leaned against the trunk below the lowest branches.","A ladder leaned against the trunk below the lowest branches.\nGlossary\ntrunk: an elephant’s long nose\ntrunk: the thick main stem of a tree\nroot: a part of a plant usually beneath the soil","A large bird launched from a ledge above the valley. It soared, sailing high on a rising current of warm air. Its broad wings stayed spread instead of beating quickly each moment. The walkers watched it climb until the ledge looked far below. Sometimes it turned in a wide circle without losing much height. A second bird skimmed close to the ground near the stream. That low flight looked quite different from the first bird''s movement. Soon the high bird was a small shape against the clouds.","A large mirror had been put across the lake.","A leaf fell on my head!","A leaf fell on my head.","A leaf fell on my head?","A leaf fell on my head___","A librarian lends and records books; an author writes them.","A little crab hid beneath the rock.","A long ring came from the old telephone.: Silver and a gift box identify jewelry; bells and telephones identify sounds.","A loud note can be heard.","A low branch blocked the way above its tall roof.","A magnifier enlarges apparent size; a mirror reflects and an ordinary window does not enlarge.","A magpie took the shiny bottle top from outside.","A map hung by the door.","A map’s position does not establish quiet behaviour.","A matches a. M matches m. T matches t.","A message appeared ___ the screen when I tapped it.","A message appeared on the screen when I tapped it. Build the missing word.","A moon speaking to a person is fantasy; moonrise and moving reflections can occur.","A moth flew past me.","A mother with a wing and baby is an animal; handles and match equipment are the other sense.","A mountain cannot decide where to live or walk on feet.","A mouse found a piece of cheese beside the garden wall. It nibbled one corner, taking a series of tiny bites. Each bite left a small mark rather than removing the whole piece. The mouse paused between bites to look towards the nearby hedge. A dog watched from behind the gate but could not reach 