ed initial capital for places.","On Sundays, Dad liked a slow walk beside the river. He stopped often to look at birds or talk with neighbours. His pace on school mornings was brisk instead. Ida needed several quick steps to match each of his strides. They still used the same smooth path beside the river. Dad did not stumble or walk quietly on his toes. He moved with purpose, reaching the gate before the bell. On the return journey, he slowed down and watched the ducks again.","On ___ we read a space book.","On a cold walk, one child wore mittens while another carried the same kind in a pocket. The child wearing mittens said her hands stayed warm. The other’s hands became cold until he put his mittens on. Both children wore blue coats.","On and the do not have matching spoken endings.","On her way home, Alia found a purse beside the bus stop. She took it to the shop, where a worried customer was searching under a chair. The customer recognized the purse and thanked Alia.","On market days, stalls usually filled several narrow streets near the square. This week, all the sellers gathered in the square instead. The side streets looked bare beside that busy collection of tables. There were no baskets, hanging lanterns or waiting customers along them. Their stone surfaces were still dusty from the previous dry day. Nobody had washed or repainted the buildings during the move. Children could see the walls normally hidden behind the stalls. They walked through the empty space to reach the crowded square.","On my next birthday, my age will ___ seven.","On one foot, I held my arms out to ___.","On one foot, I held my arms out to …. Which word fits?","On the map, this dot means “You are ___.”","On the map, this dot means “You are ….” Which spelling fits?","On the path we noticed ___ enormous snail moving slowly.","On the path we noticed an enormous snail moving slowly. Build the missing word.","On. Which letter matches the first sound?","Once the children had finished, ___ tid