___ the doghouse.","The dog walked past a pond.","The dog wore a soft collar. Its metal tags jingled.","The dogs play outside.","The dogs run home.","The dogs\nrun across the field.","The door creaked. A shadow slid across the room. A clock ticked steadily beside an open book.","The door does not literally have feelings.","The door felt unhappy about being opened.","The door had a brass handle.","The door made a long, low creaking sound.","The door swung on a ___ fixed to the frame.","The door swung on a … fixed to the frame. Which word fits?","The door was delivered on a large truck.","The doorbell rang we opened the door","The doorbell rang we. opened the door.","The doorbell rang. We opened the door.","The doorbell waking a dog supplies auditory evidence; diamonds and a box describe jewelry.","The doorbell’s ring sent the puppy running.: A finger-worn ring can be lost in grass; timers and doorbells produce sounds.","The drama class practised speaking across the large school hall. Ade spoke clearly, sending each word towards the children at the back. Bola began to mutter when her turn arrived. Her low, unclear words barely reached the person standing beside her. The teacher could not tell whether she was repeating the right sentence. She asked Bola to lift her head and speak more openly. This time, the words reached the other end of the room. The script itself had not changed between Bola''s two attempts.","The driver answers by nodding.","The driver identifies herself as Mrs Bell.","The driver rang the bell before the passenger lifted her suitcase from the rack.","The driver said these words: Please take a seat.","The driver said, “Please take a seat.”","The driver wore a clean blue shirt.","The driver “said, Please take a seat.”","The drum comparison conveys a strongly felt repeated beat.","The dry biscuit broke into many loose bits; it was ___.","The dry biscuit broke into many loose bits; it was …. Which word fits?","The dry food is evidence that the shelter protected it.","The dry shells were stored in the tin; the wet shoes were outside.","The dry twig snapped with a light touch because it was ___.","The duck is ___ the pond.","The ducks ___ muddy in the rain.","The ducks are swimming across the pond.","The ducks quacked when ___ saw us.","The ducks quacked when they saw us. Listen. Pick the missing word.","The ducks swim in the pond.","The ears felt very smooth and soft.","The ears were sewn from cloth.","The edge pattern identifies the towel.","The egg fell. ___ cracked on the floor.","The elves? ___ hid well.","The empty basket felt as light as a feather when Ana lifted it from the shelf.","The empty basket was light enough for the small child to carry.","The empty basket was light enough for the small child to carry.\nGlossary\nlight: not heavy\nlight: brightness that lets you see\ndark: having little brightness","The empty bottle rolled away.","The empty box had ___ toys in it.","The empty flowerpot\nstood near the gate.","The end of the two strings ___ tied to a hook.","The end of the two strings am tied to a hook.","The end of the two strings are tied to a hook.","The end of the two strings is tied to a hook.","The ending brings together both reasons for the proposed garden.","The ending brings together the paragraph’s two reasons.","The ending captures the adjustment and improved outcome.","The ending combines practical guidance with inclusion.","The ending combines the benefits developed in the paragraph.","The ending connects the route choice with the successful arrival.","The ending connects the targeted repair with the successful retest.","The ending covers the two groups that benefit.","The ending draws together participation and experimenting with rhythm.","The ending follows packing up and removing the rubbish.","The ending follows the successful measured adjustment.","The ending reflects both finishing food and washing up.","The ending reflects the access improvement and observed visits.","The ending reflects the change that made the search succeed.","The ending reflects why the new cue worked.","The ending returns to feedback and seeing development.","The ending returns to sharing and discovering books.","The ending shows the result of finding the missing shoe.","The ending stays /a/ /t/. Replacing /k/ with /h/ makes hat.","The ending summarizes both learning opportunities.","The ending summarizes both reasons for repairing.","The ending summarizes the result of making and using the list.","The entrance hall has six tall windows.","The entry labelled beak defines it as a bird’s hard mouth.","The entry labelled cub defines it as a young bear or lion.","The entry labelled dawn defines it as the time when daylight first appears.","The entry labelled helmet defines it as a hard covering that protects the head.","The entry labelled island defines it as land surrounded by water.","The entry labelled path defines it as a track used for walking.","The entry labelled root defines it as the part of a plant growing down into soil.","The entry labelled tool defines it as an object used to do a job.","The envelope is their storage place.","The evening show began with quiet music beside the dark lake. Then a dazzling burst of white fireworks spread above the water. Its brilliant light made the far bank visible for a moment. People blinked and shaded their eyes before looking up again. The coloured bursts afterwards were beautiful but much less bright. Even the lamps along the path seemed dull during the display. Sam could hear the bangs, but he kept watching the light. He described its brightness when drawing the scene the next morning.","The events connect in this order: A seed lay in the soft soil. A little shoot grew out of the seed. Two leaves opened on the new shoot.","The events connect in this order: I poured water into an empty tray. We put the full tray in the freezer. Later, we took ice out of the tray.","The events connect in this order: Mum handed me a wrapped box. I pulled off the wrapping. Under the wrapping was a box of paints.","The events connect in this order: Our paper roof sagged under the first coin. We folded ridges into a new piece of paper. The ridged roof held several coins without sagging.","The events connect in this order: The ball rolled into a puddle. I lifted the wet ball out. I dried the ball with a towel.","The events connect in this order: The bird feeder stayed empty of visitors behind the fence. We moved it into an open space near a tree. Birds began landing where they could reach the food.","The events connect in this order: The bus stopped at our stop. We stepped off the bus onto the pavement. We waved as the bus drove away.","The events connect in this order: The clay felt too dry to shape. We worked a few drops of water into it. The softened clay could be formed into a bowl.","The events connect in this order: The room grew too dark for reading. We pulled back the thick curtains. Sunlight reached the books on our table.","The events connect in this order: The wheel wobbled each time the cart moved. We found a loose nut and tightened it. The wheel stayed straight when we tried the cart again.","The events connect in this order: Two groups needed the same paintbrushes at once. We agreed on a turn for each group. Both groups finished their pictures without arguing.","The events connect in this order: We spread a blanket on the grass. We sat on the blanket to eat our picnic. After eating, we packed the blanket away.","The facts describe a seed’s parts and contents.","The fading smile and putting away the plan show disappointment at the result.","The fallen apple\nrolled down the hill.","The fallen boxes caused a jam in the narrow passage.","The fallen boxes caused a jam in the narrow passage.\nGlossary\njam: a sweet fruit spread\njam: a blockage that stops movement\nlid: a cover on a container","The familiar slowness of a snail describes the cart’s speed.","The family has changed how it uses water. They fixed the dripping bathroom tap. Their bathroom has yellow tiles. They collect rain in a barrel for watering plants. Their garden path curves around an apple tree.","The farmer inspected a fragile shell left beside the henhouse. It was delicate and could break under even gentle pressure. She placed it in a shallow dish lined with cotton. The children looked closely without passing it from hand to hand. A small opening showed where the chick had pushed through. They drew the shell and marked the opening on their pictures. Afterwards, the teacher carried the dish back to the classroom. Everyone moved slowly to keep their find in one piece.","The farmer is explicitly the person who clapped; the dog made a different sound.","The father held his baby ___ gently.","The father held his baby … gently. Which spelling fits?","The feeder hangs from a branch beside the path.","The feeder hangs high above the ground, beyond a cat’s reach. Its roof is painted green, and a clear side shows how much seed remains. The owner fills it in the morning while wearing red gloves.","The feet’s pushing surface explains their function in swimming.","The fence runs ___ the whole garden.","The film was ___ a lost kitten.","The film was about a lost kitten. Listen. Pick the missing word.","The final chapter is the funniest.","The final instruction has one named speaker.","The final piece completes the picture.","The final warning and closing doors signal that time is running out.","The firm wheelbarrow tire rules out that referent; the bicycle’s low rim identifies its flat tire.","The first choice contains a complete main clause after the time clause.","The first clause gives the children’s time; the second gives the teacher’s different time.","The first description keeps the parts, colours and absent handle correct.","The first direction keeps the route and closure information correct.","The first ending brings together the trial process and its successful result.","The first ending closes the completed activity.","The first ending sums up the joint effort and the completed result.","The first full stop follows fell, so fell ends the first sentence.","The first message keeps the object, colour and fact that it is missing.","The first message preserves the opening time and the direction of the change.","The first message reports only the change that happened.","The first message thanks Amir for the loan and gives a return time.","The first opening covers both jobs; the other choices cover one detail or only the setting.","The first opening includes maintenance and safety; the cleaning sentence covers only one part.","The first request asks for a loan with the required return time.","The first request asks for the specified help.","The first request keeps both the time and the indoor condition.","The first sentence introduces the activity described next.","The first sentence reports the new fish as a fact.","The first sentence-ending mark follows broke; later words belong to the next sentence.","The first sentence-ending mark follows glove; later words belong to the next sentence.","The first sentence-ending mark follows here; later words belong to the next sentence.","The first sentence-ending mark follows out; later words belong to the next sentence.","The first sentence-ending mark follows puddle; later words belong to the next sentence.","The first sentence-ending mark follows slowly; later words belong to the next sentence.","The first sentence-ending mark follows tower; later words belong to the next sentence.","The first sentence-ending mark follows twice; later words belong to the next sentence.","The first sentence-ending mark follows window; later words belong to the next sentence.","The first sign satisfies both closing and not locking.","The first two sounds stay the same; the new final /t/ makes mat.","The first-person singular pronoun I uses am.","The first/next/then/finally actions form an ordered procedure.","The fish flashed with a gray, metal-like shine; it looked ___.","The fish flashed with a gray, metal-like shine; it looked …. Which word fits?","The fish swam away.","The fish swims ___ the tank.","The fish wrote a letter.","The flapping washing shows moving air.","The flippers provide the movement used for swimming; the other facts describe unrelated traits.","The flock had twelve ___, all growing thick wool.","The flock held twenty woolly ___.","The flour is kept in a blue cupboard.","The flower comparison expresses personal taste.","The flower heads bent and lifted in the breeze.","The flowers agreed with something spoken.","The flowers decorate the site but do not show how easily letters can be read.","The flute is nicer than the trumpet.","The food under the roof stayed dry throughout the shower.","The footpath became slippery after rain.","The fountain’s material does not establish mood.","The fox asked to be elected mayor.","The fox brushed snow from its fur. It followed tracks to a cabin, knocked politely, and asked the rabbit to vote for it as mayor.","The fox brushed snow from its fur. does not satisfy the complete evidence. Speaking about an election assigns a human social action to the animal.","The fox brushed snow from its fur.","The fox followed tracks through snow. does not satisfy the complete evidence. Speaking about an election assigns a human social action to the animal.","The fox followed tracks through snow.","The fox is described as it, not as a first-person narrator.","The frame’s material is neutral.","The friend is Max’s listener, not the person making the request.","The friend shared rather than wrote the book, and the message gives appreciation.","The friend’s helpful action does not itself show Suri’s feeling.","The frog ___ over the log in one big spring.","The frog hid under the largest pond leaf.","The frog leapt ___ the pond.","The frog leapt into the pond. Listen. Pick the missing word.","The frogs hop ___ the pond.","The frog’s tail-bearing early stage is a tadpole; the other names are different animals.","The from phrase identifies Tess as the lender; Max owned the ruler.","The future benefit supports caring for the tree.","The gaps stay covered when rain falls.","The garden gate faces the side of the school.","The garden has a gate. is a detail, but it does not explain the stated main idea.","The garden has a gate.","The garden is empty, and the breeze supplies the movement.","The garden opens to visitors every june.","The garden path is twenty metres long. Our neighbour calls it the prettiest path in town. I prefer its flowers to the ones by the gate.","The garden slept beneath the snow,\nOne window shone with golden glow.","The garden swing goes up so high,\nMy shoes point briefly at the sky.\nThen down I swoop towards the ground,\nAnd hear the leaves make whispered sound.","The garden was dark. An owl hooted beyond the wall.","The gardener called the soil moist. It felt slightly wet between her fingers. It was neither dusty nor full of water. She said the seeds would grow well there. We gently covered each seed and labelled the row.","The gardener noticed deep cracks in the oak’s bark.","The gardener noticed deep cracks in the oak’s bark.\nGlossary\nbark: the rough outer covering of a tree\nbark: the short loud sound a dog makes\nbranch: a part growing out from a tree trunk","The gardener picked a ___ with five pink petals.","The gate ___ locked whenever the playground closes.","The gate blocks our way. Opening it ___ our way.","The gate had been locked. Dad turned the key and it could open.","The gate has ___ open all day.","The gate is locked whenever the playground closes. Build the missing word.","The gate is red. is a detail, but it does not explain the stated main idea.","The gate is red.","The gate swung open ___ the pony trotted out.","The gate swung open and the pony trotted out. Build the missing word.","The gate was painted green.","The gate’s paint colour does not describe an animal home.","The general opening covers both methods of reducing waste.","The gift came ___ Gramps.","The girl opened her book. She read a page. Then she put a bookmark inside. What happened in the middle?","The girl owns this scarf. It is ___ scarf.","The girls put on their coats. ___ went outside.","The girls put on their coats. … went outside.","The given sequence begins with wetting the brush.","The glossary gives a young dog as the meaning of pup.","The gloves went into the pockets; the coat itself was then hung on the hook.","The glue had dried our model was ready","The glue had dried our. model was ready.","The glue had dried. Our model was ready.","The goalkeeper tipped the ball above the goal.","The goat used stepping stones; the sentence explicitly excludes the bridge and describes shallow water.","The green apple is ___ ripe yet.","The green apple is not ripe yet. Listen. Pick the missing word.","The green box was the only box with something inside.","The group leader is not the signed writer.","The guide is the person referred to by she called.","The guide offered two routes from the visitor centre beside the lake. One followed a straight path to a village across the fields. The other route was circular, forming a loop around the water. Walkers on that route passed the woods before reaching a small bridge. Beyond it, a different path followed the opposite shore home. They finished at the visitor centre without retracing their outward steps. Neither route climbed a hill or entered the nearby mountain area. The family chose the loop so their car would be waiting there.","The habit changes how taps are used during washing, not how often hands are washed.","The hall has a long corridor leading to the office.","The hall was built before the classroom next to it.","The hall was full of ___ who sang.","The handle ___ this old suitcase needs fixing.","The handle is round and made from shiny metal.","The handle material describes the brush but is not a step in rinsing.","The handle of this old suitcase needs fixing. Build the missing word.","The handle’s material says nothing about customer numbers.","The harbour was lively when the fishing boats returned each morning. Engines hummed while workers called across the dock and carried boxes. Buyers checked the catches, and drivers loaded vans for the market. By midnight, the same place was still and almost silent. The boxes remained stacked, but nobody moved among them then. Boats rested at their ropes with their engines switched off. Deep water lay beneath the dock at both times of day. At dawn, the returning crews brought movement and voices again.","The hat is lost, not offered for sale.","The head subject end is singular despite the nearby plural strings.","The heading names Lila’s diary, and I describes her actions.","The heavy books and thin paper explain why the bag broke.","The heavy books tore its thin paper sides.","The heavy load made her lose balance and take uneven steps. She ___ towards the bench.","The heavy load made her lose balance and take uneven steps. She … towards the bench.","The heavy rain stopped.","The heavy rain. does not satisfy the complete evidence. The rain stopped has both a subject and a complete action.","The helper held the bag open while the cleaner swept dust into it.","The helper needs a precise task and its targets.","The hens belong to us; we ___ ten hens.","The hiding place is stated only in Text B.","The hiker checked a ___ to find north.","The hiker checked a … to find north. Which word fits?","The hikers sleep in the tent, so it also provides rest.","The hill rose sharply, making the path ___.","The hill rose sharply, making the path …. Which word fits?","The hive ___ ten bees.","The hole gives a useful identifying feature.","The hole must exist before the seed can go into it.","The holes must exist before threading, and the string must be threaded before tying.","The horse jumped ___ the gate.","The horse lifted both front ___ to clear the fence.","The hotel ___ cleaned the room and changed the sheets.","The hotel has twelve ___, each with a bed.","The hotel … cleaned the room and changed the sheets. Which spelling fits?","The hungry gull followed the fishing boat until it sailed away from the harbour.","The hungry puppy gobbled all of his dinner. He took four huge mouthfuls without pausing to chew. In moments, the food bowl was completely empty. Then he searched its edges for one last crumb.","The ice became water in the warm room. It ___.","The ice became water in the warm room. It ….","The icy handle felt rough. Beyond the dark door, faint footsteps echoed down the hall.","The improved visibility directly addresses reading.","The ingredients are factual information about the bread.","The inside and outside voices are reversed.","The inside-rim detail distinguishes this cup from the other blue cups.","The instruction is attributed to Sal alone.","The instruction preserves both routes and who uses each.","The instructions concern hands, not hair.","The instructions say that water is needed only when the soil feels dry. Add water ___ the soil feels dry.","The invitation and benefit encourage attendance.","The invitation asks the friend to join the activity.","The invitation correctly explains that borrowing is optional.","The invitation includes supervision and the requested item.","The invitation includes the stated wet-weather plan.","The invitation keeps the meeting place, time and activity.","The invitation keeps the requested person, place, activity and time.","The invitation makes dressing up optional.","The invitation offers planting together, rather than assigning another job.","The invitation welcomes a beginner and offers instruction.","The inward hand signal and approaching friend reveal beckoned.","The island has three sandy ___ beside the sea.","The jacket kept me warm during the cold wind. Explain why.","The jam is sealed ___ the jar.","The jar is ___ tight for me to open.","The jar missed the shelf ___ an inch.","The jar of buttons ___ on the desk now.","The jar of buttons … on the desk now.","The jeweler measured the ring before repairing it.: A phone interrupting silence produces a sound; fingers and jewelry repair identify the object.","The keeper fed the hungry penguins, so they were full before the first visitors arrived.","The kettle boiled dad made tea","The kettle boiled dad. made tea.","The kettle boiled. Dad made tea.","The kettle bubbled gently. Ping! The toast sprang up. Ben scraped butter over it while a spoon clinked against his cup.","The kettle is full. ___ is heavy.","The kettle is full. … is heavy.","The kettle sends a cloud of steam,\nThe morning makes the windows gleam.\nA butter knife beside my plate\nReminds my hungry hands to wait.","The kettle was hot. It gave a thin whistle.","The key adds a main predicate to the longer subject.","The key describes visible parts, not a preference.","The key has a subject and a complete action.","The key includes a main clause after the although clause.","The key includes a subject and its action.","The key includes the outcome that completes the condition.","The key keeps the foreground and background colours distinct.","The key preserves both identifying features in their correct locations.","The key specifies both the handle and absence of wheels.","The key states a complete event.","The key states a main event and its reason.","The key states information that can be checked.","The key tells a complete action by the fish.","The key tells what happened to the bus.","The key tells what the bell did; the others are fragments.","The key tells who did what.","The keyboard broke, so I had to ___ the note by hand.","The keyboard broke, so I had to … the note by hand. Which spelling fits?","The keys beside the bowl ___ missing now.","The keys beside the bowl … missing now.","The keys lay ___ the door.","The keys lay by the door. Listen. Pick the missing word.","The kids lost ___ ball again.","The king looked out of the window.","The king sat on ___ throne.","The kitchen door stood open. Warm lamplight spilled onto the path, and friendly laughter floated out. Three chairs stood around the square table.","The kite flies ___ the pond.","The kite is red. The kite has a long tail.","The kite will rise ___ there is enough wind.","The kite will rise if there is enough wind. Build the missing word.","The kitten is ___ the tent.","The kitten is tame. The tiger is ___.","The kitten left the basket by the stairs and curled up behind the warm stove.","The kitten was found inside the tall cupboard.","The kittens followed their mother. ___ curled up together while their mother ate.","The kittens followed their mother. … curled up together while their mother ate.","The kittens licked ___ own paws.","The knot would not come loose despite many tries; I felt ___.","The knot would not come loose despite many tries; I felt …. Which word fits?","The label gives care and use directions.","The label says the cover can be washed without being damaged.","The label shows the ___ of seeds in each small packet.","The label shows the number of seeds in each small packet. Build the missing word.","The labels say these pets ___ cats, not dogs.","The laces were loose.","The ladder was ___ tall that it reached the upper branch.","The ladder was so tall that it reached the upper branch. Build the missing word.","The lake is still water rather than glass.","The lake was icy: its surface was ___.","The lake was made of glass.","The lamp ___ brightly every night.","The lamp had a black cord.","The lamp went ___, making the room dark.","The lamp … brightly every night.","The landmark identifies which path to follow.","The lane was chilly, meaning a little cold. Pip pulled his sleeves down over his fingers. The morning air felt cold against his face. He welcomed the warmth when they reached the shop.","The lantern hangs beside the blue door.","The lantern is blue. The lantern hangs beside the door.","The large clock face faced the waiting people.","The last bus\narrived after dark.","The last letter is h. The last sound uses both s and h.","The last sound is /sh/, so these sounds make fish.","The last trip of the year takes place in november.","The last whistle shrieked. “Hurry!” someone shouted as the doors began to close. A blue case stood beside a timetable on the platform.","The leaf drifted ___ to the ground.","The leaf drifted down to the ground. Listen. Pick the missing word.","The leaf matched fresh grass: it was ___.","The leaves practised steps for a show.","The left page is page 8. The right page comes next.","The lemonade was ___, making every mouth pucker.","The length can be measured.","The lesson begins at ten and lasts until eleven: one ___.","The lesson begins at ten and lasts until eleven: one …. Which spelling fits?","The letter came from japan last week.","The librarian is named directly after the quoted request.","The librarian speaks softly. We turn pages gently. Chairs have pads underneath their legs.","The librarian stamped the return date inside my borrowed book.","The library has several bookshelves near the back wall.","The library has three areas. Low shelves hold children’s books near the entrance. Study tables stand in a quiet room at the back. Newspapers and magazines are beside comfortable chairs in the middle.","The library is closed on ___ because the shelves are being repaired.","The library is making borrowing easier for people with different needs. A delivery van takes books to readers who cannot travel. The building has a red tiled roof. The library now opens late for people who work during the day. A painting of the town hangs beside the desk.","The library lends books but keeps all recorded stories for itself.","The library lends books. The library also lends recorded stories.","The library lends both books and recorded stories.","The library lends recorded stories instead of books.","The library opens late for daytime workers.","The library put simple books on low shelves. Picture labels helped children find subjects. A soft rug gave families a place to sit together, and a short story session welcomed new visitors. The changes made the space easier for young readers to use.","The lid does not fit this jar.","The lid named the owner.","The lid slipped. Clang! It struck the metal pan, then rolled quietly across the floor.","The lid was orange.","The lid was stuck. I pulled hard. At last it opened.","The lift was already full of passengers, so Mei waited downstairs for the next one.","The lift went from ten ___ to one.","The light turned green the cars moved forward","The light turned green the. cars moved forward.","The light turned green. The cars moved forward.","The lights came on ___ the room became too dark.","The lights came on when the room became too dark. Build the missing word.","The lights went out. We found a torch.","The line ends at apple. Reading continues at the left of the next line with rolled.","The line ends at beetle. Reading continues at the left of the next line with climbed.","The line ends at bridge. Reading continues at the left of the next line with the.","The line ends at bus. Reading continues at the left of the next line with arrived.","The line ends at cleared. Reading continues at the left of the next line with sunlight.","The line ends at coat. Reading continues at the left of the next line with when.","The line ends at coats. Reading continues at the left of the next line with Mum.","The line ends at flowerpot. Reading continues at the left of the next line with stood.","The line ends at giraffe. Reading continues at the left of the next line with bent.","The line ends at jar. Reading continues at the left of the next line with and.","The line ends at leaves. Reading continues at the left of the next line with landed.","The line ends at mitten. Reading continues at the left of the next line with lay.","The line ends at rang. Reading continues at the left of the next line with we.","The line ends at table. Reading continues at the left of the next line with a.","The line ends at thunder. Reading continues at the left of the next line with during.","The line ends at window. Reading continues at the left of the next line with before.","The lining’s protective action explains the purpose of padding.","The lion comparison describes courage while facing a difficult moment.","The list and instruction prepare the reader for a future activity.","The list names both things I packed: a spoon ___ a bowl.","The list names both things I packed: a spoon … a bowl.","The list’s location is neutral.","The little boat floated away ___ the changing tide.","The little boat floated away with the changing tide. Build the missing word.","The little boat stayed tied beside the dock after its passengers left. A passing ferry sent gentle waves towards its wooden hull. The boat bobbed as each wave reached the place it floated. Its nose rose above the dock''s edge, then dropped below it. The next wave brought the same change in height again. Its rope kept it near the post throughout this movement. It did not travel down the harbour or turn in circles. When the water settled, the boat''s nose stayed level beside the dock.","The load comparison gives direct evidence of greater strength.","The location is related to the shelter but does not explain waterproofing.","The log pile offers dark spaces for beetles.","The logs and nest boxes each provide shelter for living creatures.","The longest river in this book is the nile.","The loop concerns hanging the coat.","The loose ring slipped off into the sink.: A bell announcing a time produces a sound; the other rings are worn objects.","The lost mitten\nlay beside a puddle.","The low entrance helps people get on, and the clear information helps them get off at the right place.","The low entrance makes boarding easier.","The lower water proportion explains the thicker consistency.","The magical result makes this